{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check && git status --short && git check-ignore cores/bench/implementation_notes.md && cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/forward_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_alu.py\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\ncores/bench/implementation_notes.md\n# Load forwarding for RV32I consumers\n\nMEM forwards the aligned, sign- or zero-extended load result to both EX source operands. The same operands feed branch comparisons, store data and RVFI. A data-bus wait holds the consumer and preserves the older WB forwarding payload. A misaligned load loses its write qualification before forwarding priority is resolved.\n\nDependent RV32M instructions retain the one-cycle interlock. The multiplier and its ALTOPS expressions use dedicated operand muxes fed by the registered EX/MEM result, WB write data and ID/EX operands. EX shares the 32-bit base-plus-immediate sum with JALR and registers its full value alongside the memory controls. MEM uses that address for the bus, lane selection, alignment checks and RVFI.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed without warnings. All 53 fast formal checks passed on the first run.\n- All 90 cocotb cases passed, including consecutive LW-to-ADD retirement, subword loads under backpressure, dependent addresses and store data, a trapping load with an older writer, and the load-to-MULH interlock.\n- The selftest and three random programs matched the reference retirement traces with zero-wait and stalled buses. With both stalls enabled, ten CoreMark iterations took 3,990,037 bracketed cycles. All five canonical CRCs and the UART validation message matched.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 49.763, 50.844 and 50.844 MHz. The median gives 127.43 iterations/s, 7.47% above the 118.57 baseline. Option 0 used 2,817 logic cells and 632 registers, increases of 82 cells and 32 registers. The divider feedback path limits timing on all three placements.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
