{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat cores/bench/core.yaml cores/bench/LESSONS.md; rg -n 'implementation_notes|formal/work|build' .gitignore; rg --files cores/bench/test test/cosim formal\"", "aggregated_output": "name: bench\ndescription: Frozen LLM-benchmark fixture core. Identical RTL to baseline at the bench-fixture-v1\n  freeze point. The benchmark matrix clones from the bench-fixture-v1 orphan branch\n  and operates only on this directory.\nisa: rv32im\nnret: 1\ntarget_fpga: tang-nano-20k\ntargets:\n  fmax_mhz: 135\n  lut4: 10000\n  coremark_iter_s: 300\n  coremark_per_mhz: 2.2\ncurrent:\n  fmax_mhz: 48.072\n  lut4: 3101\n  ff: 623\n  coremark_iter_s: 3.0e-06\n  coremark_per_mhz: 0.0\n  source_id: hyp-20261120-003-r4s2\n  updated: '2026-11-20T21:10:13.598822Z'\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r1s0 (regression, +21.70%): Resolving two quotient bits per stage raised median Fmax from 5.442 to 6.627 MHz, but 16 serial remainder selections still limited the EX clock even though the timed CoreMark loop executes no divides.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r1s1 (improvement, +483.66%): Splitting DIV/REM across four registered passes shortened the EX timing path to eight quotient decisions per pass, and the timed CoreMark loop incurred no divider stalls because it executes neither operation.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r1s2 (regression, +11.14%): The 64-entry agree predictor and direct JAL prediction in IF saved 297,001 CoreMark cycles by avoiding one-cycle EX redirect bubbles, while the critical path through forwarding and the EX ALU still limited median Fmax to 5.648 MHz.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r2s0 (improvement, +47.44%): Padding the 32-bit dividend with three leading zeros let seven uniform passes cut the divider feedback path from eight serial subtract-and-select steps to five, raising Fmax without adding divider stalls to the timed CoreMark loop.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r2s2 (regression, +6.25%): Caching targets by fetch PC cut CoreMark JALR redirects from 13,735 to 1,647 in the 128-entry BTB prototype, but shortening the four-pass divider's critical path delivered a larger throughput gain (+47.44%).\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r3s0 (regression, +5.87%): Squashing via ID/EX.valid let register reads and load-use detection use t\n…[1328 of 4400 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:7f681e9506765d51]…\ne 4.49% CoreMark cycle cost of blocking EX on multiplies.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r4s2 (improvement, +18.94%): Caching the successor PC and instruction together under the predecessor PC let IF supply 604,950 instructions on full address matches during CoreMark imem waits and correctly predict 12,072 of 13,735 JALR targets with the same lookup.\n52:sim_build/\n54:test/cosim/sim_build/\n55:test/**/sim_build/\n63:# Bench ELFs (rebuild from sources)\n69:# Held-out benchmark ELFs (rebuild via `make -f bench/holdout/Makefile all`)\n70:bench/holdout/build/*.elf\n87:cores/*/implementation_notes.md\nformal/checks_si.cfg\nformal/wrapper.sv\nformal/run_all.sh\nformal/checks-deep.cfg\nformal/checks.cfg\nformal/wrapper_si.sv\ntest/cosim/reference.py\ntest/cosim/main.cpp\ntest/cosim/build.sh\ntest/cosim/run_cosim.py\ncores/bench/test/test_imm_gen.py\ncores/bench/test/conftest.py\ncores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\ncores/bench/test/_helpers.py\ncores/bench/test/test_decoder.py\ncores/bench/test/test_reg_file.py\ncores/bench/test/test_alu.py\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
