{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_74","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"# Early load forwarding\n\nID compares both source fields of the selected fetch word against the valid EX load's nonzero destination. It latches the two matches with an admitted instruction, clears them on a bubble or reset, and holds them during a pipeline stall.\n\nEX selects the formatted MEM load for each matched operand. The aligned read enable qualifies the bypass, and a data-bus wait holds both the load and its consumer until the transfer completes. The ordinary forwarding network uses MEM's post-trap register-write enable, so a misaligned load lets an older WB writer supply the operand. MEM/WB retains its forwarding payload throughout a wait.\n\nDependent RV32M instructions take one interlock bubble. Both hardware and ALTOPS multiply use the ordinary forwarded operands. EX shares the base-plus-immediate sum with JALR and registers the full sum for memory addressing, lane selection, alignment checks, and RVFI.\n\nOne implementation detail differs from the original load formatter. MEM selects the halfword with address bit 1, then the byte with bit 0. This lets aligned halfword loads bypass the byte-selection mux. WB and EX use the same formatted result.\n\n## Verification\n\nFast formal passed all 53 checks with the single-retirement wrapper and ALTOPS enabled. The final RTL passed on its first run.\n\nVerilator lint passed without warnings. All 99 cocotb cases passed. Directed cases check consecutive retirement, both source operands, subword extension, dependent addresses and stores, branch and JALR targets, x0, fallback past a trapping load, and the MULH interlock. The saved-successor test holds both buses and changes the unaccepted read data while the consumer waits.\n\nCosimulation matched the reference ISS on the selftest and all three random programs with ready and randomly stalled buses. The stalled CoreMark run passed all five CRC checks and printed `Correct operation validated.`.\n\n## FPGA fitness\n\nTen iterations took 3,204,188 bracketed cycles with both stall generators enabled, saving 204,374 cycles. Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 49.888, 51.934, and 51.934 MHz. The median gives **162.08 iterations/s**, a 14.93% gain over 141.03 iterations/s.\n\nDivider feedback sets the longest path on all three placements. The live load path also has little timing margin. On placement 0 the path from data RAM through EX to fetch has a 25.270 ns arrival time, within 0.069 ns of the divider path.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
