{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_68","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check; git status --short; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/forward_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/reg_file.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n M cores/bench/test/test_reg_file.py\n# Decode-stage register reads and forwarding\n\nBoth operands come from asynchronous distributed RAM. Decode applies the WB write-first bypass and captures each value in its existing ID/EX register. A separate written mask clears on synchronous reset. Its x0 bit is tied low, and each other bit is set by a write to that row.\n\nDecode compares the fetched sources against the EX and MEM destinations using each stage's write control after trap resolution. The resulting one-hot selects advance with the operands and hold during stalls. EX masks and ORs the captured value, EX/MEM result, and MEM/WB write data. Stores, branch comparisons, and divide issue all use those operands.\n\nThe existing load and multiply interlocks, divide operand capture, and MEM/WB data hold already supplied the required timing. The new logic reuses them. The forwarding unit's input names now identify the stages that supply the decode-time comparisons.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with the requested warning flags.\n- Local formal passed all 53 single-retirement ALTOPS checks, including the register, order, and liveness checks.\n- All 102 cocotb cases passed, including competing writers on either source, trapping jumps and loads, a held operand after WB retirement, and both read ports after a warm reset.\n- RVFI co-simulation matched the reference ISS for selftest and all three random programs, with zero-wait buses and with both buses stalling.\n- The stalled ten-iteration CoreMark run passed the five expected CRCs and printed `Correct operation validated.`. Its timing markers were at cycles 12,247 and 3,456,466, giving 3,444,219 bracketed cycles.\n\n## Timing and area\n\nGowin measured 68.678, 71.092, and 71.092 MHz for placement options 0, 1, and 2. The 71.092 MHz median gives **206.41 CoreMark iterations/s**, an 18.37% gain over the 174.38 iterations/s baseline.\n\nPlacement 0 used 3,306 logic cells, 752 registers, 36 LUT-RAMs, seven BSRAMs, and two DSPs. The register file accounts for 32 of the LUT-RAMs. The longest reported path in every placement runs from the divider seed ROM to the quotient register. A path from the WB data selector through EX to data RAM ranks third in placement 0.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
