{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_42","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check && git status --short && cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_alu.py\n# Dedicated products for Newton division\n\nThe ALU now captures MUL operands in two signed 36-bit registers and selects the low or high product word in MEM. The divider has its own magnitude registers and four unsigned products with fixed register inputs. It computes `A - B` on pass 1 and `A >> k` on pass 2, alongside reciprocal refinement. Pass 5 takes the trial quotient from bits 65 through 34. Pass 6 registers both `A - Q*B` and `(A - B) - Q*B`. The latter's sign selects the remainder and controls the quotient increment.\n\nThe implementation follows the proposed schedule of seven clocks from divide issue to result-valid and one MUL issue per clock. The added tests exercise all 32 shift counts, exact multiples and their neighbors, dividends with every discarded bit set, and held DIVU and REMU responses. A mixed MULHSU, DIVU, MULHU sequence checks the older MEM product at divide issue and the next multiply at its first legal issue edge.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with `-Wall`.\n- Selftest and all three random programs matched the reference retirement traces with ready buses and with both buses stalling.\n- CoreMark passed all five CRC checks. Ten iterations took 3,444,219 cycles between the MMIO markers with both buses stalling.\n- Gowin placements 0, 1 and 2 reached 72.705, 76.332 and 76.332 MHz. The median yields 221.62 iterations/s, a 7.37% gain over 206.41. All three worst setup paths end in instruction fetch.\n- The resource cost is eight additional DSPs over the baseline. Placement 0 used 10 of 24 DSPs, 3,214 logic cells, 781 registers and seven BSRAMs.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
