{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_99","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"# Pending-pointer load prediction\n\nTen CoreMark iterations took **3,097,817 cycles** with both stall generators enabled. The start and stop markers occurred at cycles 11,770 and 3,109,587. This saves 346,402 cycles (10.06%) against the champion.\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 69.544, 71.595 and 71.595 MHz. The median gives **231.11 iterations/s**, 11.97% above the champion's 206.41. Placement 0 used 3,691 logic cells and 13 BSRAMs, an increase of 385 cells and six BSRAMs.\n\n## Implementation\n\nThe 1,024-row direct-mapped buffer stores a valid bit, an eight-bit tag and the raw memory word. Decode predicts the row by XORing the I-immediate index bits with a base estimate. A matching EX load supplies its raw candidate pointer bits. Otherwise decode uses a matching MEM writer's registered result or the register file's WB-bypassed value.\n\nEX verifies the predicted row against the fully forwarded effective address before releasing a dependent instruction. The EX/MEM bypass register carries the formatted candidate alongside a separate byte address. The resolved probe reads the actual row on the same edge. I registered the load, RAM-range and alignment qualifications with that probe to shorten the MEM hit path. MEM selects its raw response or the demand bus word for both writeback and RVFI.\n\nAccepted full-word RAM stores and demand reads fill the buffer. Partial stores invalidate their row. Writes take priority over decode reads, and a same-row write vetoes both resolved reuse and early forwarding. The reset sweep uses the write port for 1,024 clocks while demand traffic proceeds.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with `-Wall`.\n- Local fast formal passed all 53 checks with the configured single-channel RVFI wrapper. No checks failed and no formal fixes were needed.\n- All five cocotb runners passed, including 36 pipeline tests. Six directed tests cover consecutive pointer-dependent retirements with the bus unavailable, cold and resident XOR misses, partial stores, both read-port collisions, reset, subword formatting, misalignment, 4 KiB aliases and MMIO. The harness supplies a poison word whenever the bus does not accept a read.\n- Selftest and three random programs matched the reference retirement trace with zero-wait and stalled buses. Stalled CoreMark produced all five canonical CRCs, printed `Correct operation validated.` and reached EBREAK without an out-of-bounds access.\n\nThe fast formal suite has a 20-cycle horizon. The post-sweep tests exercise resident-row forwarding and collision behavior beyond that horizon.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
