{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_268","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check\ngit status --short\ncat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n# Branch comparison in parallel with forwarding\n\nDecode registers a one-hot physical source pair alongside the existing forwarding selects. Both hold on `stall_id`. The two cross-bypass comparisons put the ordinary result or product first. When WB supplies rs1, decode includes equality and inverts strict order so equal operands still fail BLT and BLTU. A shared bypass word produces equality directly.\n\nEX compares all nine distinct pairs in four byte lanes. Each lane selects its equality and unsigned less-than bits before a shared high-byte-first priority network resolves the condition. Signed branches use only the two forwarded sign bits to reverse unsigned order when the signs differ. Decode also folds the predicted direction into the expected comparator result for redirect. The selected condition drives predictor training and architectural next PC, including the taken-branch alignment trap. RVFI operands still come from the full forwarding network.\n\nIn fetch, opcode bit 3 selects the B or J displacement wiring. The existing full opcode, alignment and RAM-range checks qualify predictions. No pipeline stage was added.\n\nThe pipeline tests exercise all six branch operations, the nine pairs and both cross-bypass orientations, shared writers, x0, byte priority, signed boundaries, load and divide interlocks, and misaligned targets. They check RVFI operands, retirement order, next PCs and wrong-path bus writes. The existing held-branch test also checks the fetch sequence while data memory is stalled.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with `-Wall`.\n- Local single-retirement ALTOPS formal passed all 53 checks on the first run of the final RTL.\n- All 114 cocotb cases passed, including 41 pipeline cases.\n- Cosimulation passed for all five ELFs. Ten CoreMark iterations took 3,062,533 bracketed cycles under instruction and data stalls. All five CRCs matched and the UART reported “Correct operation validated.”\n\n## Timing\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 measured 74.219, 77.300 and 77.300 MHz. The 77.300 MHz median is 2.52% below the 79.301 MHz champion and misses the 82.95 MHz acceptance threshold. Option 1 reports an 18.231 ns arrival from the divider seed RAM to the residual register and an 18.177 ns arrival from WB write data to the successor RAM. Gowin reports 4,292 logic elements and 849 registers.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
