{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_375","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"# Successor RAM lookahead\n\nTen iterations completed in 3,078,099 bracketed cycles. The 81.435 MHz median gives 264.56 iterations/s, a 2.17% improvement over the stated champion and below the hypothesis's promotion threshold.\n\nThe 512-row table now reads the predicted next fetch index each clock. A matching probe supplies a registered successor word, its RAM word address and its aligned B/J target metadata. IF checks the full address before reuse. A ready bus word supplies both the instruction and its direct prediction. The 64 agree counters retain their previous training policy.\n\nThe read carries its sampled index and same-edge write information. Promotion checks intervening writes, including while the probe is parked, and retains the original logical same-row veto. A blocked fetch can reread the last admitted predecessor and install a full-address hit during an interlock. Redirects preserve the predecessor for training. Reset and admitted FENCE.I discard speculative state and start a table sweep. A fill already clocked into the old table on the fence edge is unreachable and the sweep clears it.\n\nThe write port packs the direct target from a registered training pair. EX forms direct recovery from the issuing PC and immediate. It verifies a legal JALR forecast against the aligned address held in IF. To shorten the admission path, the design separately registers predicted, recovery and held PCs. Separate fabric registers carry the incoming row, the parked probe and the previously selected response. This keeps the late admission decision off their wide clock enables. EX uses the same parallel address comparison for JALR verification and the early-load interlock. A local SAT check proved the comparison equivalent to a full 32-bit addition followed by word-address equality for every base, offset and expected address.\n\nThe directed tests exercise a bus-low warm direct loop, a changed direct target on a ready bus, demand recovery after a different-index redirect, and a queued write to a 512-row alias during a divide hold. The FENCE.I test admits the fence immediately after queuing a fill. The tests check channel-0 retirement order, PCs, instructions, memory effects and one retirement per cycle on the warm loop.\n\n## Local verification and fitness\n\n- Verilator strict lint passed. The single-retirement riscv-formal ALTOPS suite passed all 53 checks on its first run for this RTL.\n- All 115 cocotb cases passed, including 42 pipeline cases. Co-simulation passed the selftest and three random programs against the ISS both with and without stalls.\n- The ten-iteration stalled CoreMark run crossed the start marker at cycle 11,866 and the stop marker at 3,089,965, giving **3,078,099 bracketed cycles**. UART reported all five expected CRCs and “Correct operation validated.” The simulator reported no out-of-bounds access.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached **80.589, 81.435 and 81.435 MHz**. The two median-frequency placements had their longest path from the divider seed ROM to the residual register. At this cycle count, the promotion threshold scales to approximately 83.37 MHz. The lookahead adds 15,566 bracketed cycles relative to the champion. A missed probe can still cost a bus-low cycle after a redirect or an interlock.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
