{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git status --short -uall; git check-ignore cores/bench/implementation_notes.md; git diff --check; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_alu.py\ncores/bench/implementation_notes.md\n# Divider correction retiming\n\nThe implementation follows the proposed seven-pass schedule. Passes 1 and 3 capture the 33-bit magnitude difference A−B alongside the refinement factor. Pass 5 saves the quotient estimate Q. Pass 6 registers the low word of Q×B directly from the DSP and computes Q+1 or Q−1 according to the result sign.\n\nThe output cycle checks the saved borrow and compares Q×B with A−B while both remainder candidates are added in parallel. A sign-dependent carry-in and a shared final XOR restore the sign of the selected quotient or remainder. A zero divisor forces the quotient to all ones and yields the original dividend for REM.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with the requested warning flags.\n- All 113 cocotb cases passed, including 30 ALU and 39 pipeline cases. The new test covers 6/3, 5/3 and 2/3 for quotient and remainder with all four operand sign combinations. It also holds a negative remainder by zero under backpressure for three extra clocks.\n- A 12-step SAT comparison against the previous RTL proved matching ready, valid and result words across issue, seven passes, backpressure and transfer. Both designs received the same symbolic DSP outputs, with P ≤ A on pass 6 and P = 0 for a zero divisor.\n- Selftest and all three random programs matched reference retirement with and without bus stalls. The stalled 10-iteration CoreMark run took 3,081,605 bracketed cycles, matched all five CRCs and printed “Correct operation validated.”\n\n## Local FPGA result\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 83.100, 87.580 and 87.580 MHz. The median gives **284.20 iterations/s**, 0.28 iterations/s above the 283.92 promotion threshold. The longest path on both median-frequency placements runs from the resolved load-buffer tag to MEM/WB write data. Placement 0 used 3,601 logic cells, 1,078 registers, four DSPs and 13 block RAMs.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
