{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_128","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"# Divide and writeback retiming\n\nThe divider reuses `normalized_q` for `dividend >> k_q` on pass 4. Pass 5 takes the fixed product slice `[65:34]`. Pass 6 computes the residual from the original dividend and divisor. The corrected quotient or remainder is captured when EX transfers the instruction to MEM.\n\nMEM selects the multiply, divide, formatted load, or ordinary EX word before the MEM/WB data register. That register drives the register file and both WB forwarding inputs. A data-bus wait clears retirement validity while holding the forwarding word. An immediate DIV/REM consumer takes one dependency bubble. Integer results and verified load hits still bypass from EX/MEM.\n\nThe additional timing change places WB data on the final EX operand mux. EX uses the two-bit select already produced in ID, where forwarding priority is resolved. The top-level ports follow the target's declared `nret: 1`.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator passed the requested `-Wall` lint check. All 110 cocotb cases passed across five test modules.\n- Local formal passed all 53 checks on the final RTL with zero failures or retries. The single-channel configuration used ALTOPS for M-extension routing and retirement. Cocotb and ISS co-simulation exercised the hardware arithmetic.\n- Directed tests cover maximal discarded dividend bits at every divisor exponent, exact multiples and their neighbors, and held quotient and remainder responses.\n- The MUL–DIV–MUL–DIV sequence retired with gaps of 9, 2, and 10 clocks. The dependent store retired four clocks after DIV with two bus waits and one accepted write. A separate test checked a held consumer after the divide had retired.\n- Selftest and all three random programs matched the ISS with ready buses and with both stall generators. Ten CoreMark iterations passed the canonical CRC checks in 3,097,817 bracketed cycles.\n\n## Timing\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 74.570, 76.071, and 76.071 MHz. The 76.071 MHz median exceeds the 74.887 MHz threshold by 1.184 MHz and yields **245.56 CoreMark iterations/s**. The median placements' worst setup path now runs from the reciprocal ROM through the shared DSP to MEM/WB write data. Placement 0 is limited by load-buffer hit logic feeding fetch control.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
