{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_187", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'date -u; tail -5 \"$TMPDIR/retainedsim.log\"; tail -4 \"$TMPDIR/retainedtests.log\"; tail -6 \"$TMPDIR/retainedtiming.log\"; rg -c '\"'\"'^@cocotb.test'\"\\\\(' cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py; git status --short; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md; tail -3 \\\"\"'$TMPDIR/bestformal.log\"; tail -3 \"$TMPDIR/tagformal.log\"'", "aggregated_output": "Sat Nov 21 01:48:41 UTC 2026\npython3 -m tools.eval.cosim . bench\n{\n  \"passed\": true,\n  \"elfs_tested\": 2\n}\n......                                                                   [100%]\n6 passed in 8.94s\n40\n M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n?? cores/bench/rtl/fetch_blocks.sv\n?? cores/bench/test/test_fetch_blocks.py\n# Registered four-word fetch blocks\n\nThe fetch store has 128 direct-mapped rows. Four word banks share a block tag and a four-bit presence mask. Both resident buffers are clocked read destinations of those banks. A fill can also update one resident slot. On replacement, only words belonging to the new tag become valid. A same-bank read/write collision forwards the accepted fill.\n\nThe issue cursor contains a full block address and a two-bit slot. Two registered match bits bind the cursor to the resident buffers, accounting for a simultaneous replacement. Decode and load-use checking consume the selected instruction. A registered block request can run during an interlock. The request follows the first predicted transfer at or beyond the cursor, or the sequential block. A missing demand word has priority on the external bus. A resident hit frees the bus for a registered refill address.\n\nThe ready-edge demand bypass predicts fall-through. The following cycle computes direct-control metadata from the captured bus word and its own PC. The agree counters and learned JALR targets are banked by slot. ID/EX holds each word's predicted successor through stalls. Advancing EX instructions verify the full successor and train the predictor. On repair, the first corrected fetch uses the demand path while the cursor reestablishes its resident match. FENCE.I squashes younger deliveries, clears the resident buffers and starts a 128-cycle directory invalidation. Demand delivery continues during the sweep.\n\nThe existing single-channel RVFI interface follows the target's `nret: 1` configuration. Directed tests cover entering a partial block at its last slot, concurrent lookup and refill collisions, held consumers, changing JALR targets, wrong-path stores, self-modifying code synchronized with FENCE.I, and consecutive retirement across two blocks with the instruction bus stalled.\n\nThe local formal run for the registered cursor binding passed all 53 checks. Two earlier attempts exposed Yosys elaboration limitations with a type argument to `$bits` and a two-dimensional unpacked struct array. Fixed-width packed bank ports resolved both. An experimental additional delivery stage failed the bounded liveness check and was removed.\n\nVerilator lint, all six cocotb runners, and co-simulation passed. Selftest matched the ISS with and without random stalls. The ten-iteration CoreMark run produced the five expected CRCs, the validation message and no out-of-bounds access. The timing markers were at cycles 11,943 and 3,119,035, giving 3,107,092 measured cycles.\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 71.198, 77.164 and 77.164 MHz. Median throughput was 248.35 iterations/second. The implementation missed the promotion threshold. The worst path on placement 1 runs from a forwarded EX result through address generation to the resident-block match register. Cross-block demand bypass and the initial demand fetch after a repair also contribute to the cycle count.\nSBY  2:47:43 [insn_xor_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\nS