aving it for retry on stalls eliminated the taken-redirect bubble, cutting CoreMark cycles by 7.91%.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r3s2 (improvement, +13.68%): Latching the direction IF used and the alternate PC f\n…[6122 of 9194 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:7228b09c00cdbe1f]…\nulation, limiting median Fmax to 80.074 MHz.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-001-r11s0 (regression, +2.17%): Probing the successor RAM one fetch early and registering its response moved the median critical path to the divider's seed-to-residual logic at 81.435 MHz, while missed probes after redirects or interlocks could expose instruction-bus waits (15,566 extra cycles over ten CoreMark iterations).\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r11s1 (improvement, +4.82%): Steering JAL and predicted-taken branches from the registered successor PC removed the target adder from the fetch path, while training the RAM on consecutive MEM transfers kept EX redirect and load-use logic off its write enable.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r11s2 (regression, -4.72%): Probing both outcomes of the carry into address bit 6 saved 4,954 cycles (0.16%) over ten CoreMark iterations, but the path from the saved instruction to the fetch PC limited median Fmax to 75.438 MHz and the path from that instruction to the early bank read was second longest.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
