{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_16", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md | tail -12; cat cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | tail -c 1600; cat bench/programs/Makefile; sed -n '1,220p' test/cosim/run_cosim.py\"", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-001-r9s0 (improvement, +5.45%): A dedicated EX multiplier removed the divider seed ROM from the multiply writeback path, and a one-hot bypass selected in decode let the next instruction use the registered product without a stall.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r9s1 (regression, -1.69%): Registering the low and high DSP product words separately at MEM/WB widened each EX bypass mux to five inputs, putting the rs2 high-product select on the critical path to fetch and lowering median Fmax from 76.071 to 74.789 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r9s2 (regression, +4.17%): Only 11,921 of 65,940 correct MUL operand forecasts in the baseline trace had an immediate consumer, so replaying misses in EX and computing wide products in 16×16 passes cost more than the saved stalls, adding 7,541 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-001-r10s0 (regression, -2.52%): Comparing nine branch operand pairs in parallel added 677 LUT4s without saving a CoreMark cycle, and the divider's seed-to-residual path limited two of three placements to 77.300 MHz.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r10s1 (regression, -4.09%): With registered four-word fetch blocks, the path from EX forwarding through address generation to the resident-block match register limited median Fmax to 77.164 MHz, and demand fetches at block crossings and after redirects contributed to 44,559 extra cycles over ten CoreMark iterations.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r10s2 (regression, +0.97%): Registering both upper sums for direct branch and JAL targets preserved CoreMark's 3,062,533 cycles, but the successor RAM write enable still depended on EX operand forwarding and address calculation, limiting median Fmax to 80.074 MHz.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-001-r11s0 (regression, +2.17%): Probing the successor RAM one fetch early and registering its response moved the median critical path to the divider's seed-to-residual logic at 81.435 MHz, while missed probes after redirects or interlocks could expose\n…[11141 of 14213 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d28cbd2d4fbf0816]…\n       return {'passed': False, 'divergence_at': min(len(ref_trace),len(sim_trace)),\n                'field': 'retirement_count',\n                'expected': len(ref_trace), 'got': len(sim_trace)}\n    return {'passed': True, 'retired': len(ref_trace)}\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    # Extra args after the ELF are passed to the simulator (e.g. --istall\n    # --dstall to run the trace comparison under bus backpressure).\n    sim_bin, elf, sim_flags = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2], sys.argv[3:]\n    try:\n        ref, ref_completed, ref_reason = run_reference(elf)\n        sim, sim_completed, sim_returncode, sim_stderr = run_verilator(sim_bin, elf, sim_flags=sim_flags)\n        result = compare(ref, sim, ref_completed, sim_completed,\n                         ref_reason, sim_returncode, sim_stderr)\n    except (json.JSONDecodeError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired, OSError) as e:\n        result = {'passed': False, 'field': 'harness_error', 'detail': str(e)}\n    print(json.dumps(result))\n    sys.exit(0 if result['passed'] else 1)\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
