{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_204","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git status --short; git diff --check; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n# Posted RAM stores\n\nMEM can transfer one aligned ordinary-RAM store per cycle when the bus accepts writes. When the bus stalls, the registered bus address and a one-entry buffer retain the write mask and replicated payload. An acknowledgement can admit the next RAM store on the same edge. A waiting demand read or MMIO store receives its own bus cycle after the older write drains. RVFI reports the transferring MEM instruction.\n\nStore acceptance also updates the load buffer. Full stores allocate a word. SB and SH write their selected lanes and preserve validity only on a usable, exact resolved hit. The reset sweep clears metadata, and both lookup paths reject collisions. FENCE, FENCE.I and EBREAK wait until the entry is empty. FENCE.I invalidates the successor buffer and refetches PC+4, including when the instruction bus stalls.\n\nThe early probe uses a 32-entry table holding the ten index bits from each register's last writeback. This implementation choice shortens the path from register selection to the load-buffer read address. A simultaneous writeback can leave the hint one update behind. EX verifies the full address before using the candidate.\n\n## Local measurements\n\n- Verilator lint passed. Co-simulation matched the ISS on selftest and all three random programs in zero-wait and combined-stall modes.\n- All five cocotb runners passed, including 45 pipeline cases. Directed checks compare every bus presentation through acknowledgement, verify queue replacement and partial-store collisions, and exercise a stalled refetch of a rewritten instruction.\n- Ten CoreMark iterations took 3,039,851 bracketed cycles with both stall generators, saving 41,754 cycles against the starting core. All five canonical CRCs and the success message matched.\n- Gowin placements reached 81.065, 84.511 and 84.511 MHz. The median gives 278.01 iterations/s, below promotion.\n- The Gowin hierarchy report attributes ten block RAMs to the load buffer. The timed design uses 17 block RAMs in total. A trial sharing one physical read/write port per bank inferred a dual-port write mode rejected by place-and-route.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
