{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_170","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md; git diff --check; date -u'","aggregated_output":"# Prepared ALU operand and producer claims\n\nID/EX carries a prepared ALU operand, one-hot integer result enables, and separate forwarding claims for raw rs2 and the ALU operand. Only register-register OP can claim a late ALU operand. A shared sum supplies ADD, ADDI, effective addresses, and JALR. AUIPC uses the direct target sum. Stores, branches, multiplication, and RVFI retain the forwarded register values.\n\nID matches the registered EX writer before squash and verifies the selected claim against the advancing writer. Cancellation and unavailable load or divide data trigger the existing one-slot replay. A retry ignores its own EX tag. Claims, operands, and the replay decision advance together across data waits. MEM exposes a registered write tag with load alignment already checked. The register file retains its write-first behavior.\n\n## Timing and interlock changes\n\nThe first formal run passed 52 checks and failed bounded liveness. A branch correction followed by a canceled claim and a divide produced an 11-clock retirement gap. Moving the divide into MEM on its seventh and final arithmetic pass recovered one clock. The interlock still holds issue through the computation. A dedicated narrow multiply handles the first two reciprocal products with the 12-bit seed, adding one DSP.\n\nThe timing runs also motivated parallel byte comparisons for branches, parallel formatting of candidate load lanes and memory-return words, and separate registers for the branch decision and candidate PCs. Fetch computes candidate link addresses before selecting the next PC. Predictor count forwarding operates in parallel with the data wait, and the counter write still requires an advancing edge.\n\n## Verification and fitness\n\nVerilator lint passed. The single-channel ALTOPS formal suite passed all 53 checks after the interlock fix. All 117 cocotb cases passed, including directed tests for a wrong-path load with an early hit, self-dependent retry, a canceled link, redirect priority, and immediate fields that alias register destinations.\n\nCo-simulation passed selftest and three random programs against the ISS with and without stalls. Ten stalled CoreMark iterations took 3,084,030 bracketed clocks, 2,425 more than the champion. All five CRCs matched, the validation message appeared, and the simulator reported no out-of-bounds access.\n\nGowin placements 0, 1, and 2 reached 77.194, 88.100, and 88.100 MHz. The median gives 285.67 iterations/s, a 0.51% throughput improvement over the supplied champion. At the measured cycle count, promotion requires approximately 91.68 MHz. The median placement's longest path runs from the registered direction correction through instruction selection and the register-file read to the early load-buffer index.\nSat Nov 21 08:36:37 UTC 2026\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
