{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_160","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'date -u; for f in \"$TMPDIR\"/banks-proof.{dF5gs6,v0JOd9}/formal/riscv-formal/cores/*/checks/reg_ch0/logfile.txt; do tail -1 \"$f\"; done; for f in \"$TMPDIR\"/banks-five-rest/*/impl/pnr/core_bench.log; do tail -1 \"$f\"; done; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"Sat Nov 21 00:45:49 UTC 2026\nSBY  1:45:48 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:05:04  waiting for solver (5 minutes)\nSBY  1:43:11 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:01:08  waiting for solver (1 minute)\n\nGenerate file \"/srv/hwebench/tmp/ec844b20/2fb2be2e/banks-five-rest/gowin_p2/impl/pnr/core_bench.rpt.txt\" completed\n# Decode-time writeback bank selection\n\nMEM/WB captures both raw halves of the shared DSP product and a separate word for integer, load and divide results. Decode resolves each source against the youngest eligible writer and registers its bank enables with the operand. EX masks and combines the banks independently for each raw and arithmetic input. The register file, its write-first bypass and RVFI share the architectural writeback mux. The ALU test port still exposes the selected product, and both banks carry the operation-specific surrogate in ALTOPS mode.\n\nThe directed regression alternates writers to x5, checks simultaneous EX/MEM and low-product forwarding, and holds a high-product consumer behind a store for three wait cycles. It checks both source values, the result, the single store handshake and every channel-0 retirement order.\n\nDecode also classifies the producer itself when it issues. That one-hot class travels through EX/MEM into WB. ID captures a local arithmetic word for each port, substituting PC or immediate into the RF slot when appropriate. This keeps each arithmetic port at five inputs and costs two extra word registers. Raw operands have independent selectors for address generation, comparisons, stores and RVFI. A squash clears ID/EX validity, and MEM/WB resets the validity and control fields independently of the bank payload.\n\nThe fetch path appeared among the slowest paths in placement. The direct-target bounds check now evaluates the two possible upper address regions in parallel with the low 20-bit addition. The full opcode check, alignment rule and allowed target region still qualify each prediction.\n\nVerilator lint and all 111 cocotb cases passed. Gowin placement 1 reached 76.895 MHz, compared with the 79.57 MHz acceptance threshold. Its first setup path starts at the registered rs1 low-product enable and reaches the fetch response RAM enable in 18.247 ns.\n\nCosimulation passed for five ELFs, including the selftest and three random traces with and without stalls. CoreMark ran ten iterations in 3,097,817 cycles between the MMIO markers. All five canonical CRCs matched, UART reported “Correct operation validated.” and the harness reported zero out-of-bounds accesses.\n\nThe initial implementation passed all 53 ALTOPS formal checks. The final RTL is being checked with the same single-channel configuration.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
