{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_158","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 -c 'from pathlib import Path; from xml.etree import ElementTree as ET; reports=[ET.parse(f) for f in Path(\\\"cores/bench/test\\\").glob(\\\"*.result.xml\\\")]; print({tag:sum(len(t.findall(\\\".//\\\"+tag)) for t in reports) for tag in (\\\"testcase\\\",\\\"skipped\\\",\\\"failure\\\",\\\"error\\\")})' && git status --short && git diff --check && cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"{'testcase': 114, 'skipped': 0, 'failure': 0, 'error': 0}\n M cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_alu.py\n# Seven-clock RV32M divider\n\nThe divider reads a 1,024-entry, 18-bit left-endpoint reciprocal table and takes one Newton step. The unsigned 18×35 multiplier produces the 35-bit factor in pass 1 and the truncated reciprocal from product bits 51 through 20 in pass 2. A separate unsigned 32×32 multiplier estimates the quotient in pass 3 and registers the low word of the quotient times the divisor in pass 4. Pass 5 registers the residual. Pass 6 uses the borrow from subtracting the divisor to select the corrected magnitude before sign restoration.\n\nThe divider computes three byte-range flags alongside the absolute divisor at issue to shorten the path to the seed ROM. Intermediate registers sample every clock. The quotient and corrected magnitude have pass enables, and the request signs and result hold through backpressure. The arithmetic, seven busy clocks, ALTOPS handshake and one-cycle EX multiply follow the hypothesis.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with `-Wall`.\n- All 114 cocotb tests passed. The divider tests compare quotient and remainder at both representable ends of every normalized bin for exponents 10, 11, 12, 13 and 17. They exercise exact multiples and their neighbors, divisors 1–3 near the maximum unsigned dividend, both correction outcomes, signed byte boundaries and held results.\n- Local fast formal passed all 53 ALTOPS checks with the single-retirement wrapper. Real divider arithmetic was checked in simulation.\n- Selftest and all three random instruction traces matched the reference ISS with and without memory stalls. Stalled CoreMark passed all five CRCs and completed ten iterations in 3,081,605 bracketed cycles.\n\n## Timing\n\nTwo Gowin runs produced the same placement results of 81.837, 87.484 and 87.484 MHz for options 0, 1 and 2. The median gives 283.89 iterations/s and falls 0.008 MHz below the stated promotion threshold. The longest path in both median-frequency placements ran from the registered fetch reset through the register file to the early load-buffer row. Placement 0 was limited by the successor RAM to fall-through PC path.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
