{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_59", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'hyp-20261121-001-r12s0' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | tail -n 1 | cut -c 1-3800\"", "aggregated_output": "35:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261121-001-r12s0\", \"title\": \"Register the divider product before residual correction\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"On the champion's 83.645 MHz median placement, Gowin reports 17.250 ns\\nfrom the reciprocal ROM through the shared DSP to residual_q and 17.244 ns\\nfrom residual_q into the signed divide result register. Pass 6 puts a\\n32-bit subtractor after the DSP. The following cycle can compare, subtract\\nand negate in series. CoreMark executes no divides in its timed loop,\\nbut both paths constrain the clock on every instruction.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"On the champion's 83.645 MHz median placement, Gowin reports 17.250 ns\\nfrom the reciprocal ROM through the shared DSP to residual_q and 17.244 ns\\nfrom residual_q into the signed divide result register. Pass 6 puts a\\n32-bit subtractor after the DSP. The following cycle can compare, subtract\\nand negate in series. CoreMark executes no divides in its timed loop,\\nbut both paths constrain the clock on every instruction.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep the seven-pass Newton schedule and retime its final correction. Let\\nA and B be the dividend and divisor magnitudes, Q the existing quotient\\nestimate and P = Q*B. For B > 0 the estimate is exact or one low, so\\nP fits in 32 bits. The correction test A-P >= B is equivalent to\\nA >= B and P <= A-B. Precompute A-B while the reciprocal refines.\\nRegister the low product bits directly from the DSP on pass 6. The next\\ncycle can compare P with A-B while forming both candidate remainders.\\n\\nFold sign restoration into those two parallel differences. For sign\\nflag n, form X + ~P + (1-n) for X equal to A and A-B. XOR the selected\\nword with a mask of n. This gives X-P for a positive result and P-X for\\na negative result. Compute the adjacent quotient on pass 6 so DIV and\\nREM share the final XOR. All arithmetic on result words wraps at 32 bits.\\n\\nThe scratch implementation completed ten CoreMark iterations in\\n3,081,605 bracketed cycles with both buses stalling and all five CRCs\\ncorrect. Gowin placements 0, 1 and 2 reached 83.100, 87.580 and\\n87.580 MHz. The median gives 284.20 iterations/s, 4.71% above 271.43.\\nThe promotion threshold is 283.92 iterations/s, so the timing margin\\nfor an independent implementation is narrow. Placement 0 used 3,601\\nlogic cells and 1,078 registers, up 15 and 65 respectively, with the\\nsame four DSPs and 13 block RAMs. On the median placement the longest\\npath now runs from the resolved load-buffer row to MEM/WB write data.\\n\\nThe prototype passed 30 ALU cases, including the proposed boundary test,\\n39 pipeline cases and all 53 fast ALTOPS formal checks. It matched\\nreference retirement on selftest and three random programs with and\\nwithout bus stalls. A bit-vector equivalence check found no difference\\nfrom the existing 32-bit correction for any P <= A, taking P = 0 for B = 0.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"On the existing factor_q write enable (passes 1 and 3), also latch\\na_minus_b_q <= {1'b0, dividend} - {1'b0, divisor}. Its top bit\\nrecords borrow. Replace the pass-6 residual_q write with two captures,\\nraw_product_q <= product[31:0] and\\nbumped_q <= quotient_q + (negate_q ? 32'hffff_ffff : 32'd1).\\nRetain quotient_q from pass 5. The tested output equations are\\n\\n  correct = !a_minus_b_q[32] && raw_product_q <= a_minus_b_q[31:0]\\n  r0 = dividend + ~raw_product_q + {31'b0, !negate_q}\\n  r1 = a_minus_b_q[31:0] + ~raw_product_q + {31'b0, !negate_q}\\n  q = (correct ^ negate_q) ? bumped_q : quotient_q\\n  word = ({32{want_rem_q && !correct}} & r0) |\\n         ({32{want_rem_q && correct}} & r1) | ({32{!want_rem_q}} & q)\\n  div_result = !want_rem_q && zero_q ? 32'hffff_ffff : word ^ {32\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
