{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-002-r1s1.yaml | head -46'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261120-002-r1s1\ntitle: \"Move DIV and REM to a shared four-pass divider\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The 5.442 MHz baseline takes 4,491,485 cycles for ten CoreMark iterations.\n  Gowin reports a 199.837 ns, 156-cell path through forwarding and the EX ALU.\n  The ALU evaluates full-width signed and unsigned division and remainder\n  combinationally. Their serial quotient decisions are the likeliest source\n  of this depth. Disassembly of the canonical RV32IM -O3 ELF shows five\n  DIVU instructions, all in main. The measured start_time/iterate/stop_time\n  sequence executes none of them, and the ELF contains no DIV, REM or REMU.\nhypothesis: |\n  Give EX one blocking divider for DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU. Latch forwarded\n  operands on issue, divide their unsigned magnitudes with eight restoring\n  quotient-bit steps per clock for four clocks, then select and sign-correct\n  the quotient or remainder. Use the subtraction borrow to select the next\n  remainder at each step. The register between passes cuts the full-width\n  recurrence out of the one-cycle ALU path. Only division and remainder add\n  issue stalls, so matrix multiplies and ordinary integer dependencies can\n  sustain their current issue rate.\n\n  Expect nearly the same marker-bracketed cycle count. Extra divide cycles\n  during setup can shift the phase of the pseudo-random bus stalls. At the\n  baseline cycle count, 8.163 MHz would yield a 50% fitness gain. Even with\n  2% more cycles, 5.81 MHz would clear the 4.6% acceptance margin. Compare\n  median Gowin Fmax and bracketed cycles separately to test this prediction.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 50\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace the four combinational divide/remainder arms with a clocked\n      request/result interface and one shared restoring datapath. Register\n      operand magnitudes, quotient, remainder, signs and pass count. Signed\n      division truncates toward zero and the remainder takes the dividend's\n      sign. A zero divisor returns an all-ones quotient or the original\n      dividend as remainder. INT_MIN divided by -1 returns INT_MIN with\n      zero remainder. In ALTOPS mode carry the per-op expression sampled at\n      issue through the same busy/result handshake and latency.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Start once for a valid divide when an older MEM request can advance.\n      Save the original forwarded rs1/rs2 for RVFI before older writers drain.\n      While waiting, emit EX/MEM bubbles with all side-effect controls clear.\n      On result acceptance, emit the held instruction and its result through\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
