{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_15", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20261121-001-r11s0.last cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-001-r11s0.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "Wrote and schema-validated [hyp-20261121-001-r11s0.yaml](/srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6_2-astra_xhigh-v2-rep3/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261121-001-r11s0-hypgen/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-001-r11s0.yaml). The hypothesis reads the successor RAM one fetch early so a register separates its output from fetch steering.\n\nThe first prototype validated all five CoreMark CRCs in 3,107,313 timed cycles. At that count, promotion requires a median Fmax above **84.162 MHz**. The revised prototype passed all 38 pipeline tests after fixing a stalled-fetch retry. Its CoreMark fitness remains unmeasured.id: hyp-20261121-001-r11s0\ntitle: Read the successor RAM one fetch early and register its response before decode\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  Four of the five longest paths on the champion's 79.301 MHz placement ran from the successor RAM to the fetch PC. The split-target experiment reached 80.074 MHz and put EX forwarding and address generation on the path to the RAM write enable. A scratch implementation that queued the writes and stored an absolute direct target preserved all five CRCs and 3,062,533 bracketed cycles, but reached only 76.294 MHz on placement 1. A register between the RAM output and fetch steering could shorten the read path while keeping training writes off the admission path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Look up the existing 512-row successor table one fetch earlier. While fetching A and predicting B, send B's nine index bits to the synchronous read port. During B the RAM presents the row for B, which contains its previously observed successor C and C's instruction. At the edge admitting B, capture this row in fabric flip-flops. During C, the saved instruction, its address and its direct-target metadata are available from those flip-flops. A warm stream still admits one instruction per clock.\n\n  The lookup is speculative and internal. Use the low bits of the sequential or predicted direct address without waiting for full target-range validation, EX redirect or the load-use interlock. Check the regis\n…[2085 of 5157 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:7f8dff0c4d231639]…\ne existing logical same-row collision veto. On reset or an admitted FENCE.I discard pending fills, probes and held responses and let the sweep take priority.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      On a direct direction miss, form the recovery PC from the issuing instruction's registered PC and immediate. Choose PC+4 for a predicted-taken miss and the full target for a predicted-fallthrough miss. This lets IF store only the aligned RAM target in a row. Keep the advancing-instruction qualification, JALR target comparison, trap handling and channel-0 retirement behavior.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise a warm direct-branch loop with the instruction bus held low, a redirect whose correct target has a different probe index, and a held probe overwritten before promotion. Cross a 512-row alias and admit FENCE.I with a queued fill. Check fetch recovery, exact channel-0 instructions, PCs, order and store effects, plus consecutive retirements on a warmed correct lookahead.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
