{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-001-r15s0.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261121-001-r15s0\ntitle: Compare the resolved load tag inside two asymmetric block RAMs\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  On the champion's 87.580 MHz median placement, Gowin's longest path runs from the resolved tag RAM through hit selection and load formatting to MEM/WB write_data. Its arrival is 16.712 ns. Registering two writeback sources in r13s0 put another select on EX forwarding and reduced Fmax to 86.589 MHz. The existing EX lookup can perform the tag comparison as part of the memory read.\nhypothesis: |\n  Encode the eight-bit tag of each resolved load-buffer row as two one-hot nibbles. Use two GW2A semi-dual-port block RAMs. Each has a 1024 by 16 write port and a 16384 by 1 read port. On a valid row update, write 1 shifted by tag[3:0] to the first RAM and 1 shifted by tag[7:4] to the second. On invalidation, write zeros to both. On an EX probe, concatenate the ten-bit resolved row index with the respective four-bit requested tag digit to address each narrow read port. At MEM, both returned bits must be set and resolved_available_q must qualify the read. This fits in one LUT and gives the exact original hit decision. The write replaces both entire masks, so an evicted tag cannot produce a stale hit.\n  \n  Use this encoding only for the resolved tag check. The early bypass still uses its binary tag. The raw data read, load formatter, single WB result, port hold enables and same-index collision veto retain their existing timing in cycles. An exhaustive truth-table check covered all 131,072 combinations of validity, stored tag and requested tag.\n  \n  At 3,081,605 cycles per ten iterations, promotion needs a median above 91.61 MHz. The six-percent estimate corresponds to about 93 MHz. The timing risk is that address bits [19:12] now have to reach the block RAM address pins during EX. The champion's 16.710 ns EX result path also competes with the saved tag delay.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Drive both tag planes from the existing accepted row update and sweep. Gate the narrow reads with the resolved data read enable. Combine the two match bits with the registered in-range, alignment, sweep and collision qualification. Keep partial-store invalidation, demand and MMIO handshakes, and RVFI raw read data intact.\n  - file: rtl/load_tag_plane.sv\n    description: |\n      Implement one 16-to-1 asymmetric synchronous RAM plane. For the GW2A SDPB, use 16-bit writes at {write_index,4'b0} and one-bit reads at {resolved_index,requested_nibble}, with a single synchronous read and no extra output stage. Verify the widths and block mapping in the synthesis report. Use the identical addressing, latency and hold semantics in simulation and formal.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise the same row with tags 0x3a, 0x35 and 0xca so each individual nibble matches a stale tag in one case. Check external read counts, demand data and exact RVFI values after replacement, a partial store and a held read.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
