{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-002-r14s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261121-002-r14s1\ntitle: Issue from registered two-instruction path packets\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's 3,081,605 cycles leave little room for a pure IPC gain.\n  In a scratch experiment, parallel sequential and taken fetch tables\n  completed ten iterations in 3,034,084 cycles with all five CRCs correct.\n  Placement 1 reached only 79.985 MHz. Its longest path ran from the\n  sequential instruction RAM through decode to the early load index.\n  A four-word aligned block in round 10 also paid demand waits at block\n  boundaries. Two successive instructions on the executed path provide\n  two issue cycles in which to fetch and register the following pair,\n  including when a taken branch separates the words.\n\n  A retirement-trace replay of the 512-row sliding table reproduced both\n  following PCs at 2,707,544 of 2,922,015 sampled positions (92.66%),\n  counting head-tag misses as failures. This measures dynamic path reuse.\n  The trace model omits RAM port conflicts, speculative fetches and the\n  extra time between fetch and training in MEM.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the per-instruction successor lookup with a 512-entry synchronous\n  table of overlapping instruction pairs. For three consecutive ordered MEM\n  transfers P, Q, R in aligned RAM, write one row indexed by P[10:2].\n  It contains P's high address tag, the words at P and Q, Q's word address\n  and R's word address. R is the head to probe after this pair. Slide by one\n  transfer so either instruction of a hot loop can become the next head.\n\n  Issue P and Q from a pair held in fabric registers. While issuing P,\n  launch the synchronous read for R using the recorded index. The RAM\n  delivers its response during Q. At the end of Q, load the next pair\n  into the issue registers if the requested head matches. This schedule\n  sustains one instruction per clock across a warm packet boundary and\n  puts a flip-flop between the RAM output and the register-file read or\n  speculative load-buffer index. Use a ready bus word at the presented\n  PC with\n…[1667 of 4739 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:019a65528fc5ea79]…\n description: Carry the exact predicted successor and fall-through alongside the instruction through fetch and ID/EX.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Latch the fetch prediction and fall-through on the same advance as their instruction and operands.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Check the recorded prediction against the full resolved RV32IM next PC and use the matching fall-through for link writes and trap recovery.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Connect packet admission, replay, MEM transfers and redirect recovery to the existing single-retire pipeline.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Warm a pair that crosses a taken branch and retire it and the next pair\n      consecutively with imemReady low. Hold each slot across a data wait\n      and replay a dependent second slot. Exercise a changed branch outcome\n      with a wrong-path store, a head-index alias, and FENCE.I with a pending\n      response. Check channel-0 order, PC, instruction and memory effects.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
