{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r14s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261121-003-r14s2\ntitle: Cache the successor instruction's own branch target\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's row for A records the last instruction B reached from A.\n  When B is a conditional branch, a fall-through can overwrite B's own\n  row and lose its taken target. Separate outcome banks in r13s2 saved\n  26,223 cycles but still needed EX to form and check every direct target.\n  The target of a direct branch is fixed by its PC and instruction bits.\n  A row that already identifies both can carry the exact target into EX.\nhypothesis: |\n  Extend each A-to-B row with B's outgoing target and an eligibility bit.\n  For a legal direct branch or JAL at B, store the aligned in-RAM value\n  B.pc + B.imm even when B fell through. This takes 19 additional bits in\n  each of the 512 rows. While fetching B, latch the row's target alongside\n  the existing direction prediction. An exact match on B's full PC and\n  instruction certifies that target for direct-branch resolution. Keep\n  the observed JALR destination as a prediction verified from forwarded rs1.\n\n  Move the direct-target adder from EX to MEM. EX can resolve a certified\n  direct transfer with the registered target and the actual branch condition.\n  For an aligned, actually taken direct transfer without a certified target,\n  kill the younger slot, compute the full target in MEM and redirect from\n  a register one clock later. A correctly predicted fall-through can advance\n  at full rate regardless of whether its target was cached.\n\n  A scratch predictor with target metadata and the original EX adder took\n  3,069,283 bracketed cycles with both buses stalling and all five CRCs\n  correct. The PC and instruction matched on 334,111 of 343,792 taken\n  direct transfers (97.18%). 