{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'tail -c 5500 cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'", "aggregated_output": "ished canceling that producer. Check\\nthe claim in parallel against the actual advancing writer. If a source\\nclaimed a canceled writer, mark the consumer for the existing one-slot\\nreplay, just as for an unverified load. Kill the consumer before any\\nside effect. Suppress the invalid EX writer's tag during replay so a\\nself-dependent instruction can reacquire its older value. An older\\nredirect takes priority. Hold the claim and its verification together\\nunder data waits, and let the existing divide interlock govern issue.\\n\\nThe prepared-operand and shared-adder prototype reproduced all five\\nCoreMark CRCs in 3,081,605 bracketed cycles. Gowin placement 1 reached\\n88.344 MHz. Registering a new operand exposes a path from fetch\\ncorrection through the register file to ID/EX. Splitting write-through\\nfrom the RF array in issue reached only 76.833 MHz. Keep the champion's\\nwrite-first RF and move the late producer cancellation to replay.\\nAt the original cycle count promotion requires a median above 91.61\\nMHz. The 6% forecast assumes about 93 MHz and few canceled claims.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the prepared ALU operand and integer result enables to id_ex_t.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture the immediate or masked write-first RF word and the result\\nenables on issue. Export the registered writer tag before applying\\nthe combinational squash to the architectural valid bit.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the registered producer tag for issue-stage source matching.\\nRegister the operand-specific bypass claims with their consumer.\\nDetect when a selected tag was canceled and route the conflict to\\nreplay. Preserve the independent store payload and load index hint.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch a canceled-source conflict as a replay cause, independent of\\nsuccessful early-load forwarding. On retry ignore the rejected\\nwriter and reacquire sources. \n…[2428 of 5500 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:96a0d66243d7e4a0]…\nitness\": 285.67, \"delta_pct\": 0.52, \"vs_baseline\": 2257.01, \"fmax_mhz\": 88.1, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3084030, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 3775, \"ff\": 1112, \"seeds\": [77.194, 88.1, 88.1], \"perturbations\": [[0, 77.194, 11], [1, 88.1, 11], [2, 88.1, 11]], \"lutram\": 36, \"bsram\": 13, \"dsp\": 5, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_ex/fence_q_s0\", \"to\": \"cpu/early_index_q_7_s0\", \"slack_ns\": -7.954, \"arrival_ns\": 18.249, \"logic_cells\": 13, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_if\", \"cpu/u_rf\", \"cpu\"]}, \"logic_levels\": 11, \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-21T08:38:59.263596Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 15, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r15s1 (regression, +0.52%): Preparing the second ALU operand in ID/EX left the path from registered branch correction through instruction selection and the register-file read to the early load-buffer index as the median timing limit, at 88.100 MHz versus the 91.68 MHz needed for promotion.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r15s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261121-003-r15s2\ntitle: Predict counted loop exits one visit ahead\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's 64 agree counters can learn that a backedge usually repeats,\n  but they carry no information about which iteration exits. Round 13 saved\n  26,223 cycles by retaining both successor words. With both words available,\n  a predictor that recognizes a repeating trip count can supply the exit\n  instruction even during an instruction-bus wait.\nhypothesis: |\n  Compute the direction for the next visit when a conditional branch transfers\n  through MEM. Publish that one bit in a 512-entry table indexed by PC[10:2].\n  For backward branches keep 64 entries indexed by PC[7:2], each holding a\n  five-bit count of consecutive taken outcomes, the previous completed count,\n  and one confidence bit. At an exit, set confidence when two successive\n  completed runs have the same length from 1 through 30. Saturate at 31.\n  Reset the running count on exit. After a taken outcome that reaches the\n  learned count, publish fall-through for the next visit. Use the updated\n  two-bit per-PC direction counter for all other cases. A changed trip count\n  or index alias can cost a redirect. EX still resolves every branch.\n\n  Read separate 512-row taken and fall-through successor banks in parallel\n  with the direction bit. Train each observed edge with the actual successor\n  PC and word from consecutive valid MEM transfers. Gate steering with the\n  opcode actually admitted, the registered prediction and taken-row presence.\n  Legal jumps request the taken row unconditionally. Pipeline training writes\n  so each physical RAM sees a registered index, payload and enable. The loop\n  counter and equality test are outside the fetch-to-fetch recurrence.\n\n  A scratch implementation with both buses stalling took 3,031,705 bracketed\n  cycles with all five canonical CRCs and the validation banner. Disabling\n  the loop override on the same two-bank predictor took 3,046,148 cycles.\n  The champion took 3,081,605. At 3,031,705 cycles promotion requires a\n  median above 90.12 MHz. The 5% estimate assumes 90.5 MHz. Routing the\n  selected word to decode and the independent EX forwarding path are the\n  main timing risks.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Add the run-length learner, direction counters and published direction\n      bits. Train once per accepted MEM transfer. Split the successor storage\n      by observed outcome and register the fills, including the taken presence\n      update. Check collisions against the physical write in that cycle.\n      For a stalled fetch, supply a saved word only for the full selected\n      aligned RAM address. Keep the forecast and word paired through holds,\n      replay and redirects. Flush pending fills and invalidate both banks,\n      presence and held words on reset or FENCE.I.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Pass the existing EX/MEM branch type, jump type, trap status and actual\n      taken bit to IF alongside mem_transfer, mem_pc and mem_instr. Preserve\n      the single-channel retirement interface.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Remove the old EX direction-counter trainer. Retain the registered\n      direction and full destination checks against the forecast actually used,\n      including JALR and misaligned transfers.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Remove the retired predictor's train_count snapshot from the fetch and decode bundles.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Remove the corresponding ID/EX snapshot assignment.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Warm a backward loop with a fixed count, stall imem at its learned exit\n      and check the exit PC and instruction. Change the count and exercise\n      one-trip and saturated loops, an alias, a held MEM transfer and FENCE.I\n      with a pending fill. Check RVFI order and absence of wrong-path stores.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
