{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_15", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r8s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261121-003-r8s2\ntitle: Predict the quotient with a piecewise-linear reciprocal and one residual check\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion takes 3,097,817 cycles for ten CoreMark iterations at 71.595 MHz.\n  Its worst reported path goes from the reciprocal seed ROM to the quotient\n  register. The timed loop contains no divide instructions, but this path\n  sets a clock constraint on every iteration. A more accurate initial\n  reciprocal prediction can free two of the seven divide clocks for product\n  registers while preserving the issue rate of ordinary multiplies.\nhypothesis: |\n  Predict the unsigned quotient from a linear approximation to the reciprocal\n  of the normalized divisor. For nonzero divisor magnitude B, set k=floor(log2 B),\n  N=B<<(31-k), x=N/2^31, and j=513+N[30:22]. The upper edge of x's bin is\n  u=j/512. Store floor(2^20/u) and floor(2^16/u^2) in one coefficient ROM.\n  The tangent at u gives a lower bound on 1/x. With t=2047-N[21:11], form\n  P=intercept+((t*slope)>>16). This predicts 1/x in units of 2^-20.\n\n  Use S=2^34. Perform one downward-rounded Newton refinement by computing\n  E=2*S-1-floor(N*P/2^17) and R=floor(P*E/2^20). Predict the quotient as\n  Q=floor((A>>k)*R/S) for dividend magnitude A. Check the full residual\n  T=A-Q*B. If T>=B, increment Q and subtract B from T. Restore the sign\n  after correction and select the specified RV32M result for a zero divisor.\n\n  The tangent error is at most 2^-18. Rounding the intercept, distance,\n  slope and product adds at most 3*2^-20+2^-25. Thus the refined reciprocal\n  underestimates by less than 2*(2^-18+3*2^-20+2^-25)^2+2/S < 2^-32.\n  Discarding the bottom k dividend bits costs at most 1-2^-k quotient\n  units and reciprocal error on A>>k costs less than 2^-k. The prediction\n  is exact or one low, so one check suffices for every nonzero divisor.\n\n  An exhaustive integer sweep of all 2^31 normalized divisors found a maximum\n  reciprocal underestimate of 0.655 times 2^-32. A scratch prototype with\n  one shared wide multiplier ran te\n…[1027 of 4099 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:eec12bb5872b4766]…\nw word\n      of Q*B, and (7) the residual. Pre-shift A while computing P and save\n      Q separately at step five. Zero-extend each unsigned divide factor.\n      ISA multiplies load their signed or unsigned operands into the same\n      two registers on issue. Use fixed product slices for scaling. The\n      interpolation product is separate. Preserve request/result timing,\n      held results, the ALTOPS controller and issue-sampled operands.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Compare DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU against exact integer arithmetic at\n      both ends of coefficient bins and around multiples of B. Include\n      cases requiring a correction, discarded dividend bits at their\n      maximum, zero divisors, signed overflow and held results. For\n      A=0xffffffff and B=7 the trial residual must correct from 10 to 3.\n      Exercise the worst-error normalized divisor 0x82801000 with A equal\n      to B and on either side. Retain\n      the seven-clock latency and adjacent multiply issue checks.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
