th io_imemAddr and the PC passed to ID and saving it for retry on stalls eliminated the taken-redirect bubble, cutting CoreMark cycles by 7.91%.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r3s2 (improvement, +13.68%): Latching the direction IF used and the alternate PC f\n…[3947 of 7019 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:71f497d5b28774f2]…\nhe quotient estimate exact or one low, so a fixed product slice and one residual correction replaced the Newton feedback path that limited CoreMark's clock.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-003-r8s2 (regression, +5.70%): A 512-bin tangent seed eliminated the second Newton refinement, freeing two divider clocks to register the shared multiplier inputs and the quotient product before residual correction and raising Fmax at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-001-r9s0 (improvement, +5.45%): A dedicated EX multiplier removed the divider seed ROM from the multiply writeback path, and a one-hot bypass selected in decode let the next instruction use the registered product without a stall.\n- 2026-11-21 hyp-20261121-002-r9s1 (regression, -1.69%): Registering the low and high DSP product words separately at MEM/WB widened each EX bypass mux to five inputs, putting the rs2 high-product select on the critical path to fetch and lowering median Fmax from 76.071 to 74.789 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
