{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_65", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-001-r4s0.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261120-001-r4s0\ntitle: Forward completed loads directly to RV32I consumers\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion bubbles ID/EX on a load-use match even when data RAM can\n  deliver the value during the consumer's EX cycle. Removing those bubbles\n  can save 204,397 of its 4,194,434 bracketed cycles. Timing determines\n  whether that saving pays off. The champion's worst path crosses a\n  multiplier DSP on its way to the RAM address input, and routing live\n  load data through that same path makes the clock slower.\nhypothesis: |\n  Let an RV32I consumer enter EX immediately behind a load. Its MEM-to-EX\n  forwarding input supplies the aligned, sign- or zero-extended value\n  that the load will write back. A data-bus wait holds the consumer until\n  the load completes. Retain the one-cycle interlock for a dependent\n  RV32M instruction so the multiplier can use registered producer data.\n  Form each memory address with a dedicated 32-bit base-plus-immediate\n  adder. These two routing decisions keep the live load bypass out of\n  the multiplier and the multiplier out of the RAM address path.\n\n  An isolated prototype completed ten iterations in 3,990,037 bracketed\n  cycles with both bus stalls enabled and all five canonical CRCs correct.\n  Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 50.183, 50.429 and 50.429 MHz.\n  That median gives 126.39 iterations/s, 6.59% above 118.57. At this cycle\n  count the acceptance threshold requires more than 49.486 MHz. The\n  prototype's limiting path is now the divider's remainder feedback.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Add a full-width effective_addr field to ex_mem_t.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Use the MEM bypass value for both ordinary forwarded operands,\n      including store data, branch comparisons and captured RVFI source\n      values. Register forwarded rs1 + in.imm as effective_addr on the\n      same transfer as the memory controls. For the two multiply inputs,\n      reuse the existing forwarding selects with EX/MEM.alu_result,\n      WB write data and ID/EX operands as their three data sources.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Export mem_read ? load_data : alu_result for forwarding. Export\n      ctrl_with_trap.reg_write as the MEM producer qualification so a\n      misaligned load cannot shadow an older writer. Use effective_addr\n      for the bus address, alignment, byte masks, extraction and RVFI.\n      Continue to hold the previous WB forwarding payload on a dmem wait.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire the MEM value and post-trap write qualification to the EX\n      forwarding network. Supply EX/MEM.alu_result separately for multiply.\n      Qualify the hazard unit's EX-load input with the fetched word's\n      RV32M encoding (opcode 0x33 and funct7 0x01), retaining the existing\n      rd, x0 and source-match checks. Preserve divider and bus hold priority.\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Feed the three existing signed, unsigned and mixed-sign products\n      from the dedicated multiply operands. Use those same two operands\n      in the four multiply ALTOPS expressions. Keep the four result\n      selections, signedness rules and divider handshake intact.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Drive the dedicated multiply inputs in the existing MUL-family\n      vectors. Exercise different values on the ordinary ALU inputs to\n      detect accidental selection of the live load bypass.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Check consecutive retirement on a zero-wait LW-to-ADD pair and\n      check forwarding through a stalled signed byte load and an unsigned\n      halfword load. Exercise a load feeding the next memory address or\n      store data, a trapping load shadowing an older writer, and a\n      load-to-MULH pair that still interlocks. Check RVFI operands,\n      results, order and the count and data of accepted stores.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
