or retry on stalls eliminated the taken-redirect bubble, cutting CoreMark cycles by 7.91%.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r3s2 (improvement, +13.68%): Latching the direction IF used and the alternate PC for branches and JAL in ID/EX lets EX redirect on a one-bit mismatch while decode reads operands in parallel, preserving the predictor's roughly 297,000-cycle saving over ten CoreMark iterations.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r4s0 (regression, +7.47%): Forwarding extended load data from MEM to RV32I consumers in EX saved 204,397 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations, while retaining the RV32M load-use interlock and using a dedicated address adder removed the RAM-to-RAM timing path through the multiplier.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r4s1 (regression, +18.71%): Registering forwarded operands and reusing one 36×36 multiplier for Newton division broke the register-file-to-DSP-to-RAM path and removed five serial divider steps from the feedback path, so the Fmax gain outweighed the 4.49% CoreMark cycle cost of blocking EX on multiplies.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r4s2 (improvement, +18.94%): Caching the successor PC and instruction together under the predecessor PC let IF supply 604,950 instructions on full address matches during CoreMark imem waits and correctly predict 12,072 of 13,735 JALR targets with the same lookup.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
