{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor x in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n print(x[\\\"id\\\"],x[\\\"title\\\"],x.get(\\\"category\\\"),x.get(\\\"cycles\\\"),x.get(\\\"fmax_mhz\\\"))\n if x[\\\"id\\\"].endswith(\\\"r4s0\\\"): print(x[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"baseline-bench-4f20506 Baseline retest for cores/bench micro_opt 4491485 5.442\nhyp-20261120-001-r1s0 Resolve two quotient bits per combinational divider stage micro_opt 4491485 6.627\nhyp-20261120-002-r1s1 Move DIV and REM to a shared four-pass divider structural 4491485 31.773\nhyp-20261120-003-r1s2 Predict direct branches with a 64-entry agree predictor at fetch predictor 4194484 5.648\nhyp-20261120-001-r2s0 Shorten the divider feedback path to five quotient bits per clock micro_opt 4491485 46.846\nhyp-20261120-002-r2s1 Fold the divider into seven five-bit passes structural 4491485 46.846\nhyp-20261120-003-r2s2 Predict the next fetch from a 128-entry branch target buffer predictor 4180499 31.421\nhyp-20261120-001-r3s0 Remove EX redirect from register reads and load-use comparison micro_opt 4491485 49.594\nhyp-20261120-002-r3s1 Fetch the resolved target in the EX redirect cycle structural 4136096 46.659\nhyp-20261120-003-r3s2 Predict direct transfers with a one-bit EX recovery decision predictor 4194434 49.733\nhyp-20261120-001-r4s0 Forward completed loads directly to RV32I consumers micro_opt 3990037 50.844\nLet an RV32I consumer enter EX immediately behind a load. Its MEM-to-EX\nforwarding input supplies the aligned, sign- or zero-extended value\nthat the load will write back. A data-bus wait holds the consumer until\nthe load completes. Retain the one-cycle interlock for a dependent\nRV32M instruction so the multiplier can use registered producer data.\nForm each memory address with a dedicated 32-bit base-plus-immediate\nadder. These two routing decisions keep the live load bypass out of\nthe multiplier and the multiplier out of the RAM address path.\n\nAn isolated prototype completed ten iterations in 3,990,037 bracketed\ncycles with both bus stalls enabled and all five canonical CRCs correct.\nGowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 50.183, 50.429 and 50.429 MHz.\nThat median gives 126.39 iterations/s, 6.59% above 118.57. Placement\noption 0 used 2,876 logic cells and 632 registers, up from 2,735 and\n600. The limiting path now runs through the divider's remainder feedback.\nThe prototype passed all 53 fast formal checks and 89 cocotb cases.\nThe selftest and three random programs matched the reference retirement\ntraces with both zero-wait and stalled buses.\n\nhyp-20261120-002-r4s1 Share one registered multiplier across all eight RV32M operations structural 4382865 61.695\nhyp-20261120-003-r4s2 Predict the successor PC and instruction with one lookup predictor 3408562 48.072\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
