{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_56", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-001-r6s0.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261120-001-r6s0\ntitle: Register operand values and forwarding selections together at decode\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  Gowin reports a 22.304 ns path from register-file block RAM through EX\n  to data RAM on the 60.061 MHz champion. Block RAM supplies the register\n  operand after the decode clock edge. EX also compares producer register\n  numbers, resolves their priority and selects the operand before doing the\n  operation. Both the operand value and its source can be prepared in decode.\nhypothesis: |\n  Read both register operands asynchronously from distributed RAM, apply\n  the WB write-first bypass and capture them in the existing ID/EX operand\n  registers. In parallel, compare each fetched source with the destinations\n  currently in EX and MEM. Register a three-bit one-hot selection per source\n  at the same edge. In EX, mask and OR the captured operand, EX/MEM ALU\n  result and MEM/WB write data. Each bypass still delivers its value at the\n  original clock. Moving the read and priority decision before the edge\n  shortens the path through EX for memory, arithmetic and branch consumers.\n\n  An isolated prototype reached 66.872, 67.927 and 67.927 MHz under the\n  three Gowin placement options. The median and 3,444,219 bracketed cycles\n  for ten CRC-validated iterations with both buses stalling give 197.22\n  iterations/s, 13.1% above 174.38. Placement zero used 3,331 logic cells,\n  752 registers, seven BSRAMs and two DSPs. Its longest path moved into the\n  divider. The prototype matched reference retirement on selftest and all\n  three random programs with zero-wait and stalled buses.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 13\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: |\n      Infer a two-read, one-write 32x32 array with the Gowin\n      syn_ramstyle=\"distributed_ram\" attribute and clocked nonzero-rd writes.\n      Put synchronous reset on a separate 32-bit written mask. Clear all\n      mask bits on reset and set the addressed bit on a nonzero write. A\n      clear bit reads as\n…[1124 of 4196 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e4e44d95535c00fc]…\nn, stores and the original RVFI operands captured on divide\n      issue. A flushed entry already carries zero write control.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Export ctrl_with_trap.reg_write as the qualification that will enter\n      MEM/WB on advance. This accounts for a misaligned load when resolving\n      competing writers. Keep the held WB data available to the EX operand\n      mux throughout a data-bus wait, including cycles after WB has retired.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise simultaneous matches from two writers to one rd, with the two\n      consumer operands using different sources. Include a trapping writer\n      shadowing an older write and a consumer held behind a waiting memory\n      operation after WB retires. Check RVFI source values and writeback.\n  - file: test/test_reg_file.py\n    description: |\n      Populate registers, assert reset again, then rewrite one register.\n      Check both read ports for the new value, zero in stale rows and x0.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
