{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_29", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor s in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(s)\n if r['id'] in ['hyp-20261120-001-r5s0','hyp-20261120-001-r4s0','hyp-20261120-003-r5s2','hyp-20261120-003-r4s2']:\n  print(r['id']);print(r['hypothesis']['hypothesis']);print(r['changes'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-001-r4s0\nLet an RV32I consumer enter EX immediately behind a load. Its MEM-to-EX\nforwarding input supplies the aligned, sign- or zero-extended value\nthat the load will write back. A data-bus wait holds the consumer until\nthe load completes. Retain the one-cycle interlock for a dependent\nRV32M instruction so the multiplier can use registered producer data.\nForm each memory address with a dedicated 32-bit base-plus-immediate\nadder. These two routing decisions keep the live load bypass out of\nthe multiplier and the multiplier out of the RAM address path.\n\nAn isolated prototype completed ten iterations in 3,990,037 bracketed\ncycles with both bus stalls enabled and all five canonical CRCs correct.\nGowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 50.183, 50.429 and 50.429 MHz.\nThat median gives 126.39 iterations/s, 6.59% above 118.57. Placement\noption 0 used 2,876 logic cells and 632 registers, up from 2,735 and\n600. The limiting path now runs through the divider's remainder feedback.\nThe prototype passed all 53 fast formal checks and 89 cocotb cases.\nThe selftest and three random programs matched the reference retirement\ntraces with both zero-wait and stalled buses.\n\n[{'file': 'rtl/core_pkg.sv', 'description': 'Add a full-width effective_addr field to ex_mem_t.'}, {'file': 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'description': 'Use the MEM bypass value for both ordinary forwarded operands,\\nincluding store data, branch comparisons and captured RVFI source\\nvalues. Register forwarded rs1 + in.imm as effective_addr on the\\nsame transfer as the memory controls. For the two multiply inputs,\\nreuse the existing forwarding selects with EX/MEM.alu_result,\\nWB write data and ID/EX operands as their three data sources.\\n'}, {'file': 'rtl/mem_stage.sv', 'description': 'Export mem_read ? load_data : alu_result for forwarding. Export\\nctrl_with_trap.reg_write as the MEM producer qualification so a\\nmisaligned load cannot shadow an older writer. Use effective_addr\\nfor the bus address, alignment, byte masks, extraction and RVFI.\\nContinue to ho\n…[13380 of 16452 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a92c54ae5ca148e4]…\nmatch select the dedicated load result for a\\nload and the normal ALU result for other writers. Retain EX/MEM\\npriority over WB and x0 exclusion. The interlock keeps a consumer\\nof a missed load out of EX until WB can supply the demand value.\\n'}, {'file': 'rtl/mem_stage.sv', 'description': 'Use the registered effective address for the bus, alignment checks\\nand RVFI masks. An early hit suppresses io_dmemREn, writes back the\\nregistered extended value and reports its raw word to RVFI. A miss\\nsamples and extends the accepted data-bus word as before.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'After warmup, run two dependent pointer loads feeding an ADD and\\ncheck three consecutive retirements with the data bus unavailable.\\nGive an ADD producer stale latched operands, delay the true demand\\nread and check that the younger load and consumer use the corrected\\nword. Cover high-address tag aliases, a held partial store to a hot\\nword, subword extension, misalignment, read strobes and RVFI order.\\n'}]\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
