{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r1s0 (regression, +21.70%): Resolving two quotient bits per stage raised median Fmax from 5.442 to 6.627 MHz, but 16 serial remainder selections still limited the EX clock even though the timed CoreMark loop executes no divides.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r1s1 (improvement, +483.66%): Splitting DIV/REM across four registered passes shortened the EX timing path to eight quotient decisions per pass, and the timed CoreMark loop incurred no divider stalls because it executes neither operation.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r1s2 (regression, +11.14%): The 64-entry agree predictor and direct JAL prediction in IF saved 297,001 CoreMark cycles by avoiding one-cycle EX redirect bubbles, while the critical path through forwarding and the EX ALU still limited median Fmax to 5.648 MHz.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r2s0 (improvement, +47.44%): Padding the 32-bit dividend with three leading zeros let seven uniform passes cut the divider feedback path from eight serial subtract-and-select steps to five, raising Fmax without adding divider stalls to the timed CoreMark loop.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r2s2 (regression, +6.25%): Caching targets by fetch PC cut CoreMark JALR redirects from 13,735 to 1,647 in the 128-entry BTB prototype, but shortening the four-pass divider's critical path delivered a larger throughput gain (+47.44%).\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r3s0 (regression, +5.87%): Squashing via ID/EX.valid let register reads and load-use detection use the raw fetch word in parallel with EX branch resolution, raising median Fmax from 46.846 to 49.594 MHz while ten CoreMark iterations still took 4,491,485 cycles.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r3s1 (regression, +8.16%): On a same-cycle instruction bus, using the resolved EX target for both io_imemAddr and the PC passed to ID and saving it for retry on stalls eliminated the taken-redirect bubble, cutting CoreMark cycles by 7.91%.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r3s2 (improvement, +13.68%): Latching the direction IF used and the alternate PC for branches and JAL in ID/EX lets EX redirect on a one-bit mismatch while decode reads operands in parallel, preserving the predictor's roughly 297,000-cycle saving over ten CoreMark iterations.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r4s0 (regression, +7.47%): Forwarding extended load data from MEM to RV32I consumers in EX saved 204,397 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations, while retaining the RV32M load-use interlock and using a dedicated address adder removed the RAM-to-RAM timing path through the multiplier.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r4s1 (regression, +18.71%): Registering forwarded operands and reusing one 36×36 multiplier for Newton division broke the register-file-to-DSP-to-RAM path and removed five serial divider steps from the feedback path, so the Fmax gain outweighed the 4.49% CoreMark cycle cost of blocking EX on multiplies.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r4s2 (improvement, +18.94%): Caching the successor PC and instruction together under the predecessor PC let IF supply 604,950 instructions on full address matches during CoreMark imem waits and correctly predict 12,072 of 13,735 JALR targets with the same lookup.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-001-r5s0 (regression, +14.93%): Latching both source-register matches in ID/EX moved dependency checks off the load return path, letting RV32I consumers use ready MEM data in EX without a bubble and saving 204,374 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r5s1 (improvement, +23.65%): Computing products in MEM on the multiplier shared with Newton division kept the DSP off the EX path and let independent multiplies issue every clock, while an immediate product consumer needed just one stall before WB forwarding.\n- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-003-r5s2 (regression, +15.69%): Probing the word buffer in ID with low bits from an in-flight base producer let EX verify the full address and forward hits without a load-use bubble, saving 192,354 cycles over register-file-only indexing in ten CoreMark iterations.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
