{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_16", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nds=[json.loads(s) for s in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')]\nfor d in ds[-6:]:\n print(d['id'],d['critical_path'],d['fitness'],d['cycles'])\n if d['slot']==0:print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-001-r4s0 {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu/divisor_q_5_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu/remainder_q_4_s0', 'slack_ns': -15.095, 'arrival_ns': 25.389, 'logic_cells': 154, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu']} 127.43 3990037\nLet an RV32I consumer enter EX immediately behind a load. Its MEM-to-EX\nforwarding input supplies the aligned, sign- or zero-extended value\nthat the load will write back. A data-bus wait holds the consumer until\nthe load completes. Retain the one-cycle interlock for a dependent\nRV32M instruction so the multiplier can use registered producer data.\nForm each memory address with a dedicated 32-bit base-plus-immediate\nadder. These two routing decisions keep the live load bypass out of\nthe multiplier and the multiplier out of the RAM address path.\n\nAn isolated prototype completed ten iterations in 3,990,037 bracketed\ncycles with both bus stalls enabled and all five canonical CRCs correct.\nGowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 50.183, 50.429 and 50.429 MHz.\nThat median gives 126.39 iterations/s, 6.59% above 118.57. Placement\noption 0 used 2,876 logic cells and 632 registers, up from 2,735 and\n600. The limiting path now runs through the divider's remainder feedback.\nThe prototype passed all 53 fast formal checks and 89 cocotb cases.\nThe selftest and three random programs matched the reference retirement\ntraces with both zero-wait and stalled buses.\n\nhyp-20261120-002-r4s1 {'from': 'cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s', 'to': 'dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -11.808, 'arrival_ns': 22.102, 'logic_cells': 15, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']} 140.76 4382865\nhyp-20261120-003-r4s2 {'from': 'cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0', 'to': 'dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -16.773, 'arrival_ns': 27.067, 'logic_cells': 16, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']} 141.03 3408562\nhyp-20261120-001-r5s0 {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu/remainder_q_0_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu/quotient_q_0_s1', 'slack_ns': -15.045, 'arrival_ns': 25.339, 'logic_cells': 151, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu']} 162.08 3204188\nCompare the two source fields of the fetched instruction with the load\ndestination in EX. For an RV32I consumer, latch the matches as two bits\nalongside the consumer in ID/EX. On the next cycle each bit can select\nthe formatted MEM load value directly. This overlaps dependency detection\nwith address generation and lets a ready load and its consumer advance on\nconsecutive edges. Keep the load interlock for an immediately dependent\nRV32M instruction so the multiplier receives registered producer data.\nGive memory operations their own registered base-plus-immediate address\nto keep the multiply result mux out of the data RAM address path.\n\nAn isolated prototype on the current champion ran ten CRC-validated\niterations in 3,204,188 bracketed cycles with both stall generators\nenabled, saving 204,374 cycles. Gowin placements 0, 1 and 2 reached\n50.935, 52.031 and 52.031 MHz. The median gives 162.38 iterations/s,\n15.14% above 141.03. At that cycle count, 47.27 MHz clears the acceptance\nmargin. Divider feedback now sets the longest path. A load-to-fetch\ncontrol path is within 0.06 ns of it on placement 1.\n\nAll 53 fast formal checks passed. The selftest and three random programs\nmatched the reference with ready and stalled buses. Directed pipeline\ntests also passed for a saved consumer behind a waiting load, a trapping\nload shadowing an older writer, and a load followed by MULH.\n\nhyp-20261120-002-r5s1 {'from': 'cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0', 'to': 'dmem_0