{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_79","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-002-r6s1.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261120-002-r6s1\ntitle: Collect register operands and select their forwarding sources in decode\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion runs ten CoreMark iterations in 3,444,219 cycles at\n  60.061 MHz. All 25 setup paths in the placement-0 timing report start\n  at a register-file block RAM output. The worst passes through operand\n  forwarding and the ALU to data RAM. Others reach the fetch PC or the\n  ALU result register, so improving the shared operand path can shorten\n  several paths at once.\nhypothesis: |\n  Use ID/EX as the boundary for both register reads and forwarding\n  decisions. Read two 16-word distributed-RAM banks in ID and latch both\n  source values. In parallel, compare each source with rd in EX and MEM\n  and latch which producer will supply it on the next cycle. EX then\n  selects among its registered operand, EX/MEM.alu_result and WB data\n  with parallel masks. This removes the block RAM read and the\n  source-address comparisons from the execute path without adding a\n  pipeline stage.\n\n  A scratch prototype ran ten CRC-validated iterations in 3,444,219\n  bracketed cycles with both stall generators enabled. The three Gowin\n  placements reached 68.416, 66.679 and 66.679 MHz. The median gives\n  193.60 iterations/s, 11.0% above 174.38. All four RVFI cosim programs\n  matched the reference with both ready and stalled buses.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 11\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: |\n      Split x0–x31 into two 16x32 arrays indexed by address bits 3:0.\n      Use distributed RAM with two combinational reads and one write.\n      Reset a per-register written bitmap so unread initialized values\n      return zero. Drop x0 writes and preserve write-first bypass on\n      both ports. Only the bitmap needs clearing on reset.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Feed the existing forwarding comparator with the raw ID source\n      addresses and the destinations of the instructions currently in EX\n      and MEM. Qualify each match with the corresponding trap-qualified\n      write permission. Give the younger EX writer priority, exclude x0,\n      and clock the two selectors when ID/EX captures its operand values.\n      Hold them with a stalled consumer. Keep the load and multiply use\n      interlock so a dependent consumer takes its result from WB.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Expose the outgoing write permission after control-transfer trap\n      checks. For each source, mask the three candidate values with\n      the registered selector and OR them into the operand used for all\n      EX operations, store data and the RVFI source snapshot.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Expose the write permission after the memory alignment check for\n      ID's older-producer match. Preserve the MEM/WB forwarding payload\n      when a data-bus wait clears its retirement valid bit.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
