{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_52", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"tail -n 7 '/srv/hwebench/tmp/ec844b20/54a15e27/btb-prototype.lIazFK/formal-probe.log' && rg -n 'pass|fail' '/srv/hwebench/tmp/ec844b20/54a15e27/btb-prototype.lIazFK/btb-probe.log' && cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r2s2.yaml\"", "aggregated_output": "SBY 21:15:59 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:03:25 (205)\nSBY 21:15:59 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:03:10 (190)\nSBY 21:15:59 [reg_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned pass\nSBY 21:15:59 [reg_ch0] summary: engine_0 did not produce any traces\nSBY 21:15:59 [reg_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\n\nFormal: 53 passed, 0 failed\n2:1 passed in 0.55s\nid: hyp-20261120-003-r2s2\ntitle: \"Predict the next fetch from a 128-entry branch target buffer\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion takes 4,491,485 cycles for ten CoreMark iterations at\n  31.773 MHz. Every taken transfer discards one younger fetch slot. On the\n  earlier core, predicting conditional branches and JAL saved 297,001\n  cycles. Its 5.648 MHz result came from the older combinational-divider\n  design. The champion's worst path lies inside the four-pass divider.\n  A PC-indexed branch target buffer (BTB) can supply learned targets in\n  parallel with instruction fetch and can also cover recurring JALR targets.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add one 128-entry direct-mapped BTB for conditional branches, JAL and\n  JALR. Index it by fetch PC[8:2]. Each entry holds a source tag, an aligned\n  target word address and a two-bit taken counter. Only allocate aligned\n  source and target addresses in the 1 MiB instruction RAM, so an 11-bit\n  tag and 18-bit target suffice. Select the target on a valid tag hit with\n  the counter MSB set. Otherwise fetch PC+4. Reading a learned target\n  removes the instruction-to-target addition from the fetch prediction path.\n\n  Carry the actual fetch decision into EX. On every valid instruction that\n  advances, compare its predicted successor with the architectural successor.\n  This covers loop exits, changing indirect targets and stale entries for\n  code that has changed. Recover on a mismatch even if imemReady is low.\n  Correct predictions admit the successor into ID/EX on the next ready slot.\n\n  An isolated prototype on the champion completed ten iterations in\n  4,180,499 bracketed cycles with \n…[2228 of 5300 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:db67b74951dfde5f]…\nd strongly taken for jumps.\n      On a branch hit saturate the counter toward the outcome. Refresh\n      the target of a taken hit, including JALR. Invalidate a stale hit\n      on a non-control instruction, a misalignment trap or an\n      unrepresentable taken target. Retain the saved target on a\n      not-taken branch hit.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire the resolving source index, entry write data and enable from\n      EX to IF. Drive the existing hazard flush from successor mismatch.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise a warmed backedge, its exit, colliding source PCs and one\n      JALR PC alternating between two targets. Check next-cycle target\n      fetch after warmup, retired PC pairs and absence of wrong-path\n      stores. Present a non-control instruction at a previously trained\n      PC to exercise recovery from a stale hit. Include an EX correction\n      during an imem stall, a branch held behind a stalled load, and\n      misaligned taken transfers.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
