{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n '\\\"category\\\":\\\\s*\\\"predictor\\\"|predictor|branch prediction' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | cut -c 1-2300\"", "aggregated_output": "4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Predict direct branches with a 64-entry agree predictor at fetch\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline fetches PC+4 until EX resolves a transfer. Since IF feeds ID\\ncombinationally, a taken branch or jump discards one younger fetch slot.\\nA reference trace between the CoreMark markers contains 529,588 conditional\\nbranches, of which 294,425 change the successor, plus 49,367 direct jumps.\\nThe baseline's 199.837 ns worst path crosses forwarding and the EX ALU.\\nPredicting these direct transfers in IF can save cycles with a small table.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline fetches PC+4 until EX resolves a transfer. Since IF feeds ID\\ncombinationally, a taken branch or jump discards one younger fetch slot.\\nA reference trace between the CoreMark markers contains 529,588 conditional\\nbranches, of which 294,425 change the successor, plus 49,367 direct jumps.\\nThe baseline's 199.837 ns worst path crosses forwarding and the EX ALU.\\nPredicting these direct transfers in IF can save cycles with a small table.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Index 64 two-bit saturating counters with fetch PC[7:2]. Use the offset\\nsign as the default direction for a legal conditional branch (backward\\ntaken, forward fall-through). Predict taken = offset_sign XOR counter[1].\\nThe counter learns whether to invert the default, so a hot backedge and\\na usually untaken forward guard can share an entry constructively. Predict\\ndirect JAL taken. Compute the signed B/J target in parallel with the table\\nlookup and select it only for a word-aligned address in instruction RAM.\\nUse PC+4 for every other fetch, including JALR. Carry the selected direction\\nbit with the accepted instruction. Direct targets are exact by construction,\\nso EX can detect a miss by comparing this bit with the resolved direction.\\nA miss redirects to the resolved next PC and kills the younger instruction.\\nCorrect predictions let the successor enter ID/EX immediately. An isolated\\nRTL prototype completed ten 2K CoreMar\n…[3831 of 6903 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:56cfcfe37db830a5]…\nthe exact B/J target from the\\nfetched instruction. Select it only when aligned and in instruction RAM.\\nOtherwise select PC+4. JALR uses sequential prediction.\\n\\nLatch the decision actually used and the alternate direct successor in\\nID/EX. A correctly predicted branch admits its successor on the next\\nready fetch. On a miss, the forwarded branch comparison selects recovery\\nwith a one-bit direction check. The alternate address is already\\nregistered. JALR retains its forwarded target calculation in EX. Send\\nthe correction directly to the PC and to the ID/EX bubble control.\\nDecode and load-use detection can consume the raw instruction in\\nparallel because a bubble clears valid and all side-effect controls.\\n\\nAn isolated prototype of this design on the champion completed ten\\niterations in 4,194,434 bracketed cycles with both bus stalls enabled,\\nsaving 297,051 cycles. CoreMark validated all five CRCs. Gowin reported\\n47.124, 47.840 and 47.840 MHz across its three placement options. The\\nmedian gives 114\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
