{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_13", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '73,180p;345,455p' cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py; rg -n 'def validate_coremark_uart|def run_coremark_ipc' tools/eval/fpga.py && cat tools/eval/_slots.py\"", "aggregated_output": "async def _run(dut, program, dmem_init=None, max_cycles=200, dmem_ready=None,\n               *, imem_ready=None, fetch_trace=None):\n    \"\"\"Drive imem/dmem; capture RVFI retirements until EBREAK or max_cycles.\n\n    imem is read combinationally each cycle from imemAddr; dmem similarly.\n    The optional dmem_ready(ren, wen, addr, wdata) callback sets readiness\n    for the upcoming edge. Accepted stores update the Python-side dmem.\n    imem_ready(pc) controls instruction delivery. fetch_trace records each\n    presented address, including speculative requests and wait states.\n\n    Returns (retirements, dmem) where retirements is a list of dicts\n    sampled on every cycle that rvfi_valid=1.\n    \"\"\"\n    imem = program if isinstance(program, dict) else {\n        i * 4: instr & 0xFFFFFFFF for i, instr in enumerate(program)\n    }\n    dmem = dict(dmem_init or {})\n    retirements: list[dict] = []\n\n    def drive_fetch(pc):\n        if fetch_trace is not None:\n            fetch_trace.append(pc)\n        dut.io_imemReady.value = int(imem_ready(pc)) if imem_ready else 1\n        dut.io_imemData.value = imem.get(pc, EBREAK())\n\n    cocotb.start_soon(Clock(dut.clock, 10, \"ns\").start())\n\n    dut.reset.value = 1\n    dut.io_imemData.value = 0\n    dut.io_dmemRData.value = 0\n    # Start both buses in zero-wait mode.\n    dut.io_imemReady.value = 1\n    dut.io_dmemReady.value = 1\n    for _ in range(3):\n        await RisingEdge(dut.clock)\n\n    # Deassert reset and prime the imem read for the first PC=0 fetch.\n    dut.reset.value = 0\n    drive_fetch(0)\n\n    for cycle in range(max_cycles):\n        await RisingEdge(dut.clock)\n        await Timer(1, \"ns\")  # let combinational signals settle\n\n        # Sample the post-edge state.\n        ia        = int(dut.io_imemAddr.value)\n        da        = int(dut.io_dmemAddr.value)\n        ren       = int(dut.io_dmemREn.value)\n        wen       = int(dut.io_dmemWEn.value)\n        wdata     = int(dut.io_dmemWData.value)\n        rvfi_v    = int(dut.io_rvfi_valid_0.value)\n\n        # Apply dmem write side effect\n…[7998 of 11070 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a6450e5f2092e17c]…\n   d = Path(lock_dir)\n    d.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    started, told = time.time(), False\n    while True:\n        for i in range(n):\n            f = open(d / f\"slot{i}.lock\", \"w\")\n            try:\n                fcntl.flock(f, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)\n            except BlockingIOError:\n                f.close()\n                continue\n            waited = time.time() - started\n            if waited > 60:\n                print(f\"  [eval] {what}: got host eval slot {i} after {waited:.0f}s\", flush=True)\n            try:\n                yield\n            finally:\n                fcntl.flock(f, fcntl.LOCK_UN)\n                f.close()\n            return\n        if not told:\n            print(f\"  [eval] {what}: waiting for a host eval slot ({n} busy)\", flush=True)\n            told = True\n        time.sleep(5)\n\n\ndef eval_jobs(default: str | None = None) -> str | None:\n    \"\"\"make -j for a formal run holding a slot (HARNESS_EVAL_JOBS).\"\"\"\n    return os.environ.get(\"HARNESS_EVAL_JOBS\") or default\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d.get('id','').endswith('r3s2') or d.get('id','').endswith('r2s2'):\n  print(d['cycles'], d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'],d.get('critical_path'))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_14", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d.get('id','').endswith('r3s2') or d.get('id','').endswith('r2s2'):\n  print(d['cycles'], d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'],d.get('critical_path'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "4180499 Add one 128-entry direct-mapped BTB for conditional branches, JAL and\nJALR. Index it by fetch PC[8:2]. Each entry holds a source tag, an aligned\ntarget word address and a two-bit taken counter. Only allocate aligned\nsource and target addresses in the 1 MiB instruction RAM, so an 11-bit\ntag and 18-bit target suffice. Select the target on a valid tag hit with\nthe counter MSB set. Otherwise fetch PC+4. Reading a learned target\nremoves the instruction-to-target addition from the fetch prediction path.\n\nCarry the actual fetch decision into EX. On every valid instruction that\nadvances, compare its predicted successor with the architectural successor.\nThis covers loop exits, changing indirect targets and stale entries for\ncode that has changed. Recover on a mismatch even if imemReady is low.\nCorrect predictions admit the successor into ID/EX on the next ready slot.\n\nAn isolated prototype on the champion completed ten iterations in\n4,180,499 bracketed cycles with both bus stalls enabled and all five\nCRCs validated. This saves 310,986 cycles. Counting accepted EX\ntransfers between the markers, the BTB reduced conditional-branch\ncorrections from 294,425 to 42,359, JAL corrections from 49,367 to\n1,849, and JALR corrections from 13,735 to 1,647, relative to\nsequential fetch. Gowin reported 30.613, 31.656 and 31.656 MHz\nacross the three placement options. The median gives 75.72 iterations/s,\n7.04% above 70.74. At this cycle count, 30.94 MHz clears the 4.6%\nacceptance threshold. The worst path\nin placement option zero still ran between divider registers. That\nplacement used 3,251 logic cells and seven BSRAMs versus 2,844 and six\non the champion. The prototype passed all 53 single-retirement\nfast formal checks. Selftest and all three random programs matched\nthe reference retirement trace with both zero-wait and stalled buses.\n {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu/remainder_q_3_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu/quotient_q_0_s1', 'slack_ns': -26.776, 'arrival_ns': 37.071, 'logic_cells': 236, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu']}\n4194434 Predict legal conditional branches and JAL at fetch. Index 64 two-bit\nagree counters with PC[7:2]. The offset sign supplies the default\ndirection (backward taken), and the counter MSB inverts that direction\nwhen disagreement predominates. Compute the exact B/J target from the\nfetched instruction. Select it only when aligned and in instruction RAM.\nOtherwise select PC+4. JALR uses sequential prediction.\n\nLatch the decision actually used and the alternate direct successor in\nID/EX. A correctly predicted branch admits its successor on the next\nready fetch. On a miss, the forwarded branch comparison selects recovery\nwith a one-bit direction check. The alternate address is already\nregistered. JALR retains its forwarded target calculation in EX. Send\nthe correction directly to the PC and to the ID/EX bubble control.\nDecode and load-use detection can consume the raw instruction in\nparallel because a bubble clears valid and all side-effect controls.\n\nAn isolated prototype of this design on the champion completed ten\niterations in 4,194,434 bracketed cycles with both bus stalls enabled,\nsaving 297,051 cycles. CoreMark validated all five CRCs. Gowin reported\n47.124, 47.840 and 47.840 MHz across its three placement options. The\nmedian gives 114.06 iterations/s, 9.35% above the champion and above the\n109.10 iterations/s acceptance threshold. The worst reported path in\noption 0 now runs from the register file through execution to data RAM.\nThat placement used 2,759 logic cells and 12 LUT-RAM primitives, versus\n2,643 and eight on the champion. All 53 fast formal checks passed.\nThe selftest and three random programs matched their reference retirement\ntraces with both zero-wait and stalled buses.\n {'from': 'cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0', 'to': 'dmem_2_dmem_2_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -15.681, 'arrival_ns': 25.976, 'logic_cells': 13, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
