{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_58", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"git status --ignored --short && rg --files --hidden -g '*.pyc' --no-ignore tools test | head -40 && cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r4s2.yaml\"", "aggregated_output": "!! cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/\n!! formal/riscv-formal\n!! test/cosim/__pycache__/\n!! tools/__pycache__/\n!! tools/eval/__pycache__/\ntest/cosim/__pycache__/reference.cpython-313.pyc\ntools/__pycache__/__init__.cpython-313.pyc\ntools/eval/__pycache__/gowin.cpython-313.pyc\ntools/eval/__pycache__/fpga.cpython-313.pyc\ntools/eval/__pycache__/__init__.cpython-313.pyc\ntools/eval/__pycache__/_subprocess.cpython-313.pyc\ntools/eval/__pycache__/formal.cpython-313.pyc\nid: hyp-20261120-003-r4s2\ntitle: Predict the next instruction word from its predecessor PC\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The current agree predictor and direct JAL prediction already save 297,051\n  cycles over ten CoreMark iterations. Even a correctly predicted successor\n  still waits for imemReady. With 22% instruction bus stalls, predicting the\n  word at the successor offers a larger opportunity than predicting more\n  branch directions. At 49.733 MHz the champion needs to save about 185,000\n  of its 4,194,434 cycles to clear the 4.6% acceptance margin.\nhypothesis: |\n  Train a 512-entry last-successor predictor on consecutive admitted\n  instructions. Each predecessor PC indexes one record containing the last\n  successor's aligned RAM address and its instruction word. Look up the\n  record synchronously on admission of the predecessor, in parallel with\n  the normal next-PC calculation. On the following cycle the registered\n  fetch PC validates the forecast. A matching record can supply the exact\n  instruction during an imem wait, including the first word reached through\n  a taken branch. A miss costs the usual demand fetch. A change of path or\n  table collision is harmless because reuse requires a full successor PC\n  match. The existing branch predictor steers fetch and EX resolves it.\n\n  Use a ready bus word preferentially and train only from accepted bus\n  words. Hold a forecast through load-use and downstream stalls. On an EX\n  correction discard the younger slot and check the saved forecast against\n  the corrected PC on retry. On reset or admission \n…[1336 of 4408 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ce007cb86ef19ef5]…\nvance predecessor history only on a fetch admitted to ID/EX. Use\n      the selected instruction consistently for branch predecode, offsets,\n      predictor metadata and out.instr. Set out.valid on bus ready or a\n      validated forecast. Preserve EX recovery priority over fetch holds.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Feed if_id_w.valid to the hazard unit's fetch-availability input.\n      IF still receives raw io_imemReady to distinguish a bus word from a\n      forecast. Keep availability independent of stall_if and redirect.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Warm a repeated path, withhold imemReady on a learned edge, poison\n      the unready bus word and check that the successor retires on time.\n      Change a branch outcome and collide predecessor indices with different\n      successors. Check precise recovery, channel-0 order and store strobes\n      across data stalls. After FENCE.I replace a learned word and verify\n      that the new instruction is fetched and retired.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
