{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_70", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"git diff --name-only && git ls-files --others --exclude-standard && cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r4s2.yaml | sed -n '1,105p'\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261120-003-r4s2\ntitle: Predict the successor PC and instruction with one lookup\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The current agree predictor and direct JAL prediction already save 297,051\n  cycles over ten CoreMark iterations. Fetch still waits for imemReady on\n  correctly predicted paths, and JALR still incurs an EX redirect. A repeated\n  instruction pair reveals both the successor address and the word needed\n  there. Learning that pair can hide instruction bus stalls and supply the\n  target of a recurring indirect jump from the same table entry.\nhypothesis: |\n  On admission of each instruction, use its PC to launch a synchronous\n  lookup of its last observed successor. Each of 512 entries packs a valid\n  bit, an 18-bit successor word address and the 32-bit instruction fetched\n  there. Train the preceding instruction's row when its successor enters\n  ID/EX with a ready bus word. This learns taken, fall-through and indirect\n  edges using one read port and one write port.\n\n  For sequential and direct transfers, check the predicted address against\n  the full registered fetch PC. For a legal JALR with a valid response,\n  present the predicted target directly at fetch while EX independently\n  resolves the forwarded operand. Use the bus word when ready. During an\n  imem wait, a matching record supplies the instruction. A correct JALR\n  prediction admits the target on the same edge that advances the jump.\n  On a miss, EX squashes the younger instruction and installs the resolved\n  PC even during an imem wait. Source-index aliases may predict the wrong\n  edge, so EX checks the full JALR target before admitting any side effect.\n\n  An isolated RTL prototype supplied 604,950 instructions during imem waits\n  and correctly steered 12,072 of 13,735 JALRs between the CoreMark markers.\n  Ten iterations with both stall generators took 3,408,562 bracketed cycles,\n  saving 785,872 cycles with all five CRCs correct. Gowin reported 44.951,\n  47.568 and 47.568 MHz. The median gives 139.55 iterations/s, 17.7% above\n  118.\n…[1535 of 4607 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:fed615bc2125b471]…\nhe link write and recovers to PC+4 if fetch used a forecast. Keep\n      the one-bit direction check and registered alternate PC for direct\n      transfers. Drive RVFI pc_next from the architectural resolution.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire IF's JALR forecast-valid bit and selected fetch address to EX.\n      Feed if_id_w.valid to the hazard unit's fetch-availability input.\n      IF still receives raw io_imemReady to distinguish bus data from a\n      prediction. Keep availability independent of stall_if and redirect.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Warm beyond the reset sweep, withhold imemReady on a learned edge\n      and poison unready bus data. Check adjacent retirement on a JALR hit.\n      Change its target, then supply a misaligned target. Cover colliding\n      predecessor indices, a JALR held behind a stalled load, channel-0\n      order and wrong-path store strobes. Replace a learned word across\n      FENCE.I and verify that fetch waits for the updated word.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
