{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_107", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r5s2.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261120-003-r5s2\ntitle: Predict a load's word index from the instruction producing its base\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion supplies 604,950 instructions during fetch waits, yet ten\n  CoreMark iterations still take 3,408,562 cycles. A load followed by a\n  consumer incurs an interlock, and a data-bus wait holds both instructions.\n  A tagged word buffer could deliver the load at the EX/MEM edge if ID\n  can predict its read index one cycle earlier. Pointer chasing makes\n  the base register particularly important: the preceding instruction\n  often holds a better estimate of that base than the register file.\nhypothesis: |\n  Predict the low 12 address bits of a load while it is in ID. Start with\n  the register-file base, including its WB bypass. For a matching nonzero\n  destination in EX/MEM, use the low bits of its registered ALU result or\n  its verified early load result when available. The younger producer in\n  ID/EX gets priority when it can supply an estimate. For a word load,\n  use the low 12 bits of its candidate data word even while its tag check\n  is pending. For a non-load with ALU_ADD, estimate the sum using the low\n  12 bits of its latched operands and its register/immediate selection.\n  This deliberately allows stale operands in the predictor. Add the new\n  load's I-immediate modulo 4096 and use bits [11:2] to launch one\n  synchronous read of a 1024-word tagged buffer.\n\n  EX independently adds the fully forwarded rs1 and the immediate. An\n  early hit requires a valid row, equality of the complete architectural\n  word address with the saved tag and probed index, natural alignment,\n  and an address in the 1 MiB RAM. Register the extracted byte, halfword\n  or word on a dedicated forwarding path. On a hit, admit the dependent\n  instruction immediately and complete the load without a demand read.\n  A wrong guess uses the existing load-use interlock and demand read.\n  Only the resolved address can issue an external memory transaction.\n\n  The buffer learns whole words from accepted RAM reads and full-word\n  stores. An accepted byte or halfword store invalidates its indexed row.\n  Suppress a response on a same-row read/write collision and veto an EX\n  hit when an older MEM store has the probed index, including a held store.\n  Hold the probe response with ID/EX. Reset starts a 1024-clock validity\n  sweep while ordinary demand access continues. Carry the actual address,\n  mask and raw word through retirement for both early and demand loads.\n\n  An isolated prototype verified 524,067 early results among 544,852\n  bracketed loads. It admitted 202,859 following instructions that\n  matched the existing load-use interlock. Ten iterations took 3,058,700\n  cycles with both bus stalls enabled and all five CRCs correct. Using\n  the decode register-file base alone with the same word buffer took\n  3,251,054 cycles. Predicting the in-flight producer saved 192,354 cycles.\n  Gowin's placement options reached 48.047, 49.966 and 49.966 MHz.\n  The median gives 163.36 iterations/s, 15.83% above the 141.03 champion.\n  At this cycle count 45.122 MHz clears the 4.6% acceptance margin.\n  Placement option zero used 3,308 logic cells, 707 registers and ten BSRAMs.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 16\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Implement the 12-bit producer estimate and a synchronous 1024 x 41\n      word buffer with one read and one write per cycle. Each row holds\n      valid, address[19:12] and the raw word. On the ID/EX enable latch\n      the row, actual probe index and collision qualification. Use accepted\n      bus strobes for buffer updates. Route the hit to the load-use and\n      data-bus stall predicates, and the registered early result to EX.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Add the architectural effective address, verified-hit bit, raw\n      predicted word and extended load result to EX/MEM. Keep the decoded\n      load control and width for RVFI and writeback.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Form and register the dedicated full-width effective address from\n      forwarded operands. Compare it with the probe in parallel with lane\n      selection and sign extension. Capture the hit flag, candidate word\n      and extended result on the EX/MEM transfer. Add the registered load\n      result as a fourth source to each operand forwarding mux.\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      On a winning EX/MEM match select the dedicated load result for a\n      load and the normal ALU result for other writers. Retain EX/MEM\n      priority over WB and x0 exclusion. The interlock keeps a consumer\n      of a missed load out of EX until WB can supply the demand value.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Use the registered effective address for the bus, alignment checks\n      and RVFI masks. An early hit suppresses io_dmemREn, writes back the\n      registered extended value and reports its raw word to RVFI. A miss\n      samples and extends the accepted data-bus word as before.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      After warmup, run two dependent pointer loads feeding an ADD and\n      check three consecutive retirements with the data bus unavailable.\n      Give an ADD producer stale latched operands, delay the true demand\n      read and check that the younger load and consumer use the corrected\n      word. Cover high-address tag aliases, a held partial store to a hot\n      word, subword extension, misalignment, read strobes and RVFI order.\nYAML\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r5s2.yaml')\nh=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\njsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert p.stem==h['id'] and h['category']=='predictor'\nassert all((Path('cores/bench')/c['file']).is_file() for c in h['changes'])\nprint('Valid predictor hypothesis')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
