{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nds=list(map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')))\nprint(ds[-2])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "{'id': 'hyp-20261120-002-r5s1', 'title': 'Pipeline RV32M products through MEM and reuse the multiplier for division', 'category': 'structural', 'motivation': \"The champion takes 3,408,562 cycles for ten iterations at 48.072 MHz.\\nGowin reports a 27.067 ns path from the register file through the EX ALU\\nto data RAM. The five-step divider feedback path reached 25.389 ns on\\nthe related load-forwarding design. Round 4's shared multiplier removed\\nboth arithmetic paths and reached 61.695 MHz, but holding EX for every\\nmultiply added 188,431 cycles to its parent. An earlier MEM-stage multiply\\ntrial on that parent added only 35,569 cycles. The timed loop has no divides.\\n\", 'hypothesis': {'motivation': \"The champion takes 3,408,562 cycles for ten iterations at 48.072 MHz.\\nGowin reports a 27.067 ns path from the register file through the EX ALU\\nto data RAM. The five-step divider feedback path reached 25.389 ns on\\nthe related load-forwarding design. Round 4's shared multiplier removed\\nboth arithmetic paths and reached 61.695 MHz, but holding EX for every\\nmultiply added 188,431 cycles to its parent. An earlier MEM-stage multiply\\ntrial on that parent added only 35,569 cycles. The timed loop has no divides.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Give MEM a single registered-input, signed 36-by-36 multiplier. EX captures\\nforwarded operands on every admitted multiply. MEM computes the product in\\nparallel with the next instruction's EX, and MEM/WB captures its selected\\nword. Independent multiplies can issue every clock. An immediate consumer\\nof the product takes one load-style interlock and forwards from WB.\\n\\nDuring a divide, reuse this same multiplier for two fixed-point Newton\\nreciprocal refinements and a final exact remainder check. Keep the existing\\nseven busy clocks and blocking divide protocol. An older MUL in MEM can\\nfinish on the divide issue edge, before the divider needs the multiplier.\\nThis removes both the combinational EX products and the five serial\\nsubtract/select steps per divide clock.\\n\\nA scratch prototype on \n…[7707 of 10779 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e588a5f6b01af196]…\nvement', 'fitness': 174.38, 'delta_pct': 23.65, 'vs_baseline': 1338.78, 'fmax_mhz': 60.061, 'ipc_coremark': 3e-06, 'cycles': 3444219, 'iterations': 10, 'lut4': 3268, 'ff': 776, 'seeds': [58.79, 60.061, 60.061], 'perturbations': [[0, 58.79, 15], [1, 60.061, 16], [2, 60.061, 16]], 'lutram': 12, 'bsram': 9, 'dsp': 2, 'critical_path': {'from': 'cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0', 'to': 'dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -12.01, 'arrival_ns': 22.304, 'logic_cells': 15, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}, 'logic_levels': 15, 'formal_passed': True, 'cosim_passed': True, 'error': None, 'implementation_notes': '', 'timestamp': '2026-11-20T21:27:30.503430Z', 'slot': 1, 'round_id': 5, 'lesson': '- 2026-11-20 hyp-20261120-002-r5s1 (improvement, +23.65%): Computing products in MEM on the multiplier shared with Newton division kept the DSP off the EX path and let independent multiplies issue every clock, while an immediate product consumer needed just one stall before WB forwarding.'}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
