{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_80", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r6s2.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261120-003-r6s2\ntitle: Predict load-use latency and forward the last formatted result\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion interlocks every consumer directly behind a load. In a\n  ten-iteration trace, four pointer-chasing load sites accounted for 85,446\n  missed dependent loads in a trial that predicted addresses from PC stride.\n  The resolved load address is available in EX. Predicting when the result\n  will be ready lets the next instruction issue before that address has\n  finished indexing a synchronous RAM.\nhypothesis: |\n  Let each static load predict whether its dependent consumer can issue\n  immediately. Use 64 one-bit entries indexed by load PC[7:2], initialized\n  to fast and trained on the last completed load's forwarding hit. A slow\n  prediction takes the existing load-use bubble. On a fast prediction admit\n  the consumer, even though the result lookup and its tag check are pending.\n\n  Index a 1024-row result buffer in EX using the actual forwarded effective\n  address. Store the full raw word, the last extracted 32-bit result, its byte\n  offset, width and signedness, a RAM address tag and validity. The candidate\n  result reaches the consumer's EX forwarding mux directly from the RAM\n  output. In parallel, MEM checks the tag, access format, alignment and valid\n  bit. On a match, the consumer advances and the load completes internally.\n  On a miss, complete the ordinary demand load. If the admitted consumer\n  depends on it, hold that consumer through bus waits and then for the edge\n  that deposits the load in WB. Retry it with WB forwarding. An independent\n  follower can advance on the demand response edge.\n\n  A scratch implementation completed ten CRC-validated iterations in\n  3,165,115 bracketed cycles with both stall generators, versus 3,444,219\n  on the champion. Gowin reached 61.561, 61.670 and 61.670 MHz. The median\n  gives 194.84 iterations/s, 11.73% above 174.38. At that cycle count the\n  acceptance threshold requires more than 57.732 MHz. A comparison build\n  that extracted every cached operand on the consumer path took 3,093,669\n  cycles but reached only 57.604 MHz and 186.20 iterations/s.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 12\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Add the latency table and one synchronous-read, one-write 1024 x 78\n      result RAM. Probe address[11:2] on the EX/MEM enable. Hold its response\n      through an older bus wait. A hit requires the full aligned RAM word\n      address (high bits zero and address[19:12] equal), natural alignment,\n      a valid row and matching access format. Ignore signedness for LW.\n      Suppress ambiguous same-row read/write responses. Sweep all valid bits\n      after reset while ordinary demand access continues. Train on completed\n      accesses only. Accepted aligned RAM reads install raw and extracted\n      values. Full-word stores install their word as an LW result. Accepted\n      byte and halfword stores invalidate the row. Exclude MMIO from reuse.\n      Drive the existing hazard inputs with predicted load latency, actual\n      MEM demand and dependent retry. Keep the multiply interlock.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Carry a separate full resolved effective address in EX/MEM alongside the memory controls.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Drive the probe and the registered memory address with forwarded rs1\n      plus the decoded immediate. Add the speculative result as a fourth\n      operand source. On a dependent miss, suppress redirect, branch training\n      and M-unit issue. Bubble the EX/MEM valid and controls on the demand\n      completion edge and hold ID/EX. Preserve normal dmem-hold priority.\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Select the candidate operand on an EX/MEM load match. Preserve\n      youngest-writer priority and x0 exclusion. Use MEM's post-trap\n      register-write qualification.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Keep the consumer payload on a retry. On the edge when the demand load\n      displaces WB, capture the outgoing WB value into either held operand\n      whose source it writes. Include retained WB forwarding after a bus\n      stall. On the retry the younger load gets ordinary WB priority.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Use the dedicated address for masks, alignment and dmem. A verified\n      result completes independently of dmemReady and suppresses io_dmemREn.\n      Return the extended result to WB and the raw word with its actual byte\n      mask to RVFI. Supply the demand word and its formatted result for fill.\n      Preserve trap and link semantics on all retry paths.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Warm a load site and issue its dependent on a hit with dmemReady low.\n      Force a tag or width mismatch at the same PC. Hold the bus while the\n      consumer also needs an outgoing WB value. Check its retry operands\n      and exactly one retirement. Cover a stale candidate steering a branch\n      or feeding a store, byte and halfword sign extension, partial-store\n      invalidation, same-row RAM collisions, MMIO exclusion and RVFI order.\nYAML\npython - <<'PY'\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r6s2.yaml')\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
