{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_110", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r7s2.yaml && ls -la | head -24'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261120-003-r7s2\ntitle: \"Predict dependent load indices from a pending pointer and verify them in EX\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion takes 3,444,219 cycles for ten CoreMark iterations. A PC-stride\n  predictor has trouble when one load returns the address of the next list\n  node. In a scratch run, predicting the index from each load PC's last\n  stride released 50,764 immediate dependencies. The pointer bits needed\n  for the next lookup are often already present at the previous lookup's\n  registered data output, one clock before the register file sees them.\nhypothesis: |\n  In decode, predict word-index bits [11:2] by XORing a base estimate\n  with the corresponding I-immediate bits. Start with the register-file\n  value including WB bypass. A matching MEM writer supplies its registered\n  result. A matching younger load in EX takes priority and supplies its\n  candidate raw word. Use even an unchecked candidate for this internal\n  RAM index. This lets one load start the lookup for its dependent\n  successor before either the pointer or the addition has been verified.\n\n  In EX, use fully forwarded rs1 plus the sign-extended immediate to check\n  the entire architectural word address against the early row's tag and\n  saved index. A valid aligned hit releases the dependent and places the\n  formatted load value in the existing EX/MEM forwarding register. On a\n  bad guess the existing interlock bubbles the consumer. At the same edge\n  probe the buffer with the resolved address. A resident word then completes\n  in MEM even if the first guess was wrong and dmemReady is low. A cold\n  word uses the ordinary demand read. The resolved probe provides the raw\n  retirement word in both hit cases. Index predictions never issue bus reads.\n\n  With just the resolved probe and a mandatory dependency bubble, ten\n  iterations took 3,295,975 cycles. Enabling pointer-based prediction\n  reduced that to 3,097,817, saving 198,158 cycles against that control\n  and 346,402 against the champion. Both bus stalls were enable\n…[3127 of 6199 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:886754d1fc2cb326]…\n7 .agents\ndr-xr-xr-x   2 hwebench hwebench    40 Nov 20 23:37 .aws\ndr-xr-xr-x   2 hwebench hwebench    40 Nov 20 23:37 .codex\n-rw-rw----+  1 nobody   nogroup    101 Nov 20 23:22 .git\n-rw-rw----+  1 nobody   nogroup   5999 Nov 20 23:22 .gitignore\n-rw-rw----+  1 nobody   nogroup   2041 Nov 20 23:22 ARCHITECTURE.md\n-rw-rw----+  1 nobody   nogroup  10455 Nov 20 23:22 CLAUDE.md\n-rw-rw----+  1 nobody   nogroup  11358 Nov 20 23:22 LICENSE\n-rw-rw----+  1 nobody   nogroup   7699 Nov 20 23:22 Makefile\n-rw-rw----+  1 nobody   nogroup    118 Nov 20 23:22 NOTICE\ndrwxrwx---+  3 nobody   nogroup   4096 Nov 20 23:22 bench\ndrwxrwx---+  3 nobody   nogroup   4096 Nov 20 23:22 cores\ndrwxrwx---+  2 nobody   nogroup   4096 Nov 20 23:22 formal\ndrwxrwx---+  4 nobody   nogroup   4096 Nov 20 23:22 fpga\ndrwxrwx---+  2 nobody   nogroup   4096 Nov 20 23:22 schemas\n-rwxrwx---+  1 nobody   nogroup   5941 Nov 20 23:22 setup.sh\ndrwxrwx---+  3 nobody   nogroup   4096 Nov 20 23:22 test\ndrwxrwx---+  4 nobody   nogroup   4096 Nov 20 23:22 tools\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
