{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor d in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if d.get('slot')==2 and d.get('round_id') in [4,5,6,7,9]:\n  print(d['id'],d.get('implementation_notes','')[:3100],str(d['motivation'])[:1100])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20261120-003-r4s2  The current agree predictor and direct JAL prediction already save 297,051\ncycles over ten CoreMark iterations. Fetch still waits for imemReady on\ncorrectly predicted paths, and JALR still incurs an EX redirect. A repeated\ninstruction pair reveals both the successor address and the word needed\nthere. Learning that pair can hide instruction bus stalls and supply the\ntarget of a recurring indirect jump from the same table entry.\n\nhyp-20261120-003-r5s2  The champion supplies 604,950 instructions during fetch waits, yet ten\nCoreMark iterations still take 3,408,562 cycles. A load followed by a\nconsumer incurs an interlock, and a data-bus wait holds both instructions.\nA tagged word buffer could deliver the load at the EX/MEM edge if ID\ncan predict its read index one cycle earlier. In a pointer chase the\npreceding load can supply the next base before it reaches the register\nfile.\n\nhyp-20261120-003-r6s2  The champion interlocks every consumer directly behind a load. A PC-stride\ntrial missed 85,446 dependent accesses at just four pointer-chasing load\nsites. The actual load address is available in EX, one clock before the\nconsumer needs the value. Predicting result availability lets that clock\nserve as the read latency of a synchronous RAM.\n\nhyp-20261120-003-r7s2  The champion takes 3,444,219 cycles for ten CoreMark iterations. A PC-stride\npredictor has trouble when one load returns the address of the next list\nnode. In a scratch run, predicting the index from each load PC's last\nstride released 50,764 immediate dependencies. The pointer bits needed\nfor the next lookup are often already present at the previous lookup's\nregistered data output, one clock before the register file sees them.\n\nhyp-20261121-003-r9s2  A profile of the champion between the CoreMark markers counted 93,960\nmultiplies. Both forwarded operands fit unsigned 16 bits on 88,920.\nAt the same multiply PC, the previous operand pair never repeated in a\n256-entry last-pair table. Predicting the upcoming operands from in-flight\nproducers worked much better. Decode's two low-half guesses exactly\nreproduced both full forwarded operands on 65,940 multiplies, including\n11,921 with a valid immediate consumer flagged by the interlock.\nGowin's worst path on champion placement option 1 ran from the Newton seed\nROM through the shared multiplier to the MEM/WB write-data register.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
