{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_31", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor d in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if d.get('round_id') in [4,7] and d.get('slot')==2:\n  print(d['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "{'motivation': 'The current agree predictor and direct JAL prediction already save 297,051\\ncycles over ten CoreMark iterations. Fetch still waits for imemReady on\\ncorrectly predicted paths, and JALR still incurs an EX redirect. A repeated\\ninstruction pair reveals both the successor address and the word needed\\nthere. Learning that pair can hide instruction bus stalls and supply the\\ntarget of a recurring indirect jump from the same table entry.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"On admission of each instruction, use its PC to launch a synchronous\\nlookup of its last observed successor. Each of 512 entries packs a valid\\nbit, an 18-bit successor word address and the 32-bit instruction fetched\\nthere. Train the preceding instruction's row when its successor enters\\nID/EX with a ready bus word. This learns taken, fall-through and indirect\\nedges using one read port and one write port.\\n\\nUse the champion's registered next PC for sequential and direct transfers.\\nFor a legal JALR with a valid response, steer fetch to the predicted\\ntarget while EX resolves the forwarded operand. A cold JALR starts at\\nfall-through. Use the bus word whenever ready. During an imem wait,\\nsupply the saved word only if its address matches the full selected PC.\\nOn an address mismatch, wait for a ready bus word. A correct JALR prediction\\nadmits the target on the same edge that advances the jump. If control\\nflow was mispredicted, EX squashes the younger instruction and installs\\nthe resolved PC even during an imem wait.\\n\\nAn isolated RTL prototype supplied 604,950 instructions during imem waits\\nand correctly steered 12,072 of 13,735 JALRs between the CoreMark markers.\\nTen iterations with both stall generators took 3,408,562 bracketed cycles,\\nsaving 785,872 cycles with all five CRCs correct. Gowin reported 44.951,\\n47.568 and 47.568 MHz. The median gives 139.55 iterations/s, 17.7% above\\n118.57. At this cycle count 42.275 MHz clears the 4.6% acceptance margin.\\nPlacement option zero used 3,039 logic cells, 623 registers and eight BSRAMs.\\nThe prot\n…[7062 of 10134 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:defc5c2218138dce]…\n': \"Add the full resolved memory address to ex_mem_t so the early\\nforwarding result and the load's real address can travel together.\\n\"}, {'file': 'rtl/mem_stage.sv', 'description': 'Use the separate resolved address for bus addressing, alignment,\\nlane masks and RVFI. Use the selected raw word for load extraction,\\nWB and RVFI. Export the aligned read request for demand qualification.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Warm three words after the reset sweep. Run LW through a cached pointer\\ninto another LW and an ADDI with the external data bus unavailable.\\nCheck consecutive retirements and exact values. Force an index miss\\nusing base 68 and displacement 4, for which XOR predicts 64 and the\\ntrue address is 72. Test both resident and cold destinations, an\\naccepted partial store to a hot word and a same-row port collision.\\nAlternate between aligned byte addresses 4 KiB apart. Check signed\\nand unsigned subword results, request counts, RVFI raw data, masks\\nand retirement order.\\n'}]}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
