{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_73","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-001-r10s0.yaml')\np.write_text(\\\"\\\"\\\"id: hyp-20261121-001-r10s0\ntitle: Select the branch comparison after forwarding\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  On champion placement option 1, Gowin reports a 17.807 ns path from\n  the rs2 forwarding select through EX to the successor RAM, just behind\n  the 17.904 ns fetch path. A scratch version that compared four byte\n  groups after forwarding passed CoreMark in 3,062,533 bracketed cycles\n  and reached 79.302 MHz median, effectively tying the 79.301 MHz champion.\n  The two operand muxes still preceded every branch comparison.\nhypothesis: |\n  Use decode's knowledge of the two physical operand sources to select\n  a one-bit branch outcome in EX. Compare the possible pairs of registered\n  source words in parallel. This moves both wide forwarding muxes off the\n  branch-to-fetch path and preserves single-cycle branch resolution.\n  The existing predictor supplies the predicted direction and alternate PC.\n  At the measured cycle count the median must exceed 82.95 MHz to clear\n  the acceptance margin. The main risks are routing the parallel comparisons\n  and the independent saved-instruction path to the PC. The parallel-pair\n  design has yet to be timed.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      At ID/EX capture the pair classification from the two existing\n      youngest-writer forwarding decisions and mul_in_ex. Each source\n      selects its captured RF operand, the ordinary EX/MEM result, the\n      registered product, or the WB word. Encode the physical pair and\n      its orientation before the clock edge. Hold it on stall_id alongside\n      the existing selects and propagate it to EX. Honor x0 and the\n      existing load and divide availability interlocks.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Compare nine distinct physical pairs directly. These are RF1/RF2,\n      RF1 against each of ordinary, product and WB, each of those three\n      against RF2, and ordinary/WB and product/WB. Ordinary and product\n      are alternatives for a single EX writer, so they cannot supply\n      different sources on the same issue. Two operands selecting the same\n      bypass word compare equal. Compute equality and ordering from the\n      registered words using parallel byte comparisons with high-byte\n      priority. For signed branches account for opposite sign bits.\n      For reversed pairs derive the opposite strict order from equality\n      and less-than. Mask and combine the one-bit results with the saved\n      pair selects. Apply funct3 polarity for BEQ/BNE, BLT/BGE and\n      BLTU/BGEU. Use the selected condition for redirect, training and\n      architectural next PC, retaining the alignment check and misaligned\n      taken-branch trap. Continue to use forwarded words for RVFI.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise both orientations of WB versus ordinary and product bypass,\n      two operands from the same writer, x0, and RF versus each bypass.\n      Include signed and unsigned operands that first differ in each byte,\n      equality at the signed boundary, a branch held by dmem backpressure\n      and a misaligned target. Check branch direction, fetch recovery,\n      retirement order and suppression of a wrong-path store.\n\\\"\\\"\\\")\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('validated',d['title'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
