{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_32", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n e=json.loads(l)\n if any(n in e['id'] for n in ('r7s1','r8s1','r8s2','r2s0')): print(e['id'], e['hypothesis']['hypothesis'][:2400])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-001-r2s0 Retune the existing blocking divider to compute five binary quotient bits per\nclock over seven passes. Feed it three leading zero dividend bits so every pass\nuses the same five-step datapath. This removes three serial carry chains and\ntheir remainder muxes from the feedback path. Seven compute clocks also leave\nroom for issue, transfer and a redirect or load-use bubble within the formal\nwrapper's ten-cycle forward-progress window.\n\nExpect close to the champion's 4,491,485 bracketed cycles for ten iterations.\nSetup divides can shift the stall generator's phase. At that cycle count a\nmedian Fmax above 33.24 MHz clears the acceptance margin. A 25% gain would\nrequire about 39.72 MHz. The single-cycle multiply path may cap the gain.\n\nhyp-20261120-002-r7s1 Dedicate one registered-input multiplier to ordinary MUL and give the\nblocking divider four separately inferred products with fixed operand\nsources. Two products implement the two Newton refinements, one estimates\nthe quotient, and one computes Q*B for exact correction. While the\nreciprocal refines, pre-shift dividend magnitude A by the divisor\nexponent k and precompute A-B. The quotient product then feeds a fixed\nbit slice. The residual product feeds two parallel subtractors whose\nregistered outputs supply both possible remainders and the correction\nbit. This keeps the seven divide clocks and one multiply issue per clock.\n\nFor nonzero divisor magnitude B let D = B / 2^k, k = floor(log2(B)),\nS = 2^34 and r = R/S after refinement. The existing 512-bin seed has\nrelative error below 1/512 + 2/4096. Each rounded Newton step keeps a\nlower bound and maps relative error e to at most e^2 + 3/S. After two\nsteps, 0 <= 1/D - r < 2^-32. Discarding the low k bits of A costs at\nmost 1 - 2^-k quotient units, and the reciprocal error on A >> k costs\nless than 2^-k. The trial Q is exact or one low. The sign of (A-B)-Q*B\ntherefore selects the exact quotient and remainder using one correction.\nAn integer sweep of all 2^31 normalized divisor words confirmed that\nth\n…[4058 of 7130 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ebc1ad73d657afab]…\nercept, distance,\nslope and product adds at most 3*2^-20+2^-25. Thus the refined reciprocal\nunderestimates by less than 2*(2^-18+3*2^-20+2^-25)^2+2/S < 2^-32.\nDiscarding the bottom k dividend bits costs at most 1-2^-k quotient\nunits and reciprocal error on A>>k costs less than 2^-k. The prediction\nis exact or one low, so one check suffices for every nonzero divisor.\n\nAn exhaustive integer sweep of all 2^31 normalized divisors found a maximum\nreciprocal underestimate of 0.655 times 2^-32. A scratch prototype with\none shared wide multiplier ran ten CRC-validated iterations in 3,097,817\nbracketed cycles with both buses stalling. Gowin reported 68.758, 73.980\nand 73.980 MHz. That median gives 238.81 iterations/s, a 3.3% gain.\nAcceptance at this cycle count requires more than 74.887 MHz. The\nlimiting path in the shared prototype moved to fetch. Placement zero used\n3,724 logic cells, 13 BSRAMs and two DSPs. Selftest and all three random\nprograms matched the reference retirement trace with ready and stalled buses.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
