{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nrows=[json.loads(l) for l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')]\nfor r in rows[-6:]:\n print(r['id'],r['critical_path'],r['cycles'])\n print(r.get('hypothesis',{}).get('hypothesis','')[-2000:])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-001-r11s0 {'from': 'cpu/fwd_rs1_sel_q_2_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_id/reg_q.ctrl.mem_width_0_s0', 'slack_ns': -7.409, 'arrival_ns': 17.703, 'logic_cells': 40, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu/u_id']} 3078099\nLook up the existing 512-row successor table one fetch earlier. While fetching A and predicting B, send B's nine index bits to the synchronous read port. During B the RAM presents the row for B, which contains its previously observed successor C and C's instruction. At the edge admitting B, capture this row in fabric flip-flops. During C, the saved instruction, its address and its direct-target metadata are available from those flip-flops. A warm stream still admits one instruction per clock.\n\nThe lookup is speculative and internal. Use the low bits of the sequential or predicted direct address without waiting for full target-range validation, EX redirect or the load-use interlock. Check the registered probe index against the actual B index before promoting its response. Keep the full word-address check before issuing C from that response. A bad probe costs a cache opportunity. A ready bus can still supply the instruction immediately. Stage accepted training pairs for one clock so the physical write port takes registered inputs.\n\nThe first lookahead prototype completed ten iterations in 3,107,313 bracketed cycles with all five CRCs. At that count the median must exceed 84.162 MHz for promotion. A saved instruction initially timed out across load-use and divide holds. Adding a demand re-read of the last admitted predecessor fixed that case and passed all 38 pipeline tests. The revised design still needs a bracketed CoreMark run and median timing. The main risks are missed probes after redirects and a new timing path into the nine-bit RAM address.\n\nhyp-20261121-002-r11s1 {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/wrong_direction_q_s0', 'to': 'cpu/early_index_q_0_s0', 'slack_ns': -7.571, 'arrival_ns': 17.865, 'logic_cells': 13, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu/u_rf', 'cpu']} 3081605\nMove direct-contr\n…[9694 of 12766 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c759e507c1a1f1ce]…\newton error D*(P/2^18-1/D)^2 and downward rounding. Converting to U\nadds less than 2^-32 in reciprocal units. At k=1, d=0, X is exact and\nthe total quotient loss is below 0.813. At 2<=k<=10, d=0 and the loss\nis below 0.657 including truncation of X. At k=11 and k=12, d is at\nmost 1/2048 and 3/4096 respectively. At k>=13, d<1/1024. In these\nthree ranges bin error costs at most 9/16 of a quotient unit, rounding\nbelow 0.001 and truncating X below 1/4. Thus Q is exact or one low and\nthe residual fits in 32 bits because Q*B<=A.\n\nA scratch prototype using the same seed and a 34-by-34-bit quotient\nmultiplier passed the ALU and pipeline suites and all five CoreMark CRCs.\nTen iterations took 3,081,605 bracketed cycles. Gowin returned 85.759,\n85.893 and 85.893 MHz, giving 278.73 iterations/s. Promotion at that\ncycle count requires more than 87.492 MHz. The proposed 32-bit operands\nnarrow the multiplier input muxes and registers. The remaining 1.6 MHz\nis a timing hypothesis, with fetch routing the main competing path.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
