{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor s in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n x=json.loads(s)\n if x['id'].endswith(('r12s0','r13s0','r11s0','r14s2','r14s1','r7s2')):\n  print(x['id'],x['hypothesis'],x.get('implementation_notes','')[:1200])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-003-r7s2 {'motivation': \"The champion takes 3,444,219 cycles for ten CoreMark iterations. A PC-stride\\npredictor has trouble when one load returns the address of the next list\\nnode. In a scratch run, predicting the index from each load PC's last\\nstride released 50,764 immediate dependencies. The pointer bits needed\\nfor the next lookup are often already present at the previous lookup's\\nregistered data output, one clock before the register file sees them.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"In decode, predict word-index bits [11:2] by XORing a base estimate\\nwith the corresponding I-immediate bits. Start with the register-file\\nvalue including WB bypass. A matching MEM writer supplies its registered\\nresult. A matching younger load in EX takes priority and supplies its\\ncandidate raw word. Use even an unchecked candidate for this internal\\nRAM index. XOR keeps carry propagation off the decode-to-RAM path.\\nThis lets one load start the lookup for its dependent successor\\nbefore the pointer is verified.\\n\\nIn EX, use fully forwarded rs1 plus the sign-extended immediate to check\\nthe entire architectural word address against the early row's tag and\\nsaved index. A valid aligned hit releases the dependent and places the\\nformatted load value in the existing EX/MEM forwarding register. On a\\nbad guess the existing interlock bubbles the consumer. At the same edge\\nprobe the buffer with the resolved address. A resident word then completes\\nin MEM even if the first guess was wrong and dmemReady is low. A cold\\nword uses the ordinary demand read. The resolved probe provides the raw\\nretirement word in both hit cases. Only the actual address reaches dmem.\\n\\nWith just the resolved probe and a mandatory dependency bubble, ten\\niterations took 3,295,975 cycles. Enabling pointer-based prediction\\nreduced that to 3,097,817, saving 198,158 cycles against that control\\nand 346,402 against the champion. Between the MMIO markers, 198,068 of\\n203,870 immediate load dependencies observed by the interlock had an\\nearly hit. The resolve\n…[22636 of 25708 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:38ca17e237e66c8c]…\non.\\n'}, {'file': 'rtl/mem_stage.sv', 'description': 'Form the full 32-bit direct target from the registered PC and\\ndisplacement. Supply it for training on both branch outcomes and\\ncapture it for recovery and RVFI pc_wdata on a delayed taken transfer.\\n'}, {'file': 'rtl/core.sv', 'description': 'Wire the MEM target into predictor training. Give a pending delayed\\nrecovery priority over younger predictions and replays. Squash younger\\nwork until IF presents the registered recovery PC, including during\\nbus waits. Let the resolving instruction retire once on channel 0.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Warm A-to-B while B falls through, then take B with imemReady low.\\nCheck the branch destination and a JAL link at that destination.\\nExercise cold recovery, a predecessor-index\\nalias, changed bus instruction at the same PC, FENCE.I, a changed\\nJALR base and a misaligned direct target. Check exact retirement PCs,\\norder and suppression of a wrong-path store on both recovery cycles.\\n'}]} \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
