{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if 'r7s0' in d['id'] or 'r7s1' in d['id']:\n  print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\n  for c in d['changes']: print(c['file'],c['description'])\nPY\nls -a fpga && ls /opt && ls -a bench\"", "aggregated_output": "Retime the final quotient calculation and its writeback. Let A and B be\nthe unsigned magnitudes and k = floor(log2(B)) for B > 0. In pass 4, the\nnormalized divisor has served its last use. Overwrite normalized_q with\nA >> k while the DSP finishes the reciprocal refinement. In pass 5 use\nthat register as DSP operand A and capture product[65:34] directly. In\npass 6 compute the residual from the original A, trial quotient and B.\n\nShifting early can discard up to 2^k - 1 dividend units. The normalized\nreciprocal is a lower bound and its existing pre-scaling quotient error\nis below 0.91 for a 32-bit dividend. The combined underestimate before\ninteger truncation is below (2^k - 1 + 0.91) / 2^k < 1. The existing\nsingle residual correction therefore gives the exact quotient and\nremainder. Zero-divisor handling and sign restoration use the original\noperands and flags.\n\nCapture the corrected divide result alongside EX/MEM on the transfer\nedge. In MEM, OR three independently masked sources into the existing\nMEM/WB ALU-result register. The sources are the product, registered\ndivide result and ordinary EX/MEM result. Give an immediate DIV/REM\nconsumer the same one-cycle WB forwarding interlock as a multiply\nconsumer. The data-address and EX/MEM-to-EX bypass paths now carry\nthe ordinary ALU result directly.\n\nA scratch implementation completed ten CRC-validated CoreMark iterations\nin 3,444,219 bracketed cycles with both buses stalling, matching the\nchampion. Gowin placements 0, 1 and 2 reached 76.575, 77.654 and\n77.654 MHz. The median gives 225.46 iterations/s, 9.23% above 206.41.\nPre-scaling alone reached 73.086 MHz on placement 1. Placement 0 of the\ncomplete design used 3,382 logic cells, 785 registers, seven BSRAMs and\ntwo DSPs. The worst path moved to fetch on placement 0 and WB-to-data-RAM\non placements 1 and 2. Selftest and all three random programs matched\nthe retirement reference on zero-wait and stalled buses. The 29 real\nALU tests and 30 pipeline tests passed, including signed arithmetic,\nzero divisors, overflow, held\n…[4713 of 7785 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:95c4fd12da97dc8f]…\n7:34] of the second as R. In parallel, register zero-extended\nA minus zero-extended B in 33 bits on pass 1 and X = A >> k on\npass 2. Pass 5 captures Q from bits [65:34] of the third product.\nPass 6 captures A-Q*B in 32 bits and (A-B)-Q*B in 33 bits. A clear\nbit 32 in the latter selects its low word for REM and increments Q\nfor DIV. Apply the existing result sign and division-by-zero rules.\nHold results through backpressure and retain the common request,\nresult-valid and ALTOPS timing.\n\ntest/test_alu.py Cover all 32 divisor exponents with dividends just below, at and\nabove exact multiples and with all k discarded bits set. Check DIVU\nand REMU against integer divmod, including held responses. Issue\nMULHSU, DIVU and MULHU consecutively at their legal issue edges to\nexercise the separate operand registers. Keep the existing signed\ncorner, reciprocal-bin, multiply-throughput and latency checks.\n\n.\n..\nbench_stall_gen.sv\nconstraints\ncore_bench.sv\ncore_bench_si.sv\nscripts\ngowin\nhwe-python\nhwe-toolchain\n.\n..\nprograms\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
