{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_128","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-001-r9s0.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261121-001-r9s0\ntitle: Forward a registered EX product to the next instruction\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion registers multiply operands in EX and produces their product in\n  MEM. An immediately dependent instruction waits a cycle for WB. On Gowin\n  placement option 1, the shared multiplier also creates an 18.440 ns timing\n  path from the divider seed ROM through the DSP to the WB data register.\n  Decode already registers the bypass choice for each source operand.\nhypothesis: |\n  Give integer multiplication a dedicated DSP fed directly by the two\n  forwarded register operands in EX. Capture the architectural product word\n  in its own EX/MEM register. Decode can identify a multiply producer and\n  select that register for its consumer one clock later. This removes the\n  product-use bubble and takes the divider ROM off the multiply writeback\n  path. The divider keeps its existing latency and arithmetic.\n  \n  A scratch prototype completed ten CRC-validated iterations in 3,062,533\n  bracketed cycles with both buses stalling, saving 35,284 cycles. Gowin\n  placement option 1 reached 76.044 MHz using four DSP blocks, giving\n  248.30 iterations/s. Acceptance at this cycle count needs over 78.663 MHz.\n  Keeping the product in a separate register lets the implementer tune the\n  consumer mux without placing a product select after the ordinary ALU.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 1\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Multiply two 33-bit signed operands formed by prefixing the required\n      sign or zero bit to each forwarded source. Select bits 31:0 for MUL\n      and bits 63:32 for the three high-word operations. Under\n      RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS use the corresponding reference add/sub and XOR\n      constants in this same path. Hold the product alongside EX/MEM during\n      a data wait. Drive the existing MEM product input from the registered\n      word and add it as an EX forwarding source for either operand. Issue\n      only divides to the existing shared arithmetic unit in this core.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Split each ID-stage match against the EX writer into ordinary-result\n      and multiply-result selects using mul_in_ex. Register those selects\n      with the operands, preserving youngest-writer priority and x0 handling.\n      Remove mul_in_ex from the late-result interlock. Keep unverified loads\n      and completed divides on their existing WB forwarding schedule.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Require consecutive retirements for an unstalled MUL-to-ALU or\n      MUL-to-MUL dependency. Exercise both source positions, signed and mixed\n      high words, a younger multiply overwriting an older destination, and\n      a dependent store held under backpressure. Check exact RVFI operands,\n      results, order and one accepted store. Preserve DIV-to-use interlocks.\nYAML\npython - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport jsonschema, json,yaml\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-001-r9s0.yaml');jsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')));print('Validated')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
