{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nrs=list(map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')))\nfor r in rs[-6:]:\n print(r['id'], r['seeds'],r['critical_path'])\n print(r['motivation'])\n if 'r10s1' in r['id'] or 'r9s0' in r['id']:print(r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'],r['implementation_notes'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-001-r9s0 [72.196, 79.301, 79.301] {'from': 'cpu/u_if/response_q.instr_response_q.instr_0_0_s', 'to': 'cpu/u_if/pc_q_31_s1', 'slack_ns': -8.851, 'arrival_ns': 19.146, 'logic_cells': 22, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_if']}\nThe champion registers multiply operands in EX and produces their product in\nMEM. An immediately dependent instruction waits a cycle for WB. On Gowin\nplacement option 1, the shared multiplier also creates an 18.440 ns timing\npath from the divider seed ROM through the DSP to the WB data register.\nDecode already registers the bypass choice for each source operand.\n\nGive integer multiplication a dedicated DSP fed directly by the two\nforwarded register operands in EX. Capture the architectural product word\nin its own EX/MEM register. Decode can identify a multiply producer and\nselect that register for its consumer one clock later. This removes the\nproduct-use bubble and takes the divider ROM off the multiply writeback\npath. The divider keeps its existing latency and arithmetic.\n\nA scratch prototype completed ten CRC-validated iterations in 3,062,533\nbracketed cycles with both buses stalling, saving 35,284 cycles. Gowin\nplacement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 70.101, 76.044 and 76.044 MHz.\nThe median gives 248.30 iterations/s using four DSP blocks. Acceptance at this cycle count needs over 78.663 MHz.\nKeeping the product in a separate register lets the implementer tune the\nconsumer mux without placing a product select after the ordinary ALU.\nSelftest and all three random programs matched the reference retirement\ntrace with both buses stalling.\n \nhyp-20261121-002-r9s1 [69.987, 74.789, 74.789] {'from': 'cpu/u_id/reg_q.rs1_sel.ex_mem_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_if/response_q.instr_response_q.instr_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -9.288, 'arrival_ns': 19.531, 'logic_cells': 38, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_if']}\nThe champion runs ten iterations in 3,097,817 cycles. Repeating Gowin\nplacement 1 reproduced its 76.071 MHz median. The first setup path runs\nfrom the recipro\n…[4152 of 7224 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:735907fb881a4987]…\n line would penalize short basic blocks. At 3.08\nmillion cycles for ten iterations, 83.43 MHz clears the promotion\nthreshold. The estimate assumes the registered boundary reaches\nabout 85.5 MHz. Cross-block branch bubbles are the main cycle risk.\n \nhyp-20261121-003-r10s2 [76.702, 80.074, 80.074] {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu/mem_product_7_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_if/response_q.disp_fields_response_q.disp_fields_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -8.037, 'arrival_ns': 18.28, 'logic_cells': 33, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu', 'cpu/u_if']}\nOn the champion's 79.301 MHz placement, four of the five longest setup\npaths run from the saved successor instruction into the fetch PC. The\nworst goes through offset selection, carry propagation and the RAM-range\ncheck before driving PC bit 30. A replay of 529,588 conditional branches\nfrom the timed trace found that expanding the agree predictor from 64 to\n256 counters would remove only 6,716 direction misses. At one bubble per\nmiss that is about 0.22% of the champion's 3,062,533 cycles.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
