{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_55","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nr=list(map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')))\nfor a in [x for x in r if 'r3s0' in x['id'] or 'r6s2' in x['id']]:\n print(a['id'], a['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20261120-001-r3s0 Decode the raw fetch word continuously and squash a younger instruction by\nclearing the ID/EX valid bit. This lets register reads and the load-use\ncomparison run in parallel with EX branch resolution. A redirect still\nselects the fetch PC on the same edge. A scratch prototype completed the\ncanonical ten-iteration CoreMark run in the champion's 4,491,485 bracketed\ncycles. Gowin reported 47.064, 49.594 and 49.594 MHz across placement options.\nThat median yields 110.42 iterations/s, 5.9% above the champion. The prototype\npassed all 53 single-retire formal checks and the three stalled random traces.\n\nhyp-20261120-003-r6s2 Let each static load predict whether its dependent consumer can issue\nimmediately. Use 64 one-bit entries indexed by load PC[7:2], initialized\nto fast and trained on the last completed load's forwarding hit. A slow\nprediction takes the existing load-use bubble. On a fast prediction admit\nthe consumer, even though the result lookup and its tag check are pending.\n\nIndex a 1024-row result buffer in EX using the actual forwarded effective\naddress. Store the full raw word, the last extracted 32-bit result, its byte\noffset, width and signedness, a RAM address tag and validity. The candidate\nresult reaches the consumer's EX forwarding mux directly from the RAM\noutput. In parallel, MEM checks the tag, access format, alignment and valid\nbit. On a match, the consumer advances and the load completes internally.\nOn a miss, complete the ordinary demand load. If the admitted consumer\ndepends on it, hold that consumer through bus waits and then for the edge\nthat deposits the load in WB. Retry it with WB forwarding. An independent\nfollower can advance on the demand response edge.\n\nBetween the CoreMark markers, 347,379 of 544,852 loads matched the saved\nresult. Of 185,949 dependent instructions admitted behind a load, 3,878\nneeded a retry. Keeping the same result buffer and forcing every dependency\nto interlock took 3,346,686 cycles. Predicting latency saved 181,571 cycles\nagainst that control.\n\nA scratch implementation completed ten CRC-validated iterations in\n3,165,115 bracketed cycles with both stall generators, versus 3,444,219\non the champion. Gowin reached 61.561, 61.670 and 61.670 MHz. The median\ngives 194.84 iterations/s, 11.73% above 174.38. At that cycle count,\n57.733 MHz clears the acceptance threshold. A comparison build\nthat extracted every cached operand on the consumer path took 3,093,669\ncycles but reached only 57.604 MHz and 186.20 iterations/s. Placement\nzero of the proposed design used 3,557 logic cells, 827 registers and\n14 BSRAMs. All 53 fast formal checks passed. The selftest and three random\nprograms matched the reference with ready and stalled buses. All 27 existing\npipeline tests and four directed warm-buffer tests passed.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
