{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_72","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-002-r12s1.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261121-002-r12s1\ntitle: \"Partition division by divisor size to remove the Newton feedback path\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  Gowin placement 1 reproduces the champion's 83.645 MHz median. Its first\n  setup path runs from the divider seed ROM to residual_q[31] in 17.250 ns.\n  The next takes 17.244 ns from residual_q through correction and sign\n  restoration. Ten CoreMark iterations take 3,081,605 cycles and execute\n  no timed divides. At that cycle count a median above 87.50 MHz clears\n  the tournament margin.\nhypothesis: |\n  Split unsigned magnitude division at B=256. For 1 <= B < 256, read a\n  256-word ROM indexed directly by B containing floor((2^32-1)/B).\n  Multiply the dividend magnitude A by this word and take the high half\n  as q. The reciprocal underestimates 2^32/B by at most one unit, so\n  q is exact or one low. Register q, its product with B, and the\n  residual on separate edges. If the residual is at least B, increment\n  q and subtract B. The seven available clocks leave room to register\n  the corrected magnitude before sign restoration.\n\n  For B >= 256, the top byte of A is already a valid partial remainder.\n  Only six hexadecimal quotient digits remain. On pass 0 register the\n  widened multiples 1B through 15B and seed the remainder from A[31:24].\n  In each of passes 1 through 6 append the next dividend nibble, subtract\n  the registered multiples in parallel, and select the difference for\n  the largest multiple that fits. Adjacent borrow bits give a one-hot\n  digit select. Use a 36-bit unsigned trial value and multiples with an\n  explicit borrow bit. After six digits the quotient and remainder are\n  exact. Restore the requested sign from the registered magnitude and\n  implement the RV32M zero-divisor and signed-overflow cases.\n\n  This removes the normalization shifter, reciprocal refinement feedback\n  and serial residual correction from the general divide path. Retain the\n  existing issue edge, seven busy clocks and held-result handshake in both\n  real arithmetic and ALTOPS. The expected gain comes from Fmax. The\n  wide remainder selection and close EX and fetch paths limit confidence.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Implement the two magnitude ranges behind the existing divide port.\n      Give the small-divisor products fixed input sources and register their\n      outputs. Build the large-divisor digit selection from parallel borrows\n      and a balanced one-hot result mux. Capture operands once and hold\n      the selected result stable until accepted.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Retain the existing latency, backpressure and arithmetic checks.\n      Add quotient and remainder cases at B=255, 256 and 257, trial digits\n      0 and 15, exact multiples and both neighbors, maximum unsigned inputs\n      and all signed operand combinations. Compare with exact integer division.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
