{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nr = [json.loads(s) for s in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')]\nfor q in r[-7:]:\n print(q['title'])\n print(q['hypothesis']['hypothesis'][:3500])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_20", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nr = [json.loads(s) for s in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')]\nfor q in r[-7:]:\n print(q['title'])\n print(q['hypothesis']['hypothesis'][:3500])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Split direct-target prediction across the fetch clock edge\nKeep the direction decision and compute the exact direct target in two\nparallel halves. For an aligned source in the 1 MiB RAM, add PC[10:2] to\noffset[10:2] in one nine-bit adder. In parallel compute both ten-bit sums\nof the zero-extended upper operands PC[19:11] and offset[19:11], one\nwith carry-in zero and one with carry-in one. For each upper candidate,\ncarry-out equal to offset[20] means the signed target is in RAM. Qualify\neach candidate with the legal B/J direction request and aligned offset.\nRegister the low sum, its carry, both upper candidates and their enables.\nThe registered carry selects the upper word bits and enable for the next\nfetch. Keep full-width fall-through and EX recovery in separate registers.\nThis moves the final carry selection across the clock edge while preserving\nfirst-encounter JAL and backward-branch prediction.\n\nPredecode the thirteen format-dependent displacement bits on a successor\nrow fill. These are offset[19:11] and offset[4:1]. For B/J, instruction\nbit 3 selects their layout while the full opcode and funct3 independently\ndecide steering eligibility. The row grows from 51 to 64 bits. On a bus\nwait the registered row supplies those fields with its word. A ready bus\nsupplies both the new word and its own fields. This removes a format mux\nahead of the two adders on the block-RAM path without changing a prediction.\n\nLet these candidate registers capture every clock. Capture the current\nfetch PC in a retry register in parallel. A single registered stall bit\nreselects that saved PC when admission failed. A registered correction\noverrides retry on a miss, including during an instruction wait. This\ntakes the late interlock off the enables of the wide target registers.\nOnly an admitted instruction launches a successor lookup or trains a pair.\n\nA split-adder prototype with separate recovery, before row predecode,\npassed the existing pipeline suite and reached 78.339 MHz on placement\noption 1. The predecoded prototype with clock-e\n…[11633 of 14705 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b746cffb8a9a5030]…\newton error D*(P/2^18-1/D)^2 and downward rounding. Converting to U\nadds less than 2^-32 in reciprocal units. At k=1, d=0, X is exact and\nthe total quotient loss is below 0.813. At 2<=k<=10, d=0 and the loss\nis below 0.657 including truncation of X. At k=11 and k=12, d is at\nmost 1/2048 and 3/4096 respectively. At k>=13, d<1/1024. In these\nthree ranges bin error costs at most 9/16 of a quotient unit, rounding\nbelow 0.001 and truncating X below 1/4. Thus Q is exact or one low and\nthe residual fits in 32 bits because Q*B<=A.\n\nA scratch prototype using the same seed and a 34-by-34-bit quotient\nmultiplier passed the ALU and pipeline suites and all five CoreMark CRCs.\nTen iterations took 3,081,605 bracketed cycles. Gowin returned 85.759,\n85.893 and 85.893 MHz, giving 278.73 iterations/s. Promotion at that\ncycle count requires more than 87.492 MHz. The proposed 32-bit operands\nnarrow the multiplier input muxes and registers. The remaining 1.6 MHz\nis a timing hypothesis, with fetch routing the main competing path.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
