{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_71","type":"agent_message","text":"The parallel fetch tables reduced the bracketed run to 3,034,084 cycles with all CRCs correct, but their RAM-to-decode path limited placement 1 to 79.985 MHz. I’m using that result to frame the final hypothesis around a registered two-instruction packet, with the next RAM lookup overlapped with issue."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_72", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-002-r14s1.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261121-002-r14s1\ntitle: Issue from registered two-instruction path packets\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's 3,081,605 cycles leave little room for a pure IPC gain.\n  In a scratch experiment, parallel sequential and taken fetch tables\n  completed ten iterations in 3,034,084 cycles with all five CRCs correct.\n  Placement 1 reached only 79.985 MHz. Its longest path ran from the\n  sequential instruction RAM through decode to the early load index.\n  A four-word aligned block in round 10 also paid demand waits at block\n  boundaries. Two successive instructions on the executed path provide\n  two issue cycles in which to fetch and register the following pair,\n  including when a taken branch separates the words.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the per-instruction successor lookup with a 512-entry synchronous\n  table of overlapping instruction pairs. For three consecutive ordered MEM\n  transfers P, Q, R in aligned RAM, write one row indexed by P[10:2].\n  It contains P's high address tag, the words at P and Q, Q's word address\n  and R's word address. R is the head to probe after this pair. Slide by one\n  transfer so either instruction of a hot loop can become the next head.\n\n  Issue P and Q from a pair held in fabric registers. While issuing P,\n  launch the synchronous read for R using the recorded index. The RAM\n  delivers its response during Q. At the end of Q, load the next pair\n  into the issue registers if the requested head matches. This schedule\n  sustains one instruction per clock across a warm packet boundary and\n  puts a flip-flop between the RAM output and the register-file read or\n  speculative load-buffer index. Use a ready bus word at the presented\n  PC with priority. On a cold or broken pair, the bus can deliver that\n  single word immediately while fetch restarts the packet lookup.\n\n  Determine each predicted successor from the actual issued opcode, the\n  existing agree counter and the recorded edge. Select a nonsequential\n  recorded edge only for a legal jump or a predicted-taken legal branch.\n  Otherwise select PC+4. If this choice diverges from Q or R, discard the\n  unused continuation. Carry the selected successor with its instruction\n  to EX for verification. An EX correction has priority over either slot.\n  Preserve the issued PC, word and prediction on an interlock or replay,\n  and qualify every saved word against its full aligned RAM PC. Invalidate\n  colliding reads and discard active pairs, pending reads and training\n  history on reset or FENCE.I.\n\n  At 3.06 million bracketed cycles a median above 90.97 MHz would clear\n  the promotion margin. The estimated gain assumes 92.5 MHz at that\n  cycle count. Packet breaks at alternating branches and the independent\n  EX forwarding path are the main risks. The packet design is unmeasured.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Build and validate the three-PC training window, store pair rows in\n      block RAM, and implement the registered two-slot issue schedule with a\n      one-word demand path. Retain a tagged pending response through holds.\n      Sweep row validity on reset and FENCE.I and cancel delayed writes.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Carry the exact predicted successor and fall-through alongside the instruction through fetch and ID/EX.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Latch the fetch prediction and fall-through on the same advance as their instruction and operands.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Check the recorded prediction against the full resolved RV32IM next PC and use the matching fall-through for link writes and trap recovery.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Connect packet admission, replay, MEM transfers and redirect recovery to the existing single-retire pipeline.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Warm a pair that crosses a taken branch and retire it and the next pair\n      consecutively with imemReady low. Hold each slot across a data wait\n      and replay a dependent second slot. Exercise a changed branch outcome\n      with a wrong-path store, a head-index alias, and FENCE.I with a pending\n      response. Check channel-0 order, PC, instruction and memory effects.\nYAML\npython - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-002-r14s1.yaml')\nx=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\njsonschema.Draft7Validator(json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text())).validate(x)\nassert x['id']==p.stem and x['category']=='structural'\nassert all((Path('cores/bench')/c['file']).is_file() for c in x['changes'])\nprint('Validated',p, p.stat().st_size)\nprint(284.20*1.046*3.06/10,92.5*10/3.06/284.2)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
