{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nrows=list(map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')))\nfor r in [rows[34],rows[35],rows[37],rows[39],rows[40],rows[42]]:\n print(r['id'],r['critical_path'],r['fmax_mhz'],r['cycles'])\n print(r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\n print(r['seeds'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-001-r12s0 {'from': 'cpu/fwd_rs1_sel_q_1_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_26_s0', 'slack_ns': -7.034, 'arrival_ns': 17.328, 'logic_cells': 35, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']} 87.58 3081605\nKeep the seven-pass Newton schedule and retime its final correction. Let\nA and B be the dividend and divisor magnitudes, Q the existing quotient\nestimate and P = Q*B. For B > 0 the estimate is exact or one low, so\nP fits in 32 bits. The correction test A-P >= B is equivalent to\nA >= B and P <= A-B. Precompute A-B while the reciprocal refines.\nRegister the low product bits directly from the DSP on pass 6. The next\ncycle can compare P with A-B while forming both candidate remainders.\n\nFold sign restoration into those two parallel differences. For sign\nflag n, form X + ~P + (1-n) for X equal to A and A-B. XOR the selected\nword with a mask of n. This gives X-P for a positive result and P-X for\na negative result. Compute the adjacent quotient on pass 6 so DIV and\nREM share the final XOR. All arithmetic on result words wraps at 32 bits.\n\nThe scratch implementation completed ten CoreMark iterations in\n3,081,605 bracketed cycles with both buses stalling and all five CRCs\ncorrect. Gowin placements 0, 1 and 2 reached 83.100, 87.580 and\n87.580 MHz. The median gives 284.20 iterations/s, 4.71% above 271.43.\nThe promotion threshold is 283.92 iterations/s, so the timing margin\nfor an independent implementation is narrow. Placement 0 used 3,601\nlogic cells and 1,078 registers, up 15 and 65 respectively, with the\nsame four DSPs and 13 block RAMs. On the median placement the longest\npath now runs from the resolved load-buffer row to MEM/WB write data.\n\nThe prototype passed 30 ALU cases, including the proposed boundary test,\n39 pipeline cases and all 53 fast ALTOPS formal checks. It matched\nreference retirement on selftest and three random programs with and\nwithout bus stalls. A bit-vector equivalence check found no difference\nfrom the existing 32-bit correction for any P <= A, taking P = 0 fo\n…[7946 of 11018 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d97b8fda24849706]…\nresolve a certified\ndirect transfer with the registered target and the actual branch condition.\nFor an aligned, actually taken direct transfer without a certified target,\nkill the younger slot, compute the full target in MEM and redirect from\na register one clock later. A correctly predicted fall-through can advance\nat full rate regardless of whether its target was cached.\n\nA scratch predictor with target metadata and the original EX adder took\n3,069,283 bracketed cycles with both buses stalling and all five CRCs\ncorrect. The PC and instruction matched on 334,111 of 343,792 taken\ndirect transfers (97.18%). Charging one extra clock on the remaining\n9,681 gives a first-order estimate of 3,078,964 cycles. Promotion then\nrequires more than 91.53 MHz. The scratch version reached 85.125 MHz on\nplacement 1. The estimate of 299 iterations/s assumes the proposed\nremoval of the EX adder and target check can reach 92.2 MHz. The ALU\nfeedback and load-return paths remain competing timing limits.\n\n[84.895, 87.426, 87.426]\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
