{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_40", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nrows=list(map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')))\nfor i in [31,32,33]:print(rows[i]['id'],rows[i]['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-001-r11s0 Look up the existing 512-row successor table one fetch earlier. While fetching A and predicting B, send B's nine index bits to the synchronous read port. During B the RAM presents the row for B, which contains its previously observed successor C and C's instruction. At the edge admitting B, capture this row in fabric flip-flops. During C, the saved instruction, its address and its direct-target metadata are available from those flip-flops. A warm stream still admits one instruction per clock.\n\nThe lookup is speculative and internal. Use the low bits of the sequential or predicted direct address without waiting for full target-range validation, EX redirect or the load-use interlock. Check the registered probe index against the actual B index before promoting its response. Keep the full word-address check before issuing C from that response. A bad probe costs a cache opportunity. A ready bus can still supply the instruction immediately. Stage accepted training pairs for one clock so the physical write port takes registered inputs.\n\nThe first lookahead prototype completed ten iterations in 3,107,313 bracketed cycles with all five CRCs. At that count the median must exceed 84.162 MHz for promotion. A saved instruction initially timed out across load-use and divide holds. Adding a demand re-read of the last admitted predecessor fixed that case and passed all 38 pipeline tests. The revised design still needs a bracketed CoreMark run and median timing. The main risks are missed probes after redirects and a new timing path into the nine-bit RAM address.\n\nhyp-20261121-002-r11s1 Move direct-control steering across the synchronous successor lookup.\nWhen fetch admits P, read the existing 512-entry table at P[10:2],\nregister P+4 and register a one-bit request to use the returned address.\nRequest it for a legal JAL or JALR and for a legal branch whose agree\ncounter predicts taken. On the following cycle select the registered\nrow's successor PC on a qualified response, or P+4 on a miss. The\nfetched instru\n…[2665 of 5737 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:deb05ac55f362cce]…\ne alongside\nthe low base hint and alignment information.\n\nFor a same-page prediction the returned RAM tag identifies the required\nbase page. Verify the actual rs1 against {12'b0, selected_tag, saved_b}\nin EX. Compare the physical operand sources in parallel and qualify the\nresults with the existing forwarding selects. This checks all 32 bits\nand removes the resolved address adder from the early-hit decision.\nA cross-page displacement, wrong base, unavailable bank or collision\nuses the existing interlock and the independent resolved-address probe\nin MEM. A scratch two-bank implementation completed ten CoreMark\niterations in 3,057,579 bracketed cycles with both buses stalling.\nAll five CRCs matched. An exhaustive check of all 16,777,216 base-low\nand displacement pairs reproduced the word index and page-crossing\ndecision. At that cycle count promotion requires more than 82.815 MHz.\nThe two-bank median Fmax is unmeasured. The main timing risks are routing\nthe two RAM responses and the independent fetch-PC path.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
