{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | cut -c1-1900\"", "aggregated_output": "3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Move DIV and REM to a shared four-pass divider\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The 5.442 MHz baseline takes 4,491,485 cycles for ten CoreMark iterations.\\nGowin reports a 199.837 ns, 156-cell path through forwarding and the EX ALU.\\nThe ALU evaluates full-width signed and unsigned division and remainder\\ncombinationally. Their serial quotient decisions are the likeliest source\\nof this depth. Disassembly of the canonical RV32IM -O3 ELF shows five\\nDIVU instructions, all in main. The measured start_time/iterate/stop_time\\nsequence executes none of them, and the ELF contains no DIV, REM or REMU.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The 5.442 MHz baseline takes 4,491,485 cycles for ten CoreMark iterations.\\nGowin reports a 199.837 ns, 156-cell path through forwarding and the EX ALU.\\nThe ALU evaluates full-width signed and unsigned division and remainder\\ncombinationally. Their serial quotient decisions are the likeliest source\\nof this depth. Disassembly of the canonical RV32IM -O3 ELF shows five\\nDIVU instructions, all in main. The measured start_time/iterate/stop_time\\nsequence executes none of them, and the ELF contains no DIV, REM or REMU.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Give EX one blocking divider for DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU. Latch forwarded\\noperands on issue, divide their unsigned magnitudes with eight restoring\\nquotient-bit steps per clock for four clocks, then select and sign-correct\\nthe quotient or remainder. Use the subtraction borrow to select the next\\nremainder at each step. The register between passes cuts the full-width\\nrecurrence out of the one-cycle ALU path. Only division and remainder add\\nissue stalls, so matrix multiplies and ordinary integer dependencies can\\nsustain their current issue rate.\\n\\nExpect nearly the same marker-bracketed cycle count. Extra divide cycles\\nduring setup can shift the phase of t\n6:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261120-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Fold the divider into seven five-bit passes\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Gowin traced the\n…[10235 of 13307 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c99f34da1ba8cf36]…\nt output. This limits the 71.092 MHz clock even though\\nthe timed CoreMark loop executes no divides.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Dedicate one registered-input multiplier to ordinary MUL and give the\\nblocking divider four separately inferred products with fixed operand\\nsources. Two products implement the two Newton refinements, one estimates\\nthe quotient, and one computes Q*B for exact correction. While the\\nreciprocal refines, pre-shift dividend magnitude A by the divisor\\nexponent k and precompute A-B. The quotient product then feeds a fixed\\nbit slice. The residual product feeds two parallel subtractors whose\\nregistered outputs supply both possible remainders and the correction\\nbit. This keeps the seven divide clocks and one multiply issue per clock.\\n\\nFor nonzero divisor magnitude B let D = B / 2^k, k = floor(log2(B)),\\nS = 2^34 and r = R/S after refinement. The existing 512-bin seed has\\nrelative error below 1/512 + 2/4096. Each rounded Newton step keeps a\\nlower bound and maps relative error e to at most e\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
