{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_72", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-002-r8s1.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261121-002-r8s1\ntitle: Compute exact RV32M division with a linear reciprocal table and two dividend guard bits\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion takes 3,097,817 cycles for ten CoreMark iterations. Its\n  reported 19.674 ns worst path runs from the Newton seed ROM to the quotient\n  register. The timed loop executes no divides, so that feedback path sets\n  the clock for unrelated instructions. The score needs a median above\n  74.887 MHz at the champion's cycle count to clear the acceptance margin.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the two Newton refinements with one piecewise-linear reciprocal\n  lookup and a feed-forward sequence of registered products. Small integer\n  divisors, which require the most quotient precision, normalize exactly\n  onto table entries. Larger divisors have smaller quotients and tolerate\n  interpolation error. Two guard bits allow the dividend shift to run\n  alongside the lookup. The quotient multiply then ends at a fixed bit\n  slice, leaving a clock to register the sign-corrected result. Give\n  ordinary MUL its own input registers to sustain one issue per clock.\n\n  For nonzero divisor magnitude B, let k = floor(log2(B)), N = B << (31-k),\n  D = N / 2\"'^31 and S = 2''^32. The 1,024 rows cover D in [1,2). Row i has\n  left endpoint L = (1024+i)/1024. A tangent at L underestimates 1/D.\n  Round its intercept down and its slope magnitude up. With d = D-L,\n  the absolute underestimate is at most d''^2 + d/2''^15 + 2/S. For k <= 10,\n  d is zero and the error is at most 1/S. At k=11 and k=12, the largest d\n  is respectively 1/2048 and 3/4096. At k >= 13 it is below 1/1024.\n  Using X = floor(4*A/2''^k) spends less than 1/4 quotient unit on truncation\n  and costs nothing for k <= 2. These bounds keep the total underestimate\n  below one quotient unit. One residual comparison therefore suffices for\n  every unsigned magnitude, including exact multiples.\n\n  A scratch implementation reached 68.938, 78.100 and 78.100 MHz across\n  Gowin placements. The median and the champion'\"'s cycle count imply\n  252.11 iterations/s, a 9.09% gain. Placement zero used 3,480 logic cells,\n  876 registers, 15 BSRAMs and seven DSPs. The median placement's worst\n  path now runs from the saved instruction to the fetch PC.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 9\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Keep the request/response controller and seven busy clocks. Register\n      signed 33-bit operands for the ordinary MUL family on mul_issue,\n      choosing sign extension per operand and returning the requested word\n      in MEM. Capture divide magnitudes A and B, operation, sign and zero\n      flags on divide issue. Use separate fixed-source products for the\n      table correction, quotient estimate and residual.\n\n      Pass 0 registers N and k. On pass 1 synchronously read the 48-bit ROM\n      row indexed by N[30:21], save t = N[20:0], X = {A,2'b00} >> k in\n      34 bits and A-B in 33 bits. For row i and m=1024+i, store the 32-bit\n      floor((2\"'^42-1)/m) and the 16-bit ceil(2''^35/m''^2). Calculate these\n      constants in unsigned 64-bit arithmetic at elaboration. Pass 2\n      registers the 37-bit product of t and the slope. Pass 3 registers\n      R = intercept - ceil(product/2''^14), using the discarded bits for\n      rounding. Pass 4 registers Q = (X*R)[65:34] from the full 66-bit\n      product. Pass 5 registers A-Q*B in 32 bits and (A-B)-Q*B in 33 bits\n      using the low word of Q*B. A clear bit 32 in the latter selects\n      that remainder and increments Q. Pass 6 registers the selected and\n      sign-corrected word, including division by zero and INT_MIN/-1.\n      Hold it through backpressure. Use the same issue, valid and transfer\n      timing for the existing ALTOPS formulas.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Check divisors at table knots and adjacent representable values for\n      k=10, 11 and 12, then at both ends of bins for larger exponents.\n      Compare DIVU and REMU against divmod for dividends around exact\n      multiples and UINT_MAX, including discarded dividend bits. Exercise\n      signed extremes, consecutive MUL issues and a held divide response.\n'\"''')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
