{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d.get('id','').endswith(('r3s2','r4s2')):\n  print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"Predict legal conditional branches and JAL at fetch. Index 64 two-bit\nagree counters with PC[7:2]. The offset sign supplies the default\ndirection (backward taken), and the counter MSB inverts that direction\nwhen disagreement predominates. Compute the exact B/J target from the\nfetched instruction. Select it only when aligned and in instruction RAM.\nOtherwise select PC+4. JALR uses sequential prediction.\n\nLatch the decision actually used and the alternate direct successor in\nID/EX. A correctly predicted branch admits its successor on the next\nready fetch. On a miss, the forwarded branch comparison selects recovery\nwith a one-bit direction check. The alternate address is already\nregistered. JALR retains its forwarded target calculation in EX. Send\nthe correction directly to the PC and to the ID/EX bubble control.\nDecode and load-use detection can consume the raw instruction in\nparallel because a bubble clears valid and all side-effect controls.\n\nAn isolated prototype of this design on the champion completed ten\niterations in 4,194,434 bracketed cycles with both bus stalls enabled,\nsaving 297,051 cycles. CoreMark validated all five CRCs. Gowin reported\n47.124, 47.840 and 47.840 MHz across its three placement options. The\nmedian gives 114.06 iterations/s, 9.35% above the champion and above the\n109.10 iterations/s acceptance threshold. The worst reported path in\noption 0 now runs from the register file through execution to data RAM.\nThat placement used 2,759 logic cells and 12 LUT-RAM primitives, versus\n2,643 and eight on the champion. All 53 fast formal checks passed.\nThe selftest and three random programs matched their reference retirement\ntraces with both zero-wait and stalled buses.\n\nOn admission of each instruction, use its PC to launch a synchronous\nlookup of its last observed successor. Each of 512 entries packs a valid\nbit, an 18-bit successor word address and the 32-bit instruction fetched\nthere. Train the preceding instruction's row when its successor enters\nID/EX with a ready bus word. This learns taken, fall-through and indirect\nedges using one read port and one write port.\n\nUse the champion's registered next PC for sequential and direct transfers.\nFor a legal JALR with a valid response, steer fetch to the predicted\ntarget while EX resolves the forwarded operand. A cold JALR starts at\nfall-through. Use the bus word whenever ready. During an imem wait,\nsupply the saved word only if its address matches the full selected PC.\nOn an address mismatch, wait for a ready bus word. A correct JALR prediction\nadmits the target on the same edge that advances the jump. If control\nflow was mispredicted, EX squashes the younger instruction and installs\nthe resolved PC even during an imem wait.\n\nAn isolated RTL prototype supplied 604,950 instructions during imem waits\nand correctly steered 12,072 of 13,735 JALRs between the CoreMark markers.\nTen iterations with both stall generators took 3,408,562 bracketed cycles,\nsaving 785,872 cycles with all five CRCs correct. Gowin reported 44.951,\n47.568 and 47.568 MHz. The median gives 139.55 iterations/s, 17.7% above\n118.57. At this cycle count 42.275 MHz clears the 4.6% acceptance margin.\nPlacement option zero used 3,039 logic cells, 623 registers and eight BSRAMs.\nThe prototype passed all 53 fast formal checks and 24 pipeline tests.\nSelftest and all three random programs matched the reference retirement\ntrace with both zero-wait and stalled buses.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
