{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_62","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'].endswith(('r2s1','r2s0','r3s0','r5s1')):\n  print(d['id'], d.get('error'),str(d.get('implementation_notes'))[:1700], d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'][:1400])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_62","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'].endswith(('r2s1','r2s0','r3s0','r5s1')):\n  print(d['id'], d.get('error'),str(d.get('implementation_notes'))[:1700], d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'][:1400])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20261120-001-r2s0 None  Retune the existing blocking divider to compute five binary quotient bits per\nclock over seven passes. Feed it three leading zero dividend bits so every pass\nuses the same five-step datapath. This removes three serial carry chains and\ntheir remainder muxes from the feedback path. Seven compute clocks also leave\nroom for issue, transfer and a redirect or load-use bubble within the formal\nwrapper's ten-cycle forward-progress window.\n\nExpect close to the champion's 4,491,485 bracketed cycles for ten iterations.\nSetup divides can shift the stall generator's phase. At that cycle count a\nmedian Fmax above 33.24 MHz clears the acceptance margin. A 25% gain would\nrequire about 39.72 MHz. The single-cycle multiply path may cap the gain.\n\nhyp-20261120-002-r2s1 None  Reuse a chain of five restoring quotient-bit steps for seven clocks per\ndivide. Prepend three zero bits to the 32-bit dividend magnitude so all\nseven passes use the same wiring. Register the partial quotient and\nremainder after each pass. This removes three dependent 32-bit\nsubtract-and-select steps from the feedback path at the cost of three\nadditional clocks per DIV, DIVU, REM or REMU.\n\nExpect about 43 MHz median and about 4.49 million bracketed cycles for\nten iterations, or roughly 96 iterations/s. Setup divides may shift the\nphase of the bus stalls. At the champion's cycle count, 33.24 MHz clears\nthe 4.6% acceptance margin. Check the new worst path in Gowin because\nforwarding through the combinational multiplier could limit the gain.\n\nhyp-20261120-001-r3s0 None  Decode the raw fetch word continuously and squash a younger instruction by\nclearing the ID/EX valid bit. This lets register reads and the load-use\ncomparison run in parallel with EX branch resolution. A redirect still\nselects the fetch PC on the same edge. A scratch prototype completed the\ncanonical ten-iteration CoreMark run in the champion's 4,491,485 bracketed\ncycles. Gowin reported 47.064, 49.594 and 49.594 MHz across placement options.\nThat median yields 110.42 iterations/s, 5.9% above the champion. The prototype\npassed all 53 single-retire formal checks and the three stalled random traces.\n\nhyp-20261120-002-r5s1 None  Give MEM a single registered-input, signed 36-by-36 multiplier. EX captures\nforwarded operands on every admitted multiply. MEM computes the product in\nparallel with the next instruction's EX, and MEM/WB captures its selected\nword. Independent multiplies can issue every clock. An immediate consumer\nof the product takes one load-style interlock and forwards from WB.\n\nDuring a divide, reuse this same multiplier for two fixed-point Newton\nreciprocal refinements and a final exact remainder check. Keep the existing\nseven busy clocks and blocking divide protocol. An older MUL in MEM can\nfinish on the divide issue edge, before the divider needs the multiplier.\nThis removes both the combinational EX products and the five serial\nsubtract/select steps per divide clock.\n\nA scratch prototype on the champion RTL took 3,444,219 bracketed cycles\nfor ten iterations with both bus stalls enabled. It passed all five CRCs.\nGowin placed it at 55.741, 59.704 and 59.704 MHz using two DSP blocks.\nThe median gives 173.35 iterations/s, 22.9% above the champion. The 35,657\nextra cycles cost 1.05%. At that cycle count the acceptance threshold is\n50.81 MHz. The prototype passed all 53 single-retire ALTOPS formal checks,\nthe 54 ALU and pipeline cocotb tests, and all four RVFI cosim programs with\nboth buses stalling.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
