y. The row grows from 51 to 64 bits. On a bus\n  wait the registered row supplies those fields with its word. A ready bus\n  supplies both the new word and its own fields. This removes a format mux\n  ahead of the two adders on the block-RAM path without changing a prediction.\n  \n  A split-adder prototype with separate recovery, before row predecode,\n  passed the existing pipeline suite and all five CoreMark CRCs in 3,062,533\n  bracketed cycles. Placement option 1 reached 78.339 MHz. At that cycle\n  count acceptance requires a median above 82.9491 MHz. The frequency\n  estimate depends on shortening the saved-word path without moving the\n  limit to the load interlock or EX forwarding.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Store the predecoded fields with the successor word on the existing\n      fill, collision and FENCE.I protocol. Use the two nine-bit word-address\n      halves and register both upper carry cases on fetch advance. Hold all\n      candidates on stalls. Give reset and a captured EX correction priority\n      over speculative selection, holding a correction until the retry can\n      advance. Use the selected PC consistently for the bus, word-tag match,\n      predictor lookup and outgoing instruction. Preserve JALR forecasting.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Remove the alternate-PC payload from IF/ID and ID/EX. Retain the actual direction and training count.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Latch the selected direction and count with the issuing instruction, and hold them across backend stalls.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      On an advancing direct miss, recover to full-width PC plus immediate\n      when IF chose fall-through, or PC plus four when IF chose taken. Keep\n      the one-bit direction comparison, independent JALR target verification,\n      architectural trap behavior and single-retirement RVFI.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise positive and negative offsets on both sides of a 2 KiB carry\n      boundary, upper and lower RAM limits, and halfword-misaligned targets.\n      Check the first fetch address on a cold direct jump and on a saved-word\n      hit with poisoned bus data. Replace a cached jump with a different ready\n      bus word, and hold a correction across a fetch wait. Check retired PCs,\n      link values, order and wrong-path store strobes.\nYAML\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema,pathlib\np=pathlib.Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r10s2.yaml'); v=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()); jsonschema.validate(v,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json'))); print(p, v['title'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r10s2.yaml Split direct-target prediction across the fetch clock edge\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
