{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d.get('round_id') in ['r10'] or '-r10' in d['id'] or 'r9s0' in d['id']:\n  print(d['id'],d['hypothesis'],d['implementation_notes'],d['critical_path'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261121-001-r9s0 {'motivation': 'The champion registers multiply operands in EX and produces their product in\\nMEM. An immediately dependent instruction waits a cycle for WB. On Gowin\\nplacement option 1, the shared multiplier also creates an 18.440 ns timing\\npath from the divider seed ROM through the DSP to the WB data register.\\nDecode already registers the bypass choice for each source operand.\\n', 'hypothesis': 'Give integer multiplication a dedicated DSP fed directly by the two\\nforwarded register operands in EX. Capture the architectural product word\\nin its own EX/MEM register. Decode can identify a multiply producer and\\nselect that register for its consumer one clock later. This removes the\\nproduct-use bubble and takes the divider ROM off the multiply writeback\\npath. The divider keeps its existing latency and arithmetic.\\n\\nA scratch prototype completed ten CRC-validated iterations in 3,062,533\\nbracketed cycles with both buses stalling, saving 35,284 cycles. Gowin\\nplacement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 70.101, 76.044 and 76.044 MHz.\\nThe median gives 248.30 iterations/s using four DSP blocks. Acceptance at this cycle count needs over 78.663 MHz.\\nKeeping the product in a separate register lets the implementer tune the\\nconsumer mux without placing a product select after the ordinary ALU.\\nSelftest and all three random programs matched the reference retirement\\ntrace with both buses stalling.\\n', 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 1, 'confidence': 'medium'}, 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'description': 'Multiply two 33-bit signed operands formed by prefixing the required\\nsign or zero bit to each forwarded source. Select bits 31:0 for MUL\\nand bits 63:32 for the three high-word operations. Under\\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS use the corresponding reference add/sub and XOR\\nconstants in this same path. Hold the product alongside EX/MEM during\\na data wait. Drive the existing MEM product input from the registered\\nword and add it as an EX forwarding source for either operand. Issue\\nonly divi\n…[12846 of 15918 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5a1480ba80b8dbdb]…\n'}, {'file': 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'description': 'On an advancing direct miss, recover to full-width PC plus immediate\\nwhen IF chose fall-through, or PC plus four when IF chose taken. Keep\\nthe one-bit direction comparison, independent JALR target verification,\\narchitectural trap behavior and single-retirement RVFI.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Exercise positive and negative offsets on both sides of a 2 KiB carry\\nboundary, upper and lower RAM limits, and halfword-misaligned targets.\\nCheck the first fetch address on a cold direct jump and on a saved-word\\nhit with poisoned bus data. Replace a cached jump with a different ready\\nbus word, and hold a correction across a fetch wait. Check retired PCs,\\nlink values, order and wrong-path store strobes.\\n'}]}  {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu/mem_product_7_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_if/response_q.disp_fields_response_q.disp_fields_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -8.037, 'arrival_ns': 18.28, 'logic_cells': 33, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu', 'cpu/u_if']}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
