{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_29", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'class ELF|def step|def run|def reset|__main__' test/cosim/reference.py && cat fpga/constraints/clock.sdc && rg -n 'direction|accuracy|total|timed' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import sys,json;exec(\\\"for l in sys.stdin:\\\\n d=json.loads(l.split(\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\",1)[1])\\\\n if d[\\\\x27id\\\\x27].endswith(\\\\x27r1s2\\\\x27):print(d[\\\\x27hypothesis\\\\x27])\\\")'\"", "aggregated_output": "138:    def step(self) -> Optional[dict]:\n307:if __name__ == '__main__':\n// Gowin timing constraint for the V2 FPGA fitness (tools/eval/gowin.py).\n// The target only steers timing-driven optimization; the score is the\n// Actual Fmax from the timing report. Fixed for every design.\ncreate_clock -name clock -period 5 [get_ports {clock}]\n{'motivation': \"The baseline fetches PC+4 until EX resolves a transfer. Since IF feeds ID\\ncombinationally, a taken branch or jump discards one younger fetch slot.\\nA reference trace between the CoreMark markers contains 529,588 conditional\\nbranches, of which 294,425 change the successor, plus 49,367 direct jumps.\\nThe baseline's 199.837 ns worst path crosses forwarding and the EX ALU.\\nPredicting these direct transfers in IF can save cycles with a small table.\\n\", 'hypothesis': 'Index 64 two-bit saturating counters with fetch PC[7:2]. Use the offset\\nsign as the default direction for a legal conditional branch (backward\\ntaken, forward fall-through). Predict taken = offset_sign XOR counter[1].\\nThe counter learns whether to invert the default, so a hot backedge and\\na usually untaken forward guard can share an entry constructively. Predict\\ndirect JAL taken. Compute the signed B/J target in parallel with the table\\nlookup and select it only for a word-aligned address in instruction RAM.\\nUse PC+4 for every other fetch, including JALR. Carry the selected direction\\nbit with the accepted instruction. Direct targets are exact by construction,\\nso EX can detect a miss by comparing this bit with the resolved direction.\\nA miss redirects to the resolved next PC and kills the younger instruction.\\nCorrect predictions let the successor enter ID/EX immediately. An isolated\\nRTL prototype completed ten 2K CoreMark iterations in 4,194,484 bracketed\\ncycles with both bus stalls enabled, versus 4,491,485 on the baseline.\\nAll five CRCs matched. Gowin reported 5.214, 5.486 and 5.486 MHz across\\nthe three placement options. The median gives 13.08 iter/s, a 7.9% gain\\nover 12.12. The prototype use\n…[1344 of 4416 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:4291f6571f1832a1]…\n preserve its trap\\nand link-write suppression. Use the resolved successor for both RVFI\\npc_next and correction. Assert redirect only on a direction mismatch\\nfor a valid instruction advancing past EX. Enable training once when a\\nvalid nontrapping conditional branch advances. Return its saved counter\\nand its resolved direction XOR its immediate sign to IF for training.\\n'}, {'file': 'rtl/core.sv', 'description': 'Wire the training enable, increment signal, resolving PC index and counter snapshot to IF. Use the EX correction to drive the existing hazard flush.'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Exercise a warmed loop and its exit, including a wrong-path store and\\ntwo branches of opposite offset sign sharing a counter. Check retired\\nPCs and store enables across imem and dmem stalls. Cover JAL, JALR,\\na taken misaligned branch and an untaken branch whose encoded target\\nlies outside instruction RAM. Check that a correctly predicted branch\\nfetches its target on the next ready cycle.\\n'}]}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
