{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_59", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'check|trig|assume|assert' formal/riscv-formal/checks/rvfi_liveness_check.sv; sed -n '48,86p' cores/bench/test/test_alu.py; python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(l)\n if r['id'].endswith(('r8s2','r8s1')):\n  print(r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "15:module rvfi_liveness_check (\n16:\tinput clock, reset, trig, check,\n32:\t\t\tif (trig) begin\n33:\t\t\t\tassume(rvfi_valid[`RISCV_FORMAL_CHANNEL_IDX]);\n34:\t\t\t\tassume(!rvfi_halt[`RISCV_FORMAL_CHANNEL_IDX]);\n35:\t\t\t\tassume(insn_order == rvfi_order[64*`RISCV_FORMAL_CHANNEL_IDX +: 64]);\n37:\t\t\tif (check) begin\n38:\t\t\t\tassert(found_next_insn);\n\n\nasync def _check_div(dut, op, a, b, expected, hold_cycles=0):\n    assert dut.div_req_ready.value == 1\n    dut.op.value = op\n    dut.a.value = a & MASK32\n    dut.b.value = b & MASK32\n    dut.div_req_valid.value = 1\n    dut.div_result_ready.value = 0\n    await _tick(dut)  # issue\n\n    dut.div_req_valid.value = 0\n    dut.op.value = ALU_ADD\n    dut.a.value = ~a & MASK32\n    dut.b.value = ~b & MASK32\n    for _ in range(7):\n        assert dut.div_req_ready.value == 0\n        assert dut.div_result_valid.value == 0\n        await _tick(dut)\n\n    for cycle in range(hold_cycles + 1):\n        assert dut.div_req_ready.value == 0\n        assert dut.div_result_valid.value == 1\n        actual = int(dut.div_result.value)\n        assert actual == (expected & MASK32), (\n            f\"op={op} a={a:#x} b={b:#x} expected={expected & MASK32:#x} got={actual:#x}\"\n        )\n        if cycle < hold_cycles:\n            await _tick(dut)\n    dut.div_result_ready.value = 1\n    await _tick(dut)\n    assert dut.div_result_valid.value == 0\n    assert dut.div_req_ready.value == 1\n\n\nasync def _check_mul(dut, op, a, b, expected, previous=None):\n    dut.op.value = op\n    dut.mul_rs1.value = a\n    dut.mul_rs2.value = b\nReplace the two Newton refinements with one piecewise-linear reciprocal\nlookup and a feed-forward sequence of registered products. Small integer\ndivisors, which require the most quotient precision, normalize exactly\nonto table entries. Larger divisors have smaller quotients and tolerate\ninterpolation error. Two guard bits allow the dividend shift to run\nalongside the lookup. The quotient multiply then ends at a fixed bit\nslice, leaving a clock to register the sign-corrected result. Give\nordinary MUL its own inp\n…[2627 of 5699 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:3e10427ba64134fc]…\nercept, distance,\nslope and product adds at most 3*2^-20+2^-25. Thus the refined reciprocal\nunderestimates by less than 2*(2^-18+3*2^-20+2^-25)^2+2/S < 2^-32.\nDiscarding the bottom k dividend bits costs at most 1-2^-k quotient\nunits and reciprocal error on A>>k costs less than 2^-k. The prediction\nis exact or one low, so one check suffices for every nonzero divisor.\n\nAn exhaustive integer sweep of all 2^31 normalized divisors found a maximum\nreciprocal underestimate of 0.655 times 2^-32. A scratch prototype with\none shared wide multiplier ran ten CRC-validated iterations in 3,097,817\nbracketed cycles with both buses stalling. Gowin reported 68.758, 73.980\nand 73.980 MHz. That median gives 238.81 iterations/s, a 3.3% gain.\nAcceptance at this cycle count requires more than 74.887 MHz. The\nlimiting path in the shared prototype moved to fetch. Placement zero used\n3,724 logic cells, 13 BSRAMs and two DSPs. Selftest and all three random\nprograms matched the reference retirement trace with ready and stalled buses.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_60","type":"agent_message","text":"I reproduced the champion’s 83.645 MHz placement and found that its two longest paths both run through the divider. I’ll test a quotient predictor that uses one reciprocal refinement and spends the freed clocks registering the product and correction. The timed CoreMark loop executes no divides, so this targets Fmax directly."}}
