{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_73","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r12s2.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261121-003-r12s2\ntitle: Predict the quotient with one refinement of a bin-edge reciprocal\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Replaying Gowin placement 1 reproduced the champion's 83.645 MHz median.\n  Its longest path runs from the divider seed RAM through the shared DSP\n  and the residual subtractor. The next path runs from the residual through\n  correction and sign restoration. The reported fetch-to-register-file path\n  belongs to placement 0. CoreMark executes no divides between markers, so\n  the divider can raise throughput by shortening the clock at the same IPC.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the two Newton refinements with a 1,024-entry, 18-bit reciprocal\n  seed at the left edge of each bin and one downward-rounded refinement.\n  Small divisors land exactly on bin edges. Off-grid divisors have shorter\n  quotients and tolerate a coarser reciprocal. Two fractional dividend bits\n  leave enough margin for the predicted quotient to be exact or one low.\n\n  For unsigned magnitudes A and B>0, set k=floor(log2 B), N=B<<(31-k),\n  D=N/2\"'^31, i=N[30:21], and S=2''^34. Read P=min(2''^18-1,\n  floor(2''^28/(1024+i))) from a constant ROM. Compute F=2*S-1-floor(N*P/2''^15),\n  R=floor(P*F/2''^18), X=floor(4*A/2''^k), and Q=floor(X*R/2''^36).\n  For T=A-Q*B, T>=B selects Q+1 and T-B. Otherwise use Q and T.\n\n  The exact Newton update for p=P/2''^18 undershoots 1/D by D*(p-1/D)''^2.\n  Rounding adds at most 2/S. With L=(1024+i)/1024 and d=D-L, the squared\n  error term is bounded by max(d''^2,2''^-35). For k<=10, d=0 and reciprocal\n  error costs at most 0.625 quotient units. For k=11 and k=12, d is at\n  most 1/2048 and 3/4096 respectively. For k>=13, d<1/1024. Across the\n  latter three cases the bin approximation costs at most 9/16 of a quotient\n  unit, and rounding costs less than 0.001. Truncating X costs less than\n  another 1/4 in all cases. One residual correction therefore suffices.\n\n  Keep the existing seven busy clocks. Use pass 0 for the ROM read and\n  normalization, 1 for F and X, 2 for R, 3 for Q, 4 for the low product\n  Q*B, 5 for T and 6 for the corrected magnitude. Registering the product\n  removes subtraction from the DSP cycle. Registering the magnitude separates\n  correction from sign restoration. At 3,081,605 bracketed cycles, the\n  median must exceed 87.492 MHz for promotion.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Implement the constant ROM, refinement and seven-pass schedule above.\n      Use unsigned products with explicit widths and fixed slices. Form X\n      from a 34-bit concatenation before shifting. Retain the DIV/REM signs,\n      division by zero, signed overflow, held result, ALTOPS handshake and\n      independent one-cycle EX multiply.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise the new 1,024 bin boundaries, both endpoints of each bin\n      representable at exponents 10 through 13, and exact multiples and\n      their neighbors near UINT_MAX. Check quotient and remainder against\n      integer division, including both correction outcomes and signed cases.\nYAML\npython - <<'\"'PY'\nimport yaml,jsonschema,json\np='cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r12s2.yaml'\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(open(p)),json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('valid')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
