{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_96", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r12s2.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261121-003-r12s2\ntitle: Predict the quotient with one reciprocal refinement and a 32-bit product\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Re-running Gowin placement 1 reproduced the champion's 83.645 MHz median.\n  Its longest path starts at the divider seed ROM, crosses the shared DSP\n  and ends after the residual subtractor. The next path runs from the\n  residual through correction and sign restoration. The reported fetch\n  path belongs to placement 0. CoreMark executes no divides between\n  markers, so shortening these paths can raise fitness at unchanged IPC.\nhypothesis: |\n  Use a 1,024-entry left-endpoint reciprocal table and one Newton refinement.\n  Small divisors normalize exactly onto table entries. Larger divisors have\n  shorter quotients and tolerate bin error. Use the two freed passes to\n  register the residual product before subtraction and the corrected magnitude\n  before sign restoration. Keep the existing seven busy clocks.\n\n  For magnitudes A and B>0, define k=floor(log2 B), N=B<<(31-k), D=N/2\"'^31,\n  i=N[30:21] and S=2''^34. In pass 0 read the 18-bit constant\n  P=min(2''^18-1,floor(2''^28/(1024+i))). In passes 1 and 2 form\n  F=2*S-1-floor(N*P/2''^15) and R=floor(P*F/2''^18). Share one 18-by-35-bit\n  multiplier for both operations. Use a second, unsigned 32-by-32-bit\n  multiplier for the quotient estimate and residual product.\n\n  In parallel with refinement, form X=A>>max(k-2,0). Latch U=floor(R/4)\n  directly from the refinement product slice [51:20]. In pass 3 use Q=A\n  for B=1, take (X*U)>>33 for k=1, and (X*U)>>34 for k>=2. In pass 4\n  register the low word of Q*B. Pass 5 forms T=A-Q*B. Pass 6 selects Q+1\n  and T-B when T>=B, or Q and T otherwise. Apply the RV32M sign and zero\n  rules to the selected magnitude.\n\n  For L=(1024+i)/1024 and d=D-L, the reciprocal underestimate before\n  truncating R to U is at most max(d''^2,2''^-35)+2/S. This follows from the\n  Newton error D*(P/2''^18-1/D)''^2 and downward rounding. Converting to U\n  adds less than 2''^-32 in reciprocal units. At k=1, d=0, X is exact and\n  the total quotient loss is below 0.813. At 2<=k<=10, d=0 and the loss\n  is below 0.657 including truncation of X. At k=11 and k=12, d is at\n  most 1/2048 and 3/4096 respectively. At k>=13, d<1/1024. In these\n  three ranges bin error costs at most 9/16 of a quotient unit, rounding\n  below 0.001 and truncating X below 1/4. Thus Q is exact or one low and\n  the residual fits in 32 bits because Q*B<=A.\n\n  A scratch prototype using the same seed and a 34-by-34-bit quotient\n  multiplier passed the ALU and pipeline suites and all five CoreMark CRCs.\n  Ten iterations took 3,081,605 bracketed cycles. Gowin returned 85.759,\n  85.893 and 85.893 MHz, giving 278.73 iterations/s. Promotion at that\n  cycle count requires more than 87.492 MHz. The proposed 32-bit operands\n  narrow the multiplier input muxes and registers. The remaining 1.6 MHz\n  is a timing hypothesis, with fetch routing the main competing path.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Implement the seed ROM, shared refinement multiplier, shared 32-bit\n      quotient/product multiplier and registered correction schedule above.\n      Keep full intermediate products for the specified slices, the ALTOPS\n      handshake, the one-cycle EX multiply, divide-by-zero results, signed\n      overflow and result retention under backpressure.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise every normalized bin edge at exponents 10 through 13, B=1,\n      2 and 3 near UINT_MAX, exact multiples and their neighbors. Compare\n      both quotient and remainder with integer reference results. Cover\n      both correction outcomes, signed operands and held results.\nYAML\npython - <<'\"'PY'\nimport pathlib,yaml,json,jsonschema\np=pathlib.Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r12s2.yaml')\nh=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\njsonschema.validate(h,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nassert h['id']==p.stem and h['category']=='predictor' and all((p.parents[2]/c['file']).is_file() for c in h['changes'])\nprint('validated',p)\nprint(85.893e7/3081605)\nPY\ntail -3 \\\"\"'$TMPDIR/r12s2p/tests.log\"\ngit status --short'", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
