{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_32", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'].endswith(('r11s0','r3s1','r4s2')):\n  print(d['id'], d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-002-r3s1 Add a bypass from the resolved EX target to the instruction address port.\nSelect the target on an aligned taken redirect and the registered fetch\nPC otherwise. Use this selected address for both io_imemAddr and the\ninstruction's PC in ID. If the front end accepts the word, advance the\nfetch PC to the selected address plus four. If a redirect coincides with\na stall, save the target itself for retry. A ready target can thus enter\nID/EX on the same edge that its branch enters EX/MEM.\n\nA scratch prototype completed ten CRC-validated iterations in 4,136,096\nmarker-bracketed cycles with both stall generators enabled, saving\n355,389 cycles (7.91%). Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached\n46.222, 46.659 and 46.659 MHz. The median gives 112.81 iterations/s\n(+8.16% over 104.30). All 15 pipeline tests passed, including two\ndirected tests for a forwarded JALR and overlapping fetch and data\nwaits. At the measured cycle count, a median above 45.124 MHz clears\nthe 4.6% acceptance margin.\n\nhyp-20261120-003-r4s2 On admission of each instruction, use its PC to launch a synchronous\nlookup of its last observed successor. Each of 512 entries packs a valid\nbit, an 18-bit successor word address and the 32-bit instruction fetched\nthere. Train the preceding instruction's row when its successor enters\nID/EX with a ready bus word. This learns taken, fall-through and indirect\nedges using one read port and one write port.\n\nUse the champion's registered next PC for sequential and direct transfers.\nFor a legal JALR with a valid response, steer fetch to the predicted\ntarget while EX resolves the forwarded operand. A cold JALR starts at\nfall-through. Use the bus word whenever ready. During an imem wait,\nsupply the saved word only if its address matches the full selected PC.\nOn an address mismatch, wait for a ready bus word. A correct JALR prediction\nadmits the target on the same edge that advances the jump. If control\nflow was mispredicted, EX squashes the younger instruction and installs\nthe resolved PC even during an i\n…[1293 of 4365 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:8e747961b2dd4ff0]…\nw bits of the sequential or predicted direct address without waiting for full target-range validation, EX redirect or the load-use interlock. Check the registered probe index against the actual B index before promoting its response. Keep the full word-address check before issuing C from that response. A bad probe costs a cache opportunity. A ready bus can still supply the instruction immediately. Stage accepted training pairs for one clock so the physical write port takes registered inputs.\n\nThe first lookahead prototype completed ten iterations in 3,107,313 bracketed cycles with all five CRCs. At that count the median must exceed 84.162 MHz for promotion. A saved instruction initially timed out across load-use and divide holds. Adding a demand re-read of the last admitted predecessor fixed that case and passed all 38 pipeline tests. The revised design still needs a bracketed CoreMark run and median timing. The main risks are missed probes after redirects and a new timing path into the nine-bit RAM address.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
