{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_38", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor r in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if r['id'].endswith(('r7s2','r5s2','r6s2','r11s2','r9s2')):\n  print(r['id'],r['hypothesis']['motivation'],r.get('implementation_notes'),r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'][-1300:])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-003-r5s2 The champion supplies 604,950 instructions during fetch waits, yet ten\nCoreMark iterations still take 3,408,562 cycles. A load followed by a\nconsumer incurs an interlock, and a data-bus wait holds both instructions.\nA tagged word buffer could deliver the load at the EX/MEM edge if ID\ncan predict its read index one cycle earlier. In a pointer chase the\npreceding load can supply the next base before it reaches the register\nfile.\n  hit when an older MEM store has the probed index, including a held store.\nHold the probe response with ID/EX. Reset starts a 1024-clock validity\nsweep while ordinary demand access continues. Carry the actual address,\nmask and raw word through retirement for both early and demand loads.\n\nAn isolated prototype verified 524,067 early results among 544,852\nbracketed loads. It admitted 202,859 following instructions that\nmatched the existing load-use interlock. Ten iterations took 3,058,700\ncycles with both bus stalls enabled and all five CRCs correct. Using\nthe decode register-file base alone with the same word buffer took\n3,251,054 cycles. Predicting the in-flight producer saved 192,354 cycles.\nGowin's placement options reached 48.047, 49.966 and 49.966 MHz.\nThe median gives 163.36 iterations/s, 15.83% above the 141.03 champion.\nAt this cycle count 45.122 MHz clears the 4.6% acceptance margin.\nPlacement option zero used 3,308 logic cells, 707 registers and ten BSRAMs.\nAll 53 fast formal checks passed. Selftest and the three random programs\nmatched their reference retirement traces with zero-wait and stalled\nbuses. Directed simulations also exercised the predictor after warmup,\nincluding two dependent pointer loads, a stale producer estimate,\ntag aliases and a stalled partial store.\n\nhyp-20261120-003-r6s2 The champion interlocks every consumer directly behind a load. A PC-stride\ntrial missed 85,446 dependent accesses at just four pointer-chasing load\nsites. The actual load address is available in EX, one clock before the\nconsumer needs the value. Predicting result availa\n…[5925 of 8997 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:de8b2a8c20c13c68]…\ne alongside\nthe low base hint and alignment information.\n\nFor a same-page prediction the returned RAM tag identifies the required\nbase page. Verify the actual rs1 against {12'b0, selected_tag, saved_b}\nin EX. Compare the physical operand sources in parallel and qualify the\nresults with the existing forwarding selects. This checks all 32 bits\nand removes the resolved address adder from the early-hit decision.\nA cross-page displacement, wrong base, unavailable bank or collision\nuses the existing interlock and the independent resolved-address probe\nin MEM. A scratch two-bank implementation completed ten CoreMark\niterations in 3,057,579 bracketed cycles with both buses stalling.\nAll five CRCs matched. An exhaustive check of all 16,777,216 base-low\nand displacement pairs reproduced the word index and page-crossing\ndecision. At that cycle count promotion requires more than 82.815 MHz.\nThe two-bank median Fmax is unmeasured. The main timing risks are routing\nthe two RAM responses and the independent fetch-PC path.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
