{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_16", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'time|slot' tools/eval/_slots.py && ls -d /opt/gowin/IDE/lib && cat .gitignore | tail -80\"", "aggregated_output": "1:\"\"\"Host-wide slots for the harness's heavy evals (formal, Gowin builds).\n3:Three concurrent runs, each evaluating up to three slots, plus the agents'\n7:set, each eval holds one of that many flock'ed slots (in HARNESS_EVAL_LOCK_DIR,\n8:operator-only) for its duration, and its timeout starts once it holds one.\n10:the agent environment does not inherit (tools/agents/_runtime.py).\"\"\"\n16:import time\n21:def eval_slot(what: str = \"eval\"):\n29:    started, told = time.time(), False\n32:            f = open(d / f\"slot{i}.lock\", \"w\")\n38:            waited = time.time() - started\n40:                print(f\"  [eval] {what}: got host eval slot {i} after {waited:.0f}s\", flush=True)\n48:            print(f\"  [eval] {what}: waiting for a host eval slot ({n} busy)\", flush=True)\n50:        time.sleep(5)\n54:    \"\"\"make -j for a formal run holding a slot (HARNESS_EVAL_JOBS).\"\"\"\n/opt/gowin/IDE/lib\n#   auto-committed alongside log.jsonl.\n# - per-iteration worktrees and per-iteration hypothesis YAMLs are\n#   transient inputs/outputs of the loop, not source of truth.\n\n# Per-core gitignored paths (Phase C)\ncores/*/worktrees/\ncores/*/generated/\ncores/*/obj_dir/\ncores/*/implementation_notes.md\ncores/*/experiments/hypotheses/\ncores/*/experiments/hypotheses/*.yml\ncores/*/experiments/hypotheses/*.yaml\n\n# Editor / OS\n.DS_Store\n*.swp\n.idea/\n.vscode/\n\n# Claude Code workspace\n.claude/\ndocs/assets/\n\n# Local git worktrees (subagent-driven dev; not the orchestrator's experiments/worktrees)\n.worktrees/\n\n# Node deps for bench-fence pi extension (installed locally for typecheck;\n# pi handles installs at run time so the deps don't need to be committed).\nnode_modules/\n\n# Pi-coding-agent session storage inside per-rep clones.\n.pi-sessions/\n\n# Per-rep agent telemetry inside bench/ is now tracked: log.jsonl carries\n# the full per-iteration data (LUT4, Fmax, IPC, error class) that the\n# rolled-up summary.json doesn't surface, and agent.log is the model's\n# verbatim transcript — both are essential for reproducibility and\n# postmortems. Combined they're ~3-4 MB per rep × 9 reps = ~35 MB; cheap.\n# (Previously gitignored as \"regenerable\", but they're only regenerable\n# by re-running the bench at considerable wall time and cost.)\n\n# repo.bundle (each rep's full git history, task-7 disk hygiene) is NOT\n# tracked, unlike orchestrator.log: a bundle runs about 45 MB binary per\n# rep, and at full compute-matrix scale that would bloat the git history\n# by gigabytes. It stays on disk as a local forensics artifact instead,\n# and tools/bench/gc.py archives (or recreates) it before deleting the\n# clone it was bundled from, so the content is not lost.\nbench/**/repo.bundle\n\n# agent.full.log.gz: the verbatim agent transcript. The tracked agent.log\n# next to it has tool-output fields capped (tools/bench/transcript.py),\n# which cut the published transcripts ~60%; the full stream stays on\n# disk for forensics, same policy as repo.bundle.\nbench/**/agent.full.log.gz\n\n# env.json (per-rep environment snapshot: PATH + resolved EDA tool paths)\n# IS tracked, decided at the first v2 rep publish (e1a static, 2026-07-16):\n# it is tiny, contains no secrets, and pins which toolchain binaries\n# produced each rep's numbers.\n\n# Throwaway harness-verification runs (one-model YAMLs + their output\n# trees). These are reliability checks, not benchmark data — output is\n# regenerable and the YAMLs only encode \"rerun this single model to\n# confirm a fix landed\". Naming convention: `*-verify` for output dirs,\n# `models-*-verify.yaml` for the throwaway model YAML.\nbench/*-verify/\nbench/smoke-*/\ntools/bench/models-*-verify.yaml\n\n# Aborted/partial bench output preserved locally with a debug-tag suffix\n# (e.g. `gpt-5_5.failed-pwd-bug/`, `gpt-5_5.validate-v3/`, sibling\n# `results.jsonl.<tag>`). They document a known-broken state for the\n# author's own postmortem; the canonical results live in the un-suffixed\n# per-model dirs.\nbench/*.failed-*\nbench/*.validate-*\nbench/*.prev-*\nbench/*.stale-*\nbench/*.partial-*\nbench/results.jsonl.*\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
