{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d.get('id','').endswith(('r1s2','r2s2','r4s2','r7s2')):\n  print(d.get('id'),d.get('implementation_notes'),d.get('hypothesis'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261120-003-r1s2  {'motivation': \"The baseline fetches PC+4 until EX resolves a transfer. Since IF feeds ID\\ncombinationally, a taken branch or jump discards one younger fetch slot.\\nA reference trace between the CoreMark markers contains 529,588 conditional\\nbranches, of which 294,425 change the successor, plus 49,367 direct jumps.\\nThe baseline's 199.837 ns worst path crosses forwarding and the EX ALU.\\nPredicting these direct transfers in IF can save cycles with a small table.\\n\", 'hypothesis': 'Index 64 two-bit saturating counters with fetch PC[7:2]. Use the offset\\nsign as the default direction for a legal conditional branch (backward\\ntaken, forward fall-through). Predict taken = offset_sign XOR counter[1].\\nThe counter learns whether to invert the default, so a hot backedge and\\na usually untaken forward guard can share an entry constructively. Predict\\ndirect JAL taken. Compute the signed B/J target in parallel with the table\\nlookup and select it only for a word-aligned address in instruction RAM.\\nUse PC+4 for every other fetch, including JALR. Carry the selected direction\\nbit with the accepted instruction. Direct targets are exact by construction,\\nso EX can detect a miss by comparing this bit with the resolved direction.\\nA miss redirects to the resolved next PC and kills the younger instruction.\\nCorrect predictions let the successor enter ID/EX immediately. An isolated\\nRTL prototype completed ten 2K CoreMark iterations in 4,194,484 bracketed\\ncycles with both bus stalls enabled, versus 4,491,485 on the baseline.\\nAll five CRCs matched. Gowin reported 5.214, 5.486 and 5.486 MHz across\\nthe three placement options. The median gives 13.08 iter/s, a 7.9% gain\\nover 12.12. The prototype used 12 LUT-RAM primitives versus 8 at baseline.\\n', 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 8, 'confidence': 'medium'}, 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/if_stage.sv', 'description': \"Initialize counters to 01 (weak agreement) at configuration. Use one\\nasynchronous read indexed by the fetch PC and one synchronous write.\n…[16061 of 19133 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:116307a1149f9ee0]…\n': \"Add the full resolved memory address to ex_mem_t so the early\\nforwarding result and the load's real address can travel together.\\n\"}, {'file': 'rtl/mem_stage.sv', 'description': 'Use the separate resolved address for bus addressing, alignment,\\nlane masks and RVFI. Use the selected raw word for load extraction,\\nWB and RVFI. Export the aligned read request for demand qualification.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Warm three words after the reset sweep. Run LW through a cached pointer\\ninto another LW and an ADDI with the external data bus unavailable.\\nCheck consecutive retirements and exact values. Force an index miss\\nusing base 68 and displacement 4, for which XOR predicts 64 and the\\ntrue address is 72. Test both resident and cold destinations, an\\naccepted partial store to a hot word and a same-row port collision.\\nAlternate between aligned byte addresses 4 KiB apart. Check signed\\nand unsigned subword results, request counts, RVFI raw data, masks\\nand retirement order.\\n'}]}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
