{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_87", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r9s2.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261121-003-r9s2\ntitle: Predict MUL operands in decode and verify a narrow product in EX\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  A profile of the champion between the CoreMark markers counted 93,960\n  multiplies. Both forwarded operands fit unsigned 16 bits on 88,920.\n  At the same multiply PC, the previous operand pair never repeated in a\n  256-entry last-pair table. Predicting the upcoming operands from in-flight\n  producers worked much better. Decode's two low-half guesses exactly\n  reproduced both full forwarded operands on 65,940 multiplies, including\n  11,921 with a valid immediate consumer flagged by the interlock.\n  Gowin's worst path on champion placement option 1 ran from the Newton seed\n  ROM through the shared multiplier to the MEM/WB write-data register.\nhypothesis: |\n  On admission of a MUL, predict each unsigned operand from the low 16 bits\n  of its decode-stage source. Use the register-file output with its WB\n  bypass, overriding it first with a matching MEM writer's registered EX\n  result and then with a matching younger load's raw candidate word. Launch\n  one unsigned 16x16 product by registering those two slices at the ID/EX\n  edge. In EX compare each zero-extended saved slice with its complete,\n  fully forwarded architectural operand, in parallel with the DSP product.\n  On two matches the 32-bit product is exact. Place it in the EX/MEM result\n  register and allow the following consumer to issue with normal forwarding.\n  An unchecked product never reaches a consumer or retirement.\n  \n  On a miss, hold the multiply, capture both true operands, and reuse the\n  narrow multiplier. If both fit, one corrected low-by-low pass suffices.\n  Otherwise MUL needs the low-by-low product and the low 16 bits of each\n  cross product to reconstruct bits 31:0. MULH, MULHU and MULHSU use all\n  four unsigned limb products, propagate carries into the high word, then\n  apply the signed-high correction for each operand designated signed.\n  The correction subtracts the opposite full operand from the unsigned\n  high word for each negative signed input. Release the consumer on the\n  final verified transfer, with the same bypass it would use on a hit.\n  Give repair priority at the DSP input and capture the following MUL's\n  prediction on the edge that releases the repaired instruction.\n  \n  The champion flagged 35,621 multiply-use slots with a valid consumer.\n  Moving completed products to the EX bypass can recover those bubbles\n  against the cost of 28,020 failed guesses and extra wide-limb passes.\n  The main frequency opportunity is removing the wide Newton multiplier\n  from integer multiply writeback. Its divide-only product slices stay\n  local to the divider. At the champion's cycle count the acceptance bar\n  is 79.57 MHz. The hit counts are measured on the champion. The cycle\n  count and Fmax of the proposed circuit are estimates until implementation.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Form both 16-bit operand forecasts from the existing register-file,\n      MEM destination/result and EX load-candidate signals. Qualify producer\n      matches and exclude x0. Send guesses and a valid MUL admission to the\n      execution unit. Replace the blanket multiply-use interlock with a\n      hold while a product is being repaired. Keep the existing load-use\n      and divider controls and coordinate holds with older dmem backpressure.\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Add the registered narrow multiplier, operand-key validation interface\n      and exact limb-repair sequencer. Retain the Newton datapath for divide\n      and remainder and remove its integer product output tap. Hold completed\n      results until accepted. Implement formal ALTOPS from the same verified\n      full operands, using the existing per-op formulas and transfer protocol.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Validate against post-forward rs1 and rs2. Snapshot those values on the\n      first miss before the producers advance. Bubble EX/MEM while repairing\n      and transfer each multiply exactly once with its result in alu_result.\n      Preserve the original operands for RVFI. Allow consecutive correct\n      predictions and suppress predictor admission on a squash or reset.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Remove the EX/MEM is_mul source selector once all multiply results use the registered EX result.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: Remove the external wide-product input and pass completed multiplies through the normal EX-result writeback path.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Check correct and incorrect operand guesses, narrow repairs, carries\n      across all four limb products, and every signed-high variant against\n      exact integer arithmetic. Exercise held results and reset during repair.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Check adjacent MUL and dependent retirement on a verified prediction.\n      Force a stale raw load hint and a wrong high half with identical low\n      halves. Check a repaired MUL followed by a dependent store, a second\n      MUL, and DIV across bus stalls. Check exact values, order and strobes.\nYAML\npython - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r9s2.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert d['id']==p.stem and d['category']=='predictor'\nassert all((Path('cores/bench')/i['file']).is_file() for i in d['changes'])\nprint('Validated',p)\nPY\ngit status --short --untracked-files=all\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_87", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261121-003-r9s2.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261121-003-r9s2\ntitle: Predict MUL operands in decode and verify a narrow product in EX\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  A profile of the champion between the CoreMark markers counted 93,960\n  multiplies. Both forwarded operands fit unsigned 16 bits on 88,920.\n  At the same multiply PC, the previous operand pair never repeated in a\n  256-entry last-pair table. Predicting the upcoming operands from in-flight\n  producers worked much better. Decode's two low-half guesses exactly\n  reproduced both full forwarded operands on 65,940 multiplies, including\n  11,921 with a valid immediate consumer flagged by the interlock.\n  Gowin's worst path on champion placement option 1 ran from the Newton seed\n  ROM through the shared multiplier to the MEM/WB write-data register.\nhypothesis: |\n  On admission of a MUL, predict each unsigned operand from the low 16 bits\n  of its decode-stage source. Use the register-file output with its WB\n  bypass, overriding it first with a matching MEM writer's registered EX\n  result and then with a matching younger load's raw candidate word. Launch\n  one unsigned 16x16 product by registering those two slices at the ID/EX\n  edge. In EX compare each zero-extended saved slice with its complete,\n  fully forwarded architectural operand, in parallel with the DSP product.\n  On two matches the 32-bit product is exact. Place it in the EX/MEM result\n  register and allow the following consumer to issue with normal forwarding.\n  An unchecked product never reaches a consumer or retirement.\n  \n  On a miss, hold the multiply, capture both true operands, and reuse the\n  narrow multiplier. If both fit, one corrected low-by-low pass suffices.\n  Otherwise MUL needs the low-by-low product and the low 16 bits of each\n  cross product to reconstruct bits 31:0. MULH, MULHU and MULHSU use all\n  four unsigned limb products, propagate carries into the high word, then\n  apply the signed-high correction for each operand designated signed.\n  The correction subtracts the opposite full operand from the unsigned\n  high word for each negative signed input. Release the consumer on the\n  final verified transfer, with the same bypass it would use on a hit.\n  Give repair priority at the DSP input and capture the following MUL's\n  prediction on the edge that releases the repaired instruction.\n  \n  The champion flagged 35,621 multiply-use slots with a valid consumer.\n  Moving completed products to the EX bypass can recover those bubbles\n  against the cost of 28,020 failed guesses and extra wide-limb passes.\n  The main frequency opportunity is removing the wide Newton multiplier\n  from integer multiply writeback. Its divide-only product slices stay\n  local to the divider. At the champion's cycle count the acceptance bar\n  is 79.57 MHz. The hit counts are measured on the champion. The cycle\n  count and Fmax of the proposed circuit are estimates until implementation.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Form both 16-bit operand forecasts from the existing register-file,\n      MEM destination/result and EX load-candidate signals. Qualify producer\n      matches and exclude x0. Send guesses and a valid MUL admission to the\n      execution unit. Replace the blanket multiply-use interlock with a\n      hold while a product is being repaired. Keep the existing load-use\n      and divider controls and coordinate holds with older dmem backpressure.\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Add the registered narrow multiplier, operand-key validation interface\n      and exact limb-repair sequencer. Retain the Newton datapath for divide\n      and remainder and remove its integer product output tap. Hold completed\n      results un